p\\npc.thumb(arch == loader::Thumb);\\npc.nextThumb(pc.thumb());\\npc.aarch64(arch == loader::Arm64);\\npc.nextAArch64(pc.aarch64());\\npc.set(getStartPC() & ~mask(1));\\ntc->pcState(pc);\\n```\\n\\nThis uses the `arch` passed to the constructor. For `ArmBareMetalProcess64`, the arch comes from the `BareMetalLoader::load()` function which calls:\\n\\n\\nCreating a new bare metal process instance...\\n\\nSo `ArmBareMetalProcess64` inherits from `ArmProcess64` and calls `Process::initState()` instead of `ArmProcess64::initState()` to avoid the Linux stack initialization. The `_arch` member is set during construction from the `loader::Arm64` parameter passed to the `ArmBareMetalProcess64` constructor.\\n\\nIn the base `ArmProcess64` constructor, `_arch` gets initialized with the same arch parameter. Now I need to check how `argsInit()` uses this `_arch` field to understand what happens during the initialization sequence.\",\"signature\":\"c3b7d52c26fc7345b3c64d2f01ce75d802a7dca821314da926eeea3e82a29542\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":389,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":80547},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"557879fb-82c2-4e2d-8490-78dc9033422e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:48:03.041Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"557879fb-82c2-4e2d-8490-78dc9033422e\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d360af64c2e34144bebc09987e0c0\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_function_9a3kd6mct9jt_1\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5/src/arch/arm/process.cc\",\"offset\":125,\"limit\":15}}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":\"tool_use\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":389,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":80547,\"output_tokens\":312,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"adad1f99-912b-4f18-880e-bc5afee500e6\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:48:04.169Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"adad1f99-912b-4f18-880e-bc5afee500e6\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_9a3kd6mct9jt_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"125\\tvoid\\n126\\tArmProcess64::initState()\\n127\\t{\\n128\\t    Process::initState();\\n129\\t    argsInit<uint64_t>(PageBytes, int_reg::Sp0);\\n130\\t    for (auto id: contextIds) {\\n131\\t        ThreadContext *tc = system->threads[id];\\n132\\t        CPSR cpsr = tc->readMiscReg(MISCREG_CPSR);\\n133\\t        cpsr.mode = MODE_EL0T;\\n134\\t        tc->setMiscReg(MISCREG_CPSR, cpsr);\\n135\\t        CPACR cpacr = tc->readMiscReg(MISCREG_CPACR_EL1);\\n136\\t        // Enable the floating point coprocessors.\\n137\\t        cpacr.cp10 = 0x3;\\n138\\t        cpacr.cp11 = 0x3;\\n139\\t        // Enable SVE.\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"4ba393b7-1201-4944-8f32-4ab7cd05702c\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:48:04.299Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"adad1f99-912b-4f18-880e-bc5afee500e6\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"4ba393b7-1201-4944-8f32-4ab7cd05702c\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d36149089568d574db727f35d1ec2\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"So `ArmProcess64::initState()` calls `argsInit()` which calls `pc.aarch64(arch == loader::Arm64)`. This sets the AArch64 bit correctly in the PC state. BUT, `ArmBareMetalProcess64::initState()` calls `Process::initState()` directly (skipping `ArmProcess64::initState()`), and NEVER sets `pc.aarch64()`. That's the bug.\\n\\nThe fix is in `ArmBareMetalProcess64::initState()`: I need to add:\\n```cpp\\npc.aarch64(arch == loader::Arm64);\\npc.nextAArch64(pc.aarch64());\\n\\n\\n```\\n\\nBut I should also verify this is actually the root cause. Let me trace through the `BareMetalWorkload::initState()` flow more carefully. The sequence is: `BareMetalWorkload::initState()` calls `SEWorkload::initState()`, then explicitly calls `proc->initState()` again, and finally creates a new PC state with just the PC value set and writes it back to the thread context.\\n\\nThis means the AArch64 flag gets cleared after being set correctly by `ArmBareMetalProcess64::initState()`, so I need to restore it in the workload's initState() method. The cleanest approach is to set the AArch64 flag based on the ELF architecture in the bare-metal workload initialization.\",\"signature\":\"a39a36c3e4bc78a06330f1958d8f68f67062056af9ae1f7c411987b062d029e6\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":45,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":81501},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"8e7637cc-22cb-46f6-8e88-b402403dedcb\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:48:12.464Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"8e7637cc-22cb-46f6-8e88-b402403dedcb\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d36149089568d574db727f35d1ec2\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"I see the bug. `ArmBareMetalProcess64::initState()` skips `ArmProcess64::initState()`, so never sets `pc.aarch64()`. But the fix needs to be in `BareMetalWorkload::initState()` where it creates the PC. Let me fix it.\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":45,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":81501},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"258a0322-6f77-4653-b9fb-63ce9a50e180\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:48:13.895Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"258a0322-6f77-4653-b9fb-63ce9a50e180\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d36149089568d574db727f35d1ec2\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":