this pattern doesn't match either.\\n\\nNow I'm verifying the isFpDataProc pattern, which covers the range 31:10, and the bits align perfectly with the expected 22-bit sequence, so the FMADD instruction is correctly routing to the decode function.\\n\\nHowever, I'm noticing that the decode function calculates a switch value from bits 23:21 and 15, giving 0x4 for this instruction, which should map to a valid case for the FMADD Dd instruction. The panic is occurring before we even reach that switch statement though—it's being triggered at tick 0, which is suspicious and suggests the issue might be happening during initialization rather than execution.\\n\\nLooking more closely at the panic message and the call stack, I'm seeing a reference to `onUDelay` and `UndefinedInstruction`, which makes me think the instruction 0x1e2e1000 is hitting the undefined instruction handler rather than being recognized as a valid FMADD instruction. I need to trace through the bit pattern more carefully to see where the instruction is being misclassified.\\n\\nExamining the FMADD instruction encoding, I can verify that 0x1e2e1000 matches the pattern for FMADD Dd with specific register fields, so the instruction itself is valid. The panic must be coming from somewhere else in the pipeline. I'm checking the bit patterns for both instructions and neither matches—FMADD has 01 in bits 19:18 while FMLA has 10, so these are definitely distinct encodings. I need to reconsider what the decoder is actually doing here.\\n\\nLet me trace through the routing: for 0x1e2e1000, the top-level decode confirms it should go to `decodeFpAdvSIMD` based on the instruction class bits, and then the isFpDataProc check passes, routing it to `decodeFpDataProc`. Within that function, the switch statement evaluates to 0x4, which maps to the FMADD Dd case and should instantiate the correct instruction object.\\n\\nBut looking at the panic message more carefully—\\\"Attempted to execute unknown instruction (inst 0x1e2e1000)\\\"—it seems the instruction is actually being decoded as Unknown64 rather than FMADDD, even though the pattern matching should work. Let me verify whether FMADDD is actually defined in the fp.isa format file, since that could explain why the instruction isn't being recognized despite passing the pattern checks.\",\"signature\":\"9722c24d58e43c9fac455eb433ec1d4e1892043b2dc3c40f20de8a26cbf0cfbb\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1787,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":63458},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"76aac87d-6819-40fb-a354-81ada107fae5\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:35:34.890Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"76aac87d-6819-40fb-a354-81ada107fae5\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d08d7846a6c512c622558e45bb5bd\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Found it. `test_neon.elf` crashes with `inst 0x1e2e1000` - an FMLA instruction. Let me decode it and trace the routing.\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1787,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":63458},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"9dfaec9a-6981-4d30-8372-b1ba9dc54878\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:35:36.422Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"9dfaec9a-6981-4d30-8372-b1