nary: 00011110001011100001000000000000\\nbit31:24  = 0x1e = 00011110\\nbit23:21  = 001 = 1\\nbit20:16  = 0x0e = 14\\nbit15     = 0\\nbit14:10  = 0x04 = 4\\nswitchVal = 02\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"uuid\":\"3a30e3f2-5510-4913-8151-7f13ce05e030\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:35:42.553Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"a03791c2-870e-45b5-98af-6ee9cedf65c2\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"3a30e3f2-5510-4913-8151-7f13ce05e030\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d08fedcb930cb41bcf6ec9cf481c8\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"`switchVal = 0x02` for FMLA Sd, Sn, Sm. But `decodeFpDataProc2Source` only handles 0x00 (FMUL), 0x01 (FDIV), 0x02 (FADD), 0x03 (FSUB), 0x04 (FMAX), 0x05 (FMIN), 0x06 (FMAXNM), 0x07 (FMINNM), 0x08 (FNMUL)...\\n\\n0x02 → FADD Sd = Sn + Sm, NOT FMLA Sd = Sd + Sn*Sm.\\n\\n\\nThe root cause is that FMLA and FMLS share the same opcode bits as FADD and FSUB respectively, making them indistinguishable at decode time. The solution is to detect FMLA/FMLS based on the opcode field when the instruction encoding matches FADD/FSUB. The problem is that the current pattern matching logic doesn't distinguish between FADD and FMLA based on these opcode bits. I need to update the decoder to check the full 6-bit opcode field—specifically bit 15 and bits 20-16—to properly separate FADD/FSUB (which have opcode=0b010000) from FMLA/FMLS (which have opcode=0b011001), then route them to the appropriate decode function.\\n\\nUpdating the pattern... I'm realizing the issue with the opcode field extraction—the current switch statement only uses bits 15 and 23:22, but to properly distinguish between FMLA/FMLS and FADD/FSUB I need to account for the full opcode encoding across multiple bit positions. Let me trace through the actual instruction encoding to see exactly which bits differentiate these operations. Now I'm seeing the pattern—FMLS uses the same opcode as FSUB, which means the top-level decoder can't distinguish between them, but FMLA has a unique opcode that separates it from FADD. The encoding space shows FMUL and FMLA are distinct, FADD and FMSUB are distinct, while FMLS gets confused with FSUB, and FNMSUB with FNADD. FNADD doesn't exist in the architecture, so this overlap might not cause practical issues.\\n\\nNow I need to focus on the actual problem: FMLA is routing to the wrong decode function and returning an incorrect result. I'm modifying the top-level decoder pattern and updating the switch statement in `decodeFpDataProc2Source` to check the full opcode field and properly distinguish FMLA/FMLS from their FSUB counterparts.\\n\\nUpdating the switch cases...\",\"signature\":\"052a932f7879bdfb241d33563d7496a9783c457cf6a630dadbc23d2484d44997\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":492,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":65200},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"a08f1118-0168-4632-828c-06d7f23f32f6\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:36:00.797Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"a08f1118-0168-4632-828c-06d7f23f32f6\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d08fedcb930cb41bcf6ec9cf481c8\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Found the root cause. `0x1e2e1000` = FMLA Sd, Sn, Sm, with `switchVal=0x02` which maps to FADD in `decodeFpDataProc2Source`. The o