"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"ded40f02-a3ec-434e-a52e-ce16b271e4d6\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_zqn0gaf51uqp_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"3257\\t    StaticInstPtr\\n3258\\t    decodeFpDataProc1Source(ExtMachInst machInst)\\n3259\\t    {\\n3260\\t        if (bits(machInst, 31) || bits(machInst, 29))\\n3261\\t            return new Unknown64(machInst);\\n3262\\t\\n3263\\t        uint8_t type   = bits(machInst, 23, 22);\\n3264\\t        uint8_t opcode = bits(machInst, 20, 15);\\n3265\\t\\n3266\\t        RegIndex rn = (RegIndex)(uint32_t)bits(machInst, 9, 5);\\n3267\\t        RegIndex rd = (RegIndex)(uint32_t)bits(machInst, 4, 0);\\n3268\\t\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"22ab89c2-f2ee-4ca8-bb5d-97f5baa28d0a\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:38:57.140Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"ded40f02-a3ec-434e-a52e-ce16b271e4d6\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"22ab89c2-f2ee-4ca8-bb5d-97f5baa28d0a\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d09c18d00e84a7f1df24deaa13136\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Now let me also check what `decodeFpDataProc2Source` does for switchVal=0x02 and switchVal=0x03. Since FMLS also passes