              0x0000000102a06d24 REDACTED + 68\\n24  gem5.opt                            0x0000000104222cc4 main + 284\\n25  dyld                                0x0000000191a2eb98 start + 6076\\n--- END LIBC BACKTRACE ---\\nFor more info on how to address this issue, please visit https://www.gem5.org/documentation/general_docs/common-errors/\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"9fc3b5c1-a5b4-4923-82a0-9dffe66997b2\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"606b16b5-4aa5-4f2e-9fe4-82af75a7e0a6\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d0a1afdd7403531cb17076dee93db\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Still failing with `inst 0x1e2e1000`. The fix in `decodeFpDataProc1Source` isn't working. Let me check why. \\n\\nMaybe `isFpDataProc1Source` doesn't match at all, or maybe `isFpDataProc` or another pattern matches first and sends it to `decodeFpDataProc` instead.\\n\\nWait, maybe the issue is that `isFpDataProc` in `decodeFpAdvSIMD` is also checked, and `isFpDataProc` matches the FMLS instruction. Let me check `decodeFpDataProc`:\\n\\n```cpp\\nif (isFpDataProc1Source(machInst)) return decodeFpDataProc1Source(machInst);\\n\\n\\n```\\n\\nSo `isFpDataProc` has lower priority than `isFpDataProc1Source`, meaning the FMLS instruction would match `isFpDataProc1Source` first and return Unknown64 before `isFpDataProc` gets a chance to check it. But wait, FMLS has `bit23:21=001`, which means bit21 is set to 1. Let me re-examine what `isFpDataProc1Source` actually matches.\\n\\nThe pattern `isFpDataProc1Source` checks `bits[23:22]=11` and `bit21=X`, so for FMLS that would be `bits[23:22]=00` and `bit21=1`. But the pattern shows `bits[23:22]=11`, not `00`, so FMLS shouldn't match `isFpDataProc1Source` at all. Let me double-check the bit positions again.\\n\\nActually, I'm confusing myself with the bit extraction. For FMLS at `0x1e2e1000`, I need to be more careful about which bits I'm reading. The bits are numbered from high to low, so I should extract `bits[23:22]` by taking `(0x1e2e1000 >> 22) & 0x3`.\\n\\nThat gives me `0b00`, which doesn't match the pattern `bits[23:22]=11` that `isFpDataProc1Source` requires. So FMLS shouldn't match `isFpDataProc1Source` at all, which means it must be matching a different pattern instead. Let me check if it matches `isFpDataProc` by looking at its pattern requirements.\\n\\nFor `isFpDataProc`, FMLS has `bit31=0`, `bit30=0`, `bit29=0`, and `bit24=1`, which all check out as wildcards or valid. So FMLS does match `isFpDataProc` and would route to the appropriate decoder function. Now I need to trace through what `decodeFpDataProc` does with this instruction.\\n\\nLooking at the switch statement, I'm extracting bits from the instruction: `switchVal` combines `bits[23:21]` and `bit15`. For FMLS, `bits[23:21]` is `001` (value 1) and `bit15` is 0, so `switchVal = 0b0010 = 0x2`. But the `default` case just returns `Unknown64`, which doesn't seem right.\\n\\nLet me verify what `decodeFpDataProc` actually does with FMLS instructions by checking the ARM spec. According to the encoding, FMLS is in the \\\"Three-register operand\\\" group with `opcode=0b011`, and looking at the instruction encoding `0b00_1:0_01101_Rm_000_Ra_Rn_Rd`, I need to trace through the bit layout more carefully. I'm mapping out the bit fields for the FMLS instruction encoding—bit 21 is set to 1, bits 20-16 hold the Rm register, bits 15-10 contain the opcode value 0x1B, and the remaining fields encode the destination and source registers. Now I'm checking what the switch statement extracts from this instruction encoding. Looking at the ARM specification, I'm realizing that the `switchVal` calculation might not be distinguishing between FMADD and FMSUB correctly, so I need to reconsider how the opcode and operand fields map to the actual instruction encoding in the manual. I'm extracting the opcode field from bits 15:10, which gives me 0x1A, then shifting and combining the instruction fields to compute the switch value: (0 << 4) | (0b011 << 0) = 6, which maps to FNMADD in the ARM spec. For FMLS, the switch value would be 0x2 instead.\\n\\nNow I'm realizing the switch cases in the decoder only handle values 0x0 through 0x7 for single/double precision and 0xC through 0xF for half precision, but FMLS with switchVal=0x2 hits the FNMADD case instead of routing to the NEON decoder. I need to check whether this is actually a bug or if there's a constraint I'm missing about which instructions should use which decoder path. So FMLS isn't matching either of those pattern matchers, which means it must be falling through to `isFpDataProc` and getting incorrectly routed to the wrong decoder. The real issue is that `isFpDataProc` is too permissive—it's matching instructions that should go to the NEON decoder instead. I need to either tighten the pattern matchers to exclude FMLS or add a guard in `decodeFpDataProc` to catch cases where the switch value doesn't correspond to a valid scalar FP instruction and route those to NEON instead.\\n\\nLooking at the switch values in `decodeFpDataProc`, I can see which cases are actually valid for scalar floating-point operations—cases 0x0 through 0x7 and 0xC through 0xF handle the various FMADD, FMSUB, FNMADD, and FNMSUB variants for single, double, and half precision. Everything else in that switch statement should be caught and redirected to NEON.\\n\\nNow I'm checking the NEON patterns more carefully. `isAdvSIMDSc2RegMisc` checks for specific bit patterns in the instruction encoding—looking at `op0`, `op1`, `op2`, and `op3` to validate that it's actually a scalar two-register miscellaneous instruction rather than something else. For FMLS, the `op1` bits are `10` instead o