 validation before dispatching to the decoder. I'm realizing the Q suffix indicates 128-bit vector size while D is 64-bit, so when q=1 we get the Q variant and when q=0 we get the D variant. Looking at the actual decode function, it seems the size parameter also plays a role in determining whether to return the BaseD or BaseQ template instantiation.\\n\\nFor the FMLA/FMLS instruction pair, both opcodes share the same size and q field encoding, but the distinction comes down to opcode bit 0: 0x19 maps to FMLA while 0x1B maps to FMLS. I can use this bit to route between the two operations.\\n\\nChecking the bit position more carefully: if opcode bit 0 is 1, that's FMLA (0x19), and if opcode bit 1 is also 1, that's FMLS (0x1B). For the FMLS instruction specifically, I need to verify the bit pattern against the test value 0x1e2e1000 to make sure my opcode extraction is correct. I'm checking the `isFpMulMadd` pattern against the instruction bits—the first three characters match '0','0','0','1' correctly for bit 31:28, and I need to verify the remaining bit groups match their expected constraints before the pattern is fully satisfied. Checking the op3 group pattern against the remaining bits, and they all match correctly. So the full instruction 0x1e2e1000 does satisfy the `isFpMulMadd` pattern, and I can extract the opcode as 0x1B from bits14:10.\\n\\nNow I'm refining the decode logic to properly distinguish between FMLA and FMLS instructions based on the opcode LSB, then I need to verify what class names to use in the implementation.\",\"signature\":\"61fad8f52be356acfa58c46e39279344533a1eac11017cf0b394f57dc321a11b\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1056,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":95361},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"40ef15c8-6de8-40bb-8e25-ce04004c60dd\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:52:51.483Z\",\"userType\":\"