nstrained to 0, which means I need to trace through this more carefully to understand why it's still matching. I see the issue now—the function checks if bit24 is 0 (which matches the constraint), but then when checking bit23, it retrieves bit23 and bit22 from the instruction, which gives 0 and 0 instead of the expected 1 and 0 pattern, so the validation fails.\\n\\nLet me reconsider how the bits are actually being extracted and indexed in this implementation. I'm seeing the pattern now—`bitPatternMatcher` uses big-endian bit ordering where the first character in the pattern corresponds to the highest bit position, and I'm checking how specific bits like bit31 are being extracted from the MachInst value to match against the pattern constraints. I'm extracting the bits for the second group and checking them against the pattern `X0XX`. The extracted bits `10` don't match the expected pattern, so this group fails to match.\\n\\nNow I'm checking the fourth group with pattern `XXX1XXXX` against the extracted bits from the instruction. I need to figure out which bits of the instruction correspond to this group, and I'm realizing I need to reconsider how the macro calculates which bits to extract based on the lsb and msb parameters. I'm getting tangled up in the bit extraction details here. Let me step back and check what the gem5 source actually says about how the `bits` function works with the top bits of the instruction.\",\"signature\":\"061c913f03e5c323745b664a6a6f2d814f34e23e8c9f2cdc95cbafb826668e04\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1237,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":112440},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"2c368679-09c6-4acb-bb72-c16fa241c27e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T03:05:53.202Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44