                0x00000001084870b4 _PyEval_EvalFrameDefault + 22980\\n22  Python                              0x00000001084813ec _PyEval_Vector + 268\\n23  gem5.opt                            0x0000000102602d24 REDACTED + 68\\n24  gem5.opt                            0x0000000103e1eea8 main + 284\\n25  dyld                                0x0000000191a2eb98 start + 6076\\n--- END LIBC BACKTRACE ---\\nFor more info on how to address this issue, please visit https://www.gem5.org/documentation/general_docs/common-errors/\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"785309ce-b3ec-4a13-9b92-3cb436e44b07\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"86441f43-fe3b-47cd-a037-f068630e612a\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d1366aff1bc7d7ec20ecaa7955102\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Still failing. Now `isConvFloatPFixedP` no longer matches (since I tightened it). Let me trace through again:\\n\\n1. `isConvFloatPFixedP`: bit22=0 → no longer matches ✓\\n2. `isConvFloatPInt`: bit22=0, bit21=1, bits20:19=01 → NO match\\n3. `isFpDataProc1Source`: bit22=0, bit21=1, bits18:15=XXXX, bit14=1 → MATCHES → `decodeFpDataProc1Source`\\n\\n\\n4. In `decodeFpDataProc1Source`, the opcode calculation gives 0x25, which falls through the switch statement and returns Unknown64\\n\\nSo the instruction is still getting caught by `isFpDataProc1Source`. I need to add a guard in that function to reject it before it even gets to the switch statement.\\n\\nActually, looking at this more carefully, the real issue is that `isFpDataProc1Source` is too broad—it matches FMLA/FMLS even though those instructions belong to the AdvSIMD scalar three-same category. The fix is to tighten the pattern matcher itself to exclude them. I can add a constraint that requires bit22 to be 1, which would disambiguate between the scalar FP instructions (where bit22=1) and the AdvSIMD ones (where bit22=0).\\n\\nWait, but FMLS also has bit22=0, so this won't work. Let me reconsider—maybe the actual distinction is in the lower bits like bit21 or bit20, which differ between the two groups. Or perhaps I need to check what `isFpCompare` and `isFpImmediate` are doing, since those might also be matching and causing the wrong handler to be selected. bit22=0, bit21=1, bits20:19=01. No match.\\n\\nContinuing through the remaining checkers: `isFpCondCompare` requires bit21=0 so it doesn't match, but `isFpMulMadd` is a wildcard that matches everything. Then `isFpDataProc2Source` also matches because it only constrains bits21 and 15, both of which are 0 for this instruction.\\n\\nAfter that, `isFpCondSel` fails the bit22 check, and `isFpDataProc` fails because bit21=1 instead of the required wildcards.\\n\\nSo the instruction is being caught by `isFpDataProc2Source` and decoded as an FADD operation, which is wrong—it's actually FMLS. The real solution is to add a guard in `decodeFpDataProc2Source` to reject instructions where bit21=1 and the opcode is in the FMLA/FMLS range.\\n\\nNow I'm reconsidering the guard logic. If I check for bit21=1 and opcode[5]=0 (meaning opcode < 0x20), that would correctly identify FMLA and FMLS instructions while excluding FMADD/FMSUB. The guard condition becomes `if (bits(machInst, 21) && (bits(machInst, 20, 16) < 0x20))`, which rejects exactly those problematic instructions and lets through the FMADD/FMSUB variants.\\n\\nThis should work, so let me verify against the actual test instruction. The FMLS instruction 0x1e2e1000 has bit21=1 and opcode=0x04, which is less than 0x20, so it correctly returns Unknown64. Meanwhile, a valid FADD instruction like 0x1e210800 has bit21=0, so it passes through the guard and gets decoded properly. The guard is doing exactly what it should.\\n\\nNow I'm reconsidering whether bit22=0 alone is enough to exclude FMLA/FMLS from the `isFpDataProc1Source` pattern, but I need to think through what other instructions might also have bit22=0 to make sure I'm not being too restrictive. Looking at the opcode values in `decodeFpDataProc1Source`, I can see the valid entries range from 0x00 through 0x13, so I need to verify that restricting bit22=0 doesn't accidentally filter out any of these legitimate instructions. Finishing the opcode breakdown, I see that 0x19 through 0x1F are all reserved or AdvSIMD instructions, so they should be excluded. Now I'm checking which opcodes actually have bit22 set to 0 to understand the bit encoding pattern across all valid instructions. Looking at the full range of opcodes in the `isFpDataProc1Source` category, they all fall between 0x00 and 0x13, so any instruction with an opcode >= 0x14 should be rejected to avoid false matches. The simplest approach is to add a guard that returns Unknown when the opcode exceeds this range.\\n\\nHowever, I need to verify this won't break valid scalar FP instructions. Checking the bit patterns: FMADD/FMSUB/FNMADD/FNMSUB instructions have bit21=1, which already distinguishes them from the other FP categories. The remaining cases like isFpCompare, isFpImmediate, isFpCondCompare, and isFpCondSel either have different bit21 values or other distinguishing factors that prevent them from conflicting with the opcode check.\\n\\nMy solution is to add a guard condition that rejects any instruction with bit21=1 and an opcode value >= 0x14, routing those to Unknown. Then I need to restore the earlier guard I removed from `decodeFpDataProc2Source` that was checking for the same conditions.\",\"signature\":\"725def031bfd3616ad03029fa3c8d4f525b69a45594da52bf7fa0bd61d62ebfc\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":902,\"cache_read_in