 coming from, whether it's in how I'm reading the bits or a discrepancy in the documentation itself. I'm extracting the opcode field from the instruction encoding—bits 14:11 give me 0001, and bit 10 is 0, so the full opcode is 0b00010, which should be 2 in decimal, but the manual lists it as 0x05 instead. There's definitely something off with how I'm reading this, or maybe the manual's opcode numbering is calculated differently than I'm assuming.\\n\\nLet me try working backwards from the actual instruction encoding—if I set opcode=0x05 and M=1 for FMLS, the binary representation should give me the right bits to extract from the instruction itself.\\n\\nActually, I'm going to stop trying to manually calculate these opcodes and just test the pattern with what the ARM manual says should work. The pattern should catch FMLS when opcode=0x05 and bit21=1, along with other similar instructions like FMLA and FADD.\\n\\nBut here's the problem: when I decode FMLS with opcode=0x05, the switch statement won't have a case for it, so it returns Unknown64. I need to either modify the pattern to use a valid opcode for FMLS, or restructure the switch to handle the correct opcode value.\\n\\nLooking at the constraint more carefully, I need to exclude FADD while including FMLS. The key difference is bit 21—in FMLS it's set to 1, but in FADD it's 0. So I can add `bit21=1` to the pattern, which will filter out FADD and keep FMLS in the catch block.\\n\\nLet me verify this works for FMLA too: it has bit21=1 as well, so it also gets caught by this pattern. That means `isFpMulMadd` with bit21=1 will correctly match both FMLS and FMLA while excluding FADD.\\n\\nNow checking what happens when `isFpMulMadd` doesn't match—FMLS would fall through to `isFpDataProc2Source`, which also matches because it has no bit21 constraint. So both FMLS and FMLA end up going to `decodeFpDataProc2Source` instead, which is a problem since they need different handling. So the pattern matching logic means both FMLS and FMLA would end up hitting `decodeFpDataProc2Source` instead of the correct dedicated decoders, which is exactly the problem we're trying to solve. Adding `bit21=1` to `isFpMulMadd` fixes this by ensuring FMLS and FMLA route to `decodeFpMulMadd` where they belong, while other instructions that don't have bit21=1 correctly fall through to `decodeFpDataProc2Source`.\\n\\nNow I'm looking at the FMLS instruction encoding from the ARM manual—it specifies bits 31:24 as 0x1E and bits 23:22 as 00, so I need to verify whether the pattern should also enforce those specific bit values or if they're already constrained by the opcode matching. I'm realizing the size field occupies bits 23:22 in the general encoding, but for this specific instruction type those bits might be repurposed or have different meaning depending on the instruction variant. Let me focus on the key differentiator: FMLS has bit21=1 while FADD has bit21=0, so I can use that single bit to distinguish between them and route to the correct decoder.\\n\\nAdding bit21=1 to the pattern will exclude FADD and correctly match both FMLS and FMLA, which both have bit21=1. This should resolve the routing issue, so let me apply this fix.\",\"signature\":\"64ecc1dba6e18f77f2c1944994eca0ab4267999da1f4242e5da0f7e339d1edc8\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1161,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":96854},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"2c6a0c43-be0d-494b-8bac-6f4139033b9d\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:25:39.794Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"2c6a0c43-be0d-494b-8bac-6f4139033b9d\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\