ts\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"1e418c3e-a167-4437-82ba-a50e639996ff\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:40:00.894Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"1e418c3e-a167-4437-82ba-a50e639996ff\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_2oh0zlnjf119_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"95\\t    // FMLA/FMLS: scalar three-same FP (bit21=1, bit15=1)\\n96\\t    // These have the same encoding as AdvSIMD scalar three-same but with\\n97\\t    // bit21=1.  Handle them separately so they don't get misrouted to the\\n98\\t    // scalar FP data-processing decoder.\\n99\\t    inline StaticInstPtr decodeFpMulMadd(ExtMachInst machInst);\\n100\\t}\\n101\\t}};\\n102\\t\\n103\\toutput decoder {{\\n104\\tnamespace Aarch64\\n105\\t{\\n106\\t    // FMLA/FMLS: 31:24=00011110, 23:22=00, 21=1, bit15=0, opcode in bits14:10\\n107\\t    // These are reserved in scalar FP space but are valid AdvSIMD scalar\\n108\\t    // three-same instructions.  Dispatch based on the 5-bit opcode:\\n109\\t    // FMLS=0x04, FMLA=0x0B (opcode & 1: 0=FMLS, 1=FMLA).\\n110\\t    StaticInstPtr\\n111\\t    decodeFpMulMadd(ExtMachInst machInst)\\n112\\t    {\\n113\\t        uint8_t q      = bits(machInst, 30);\\n114\\t        uint8_t size   = bits(machInst, 23, 22);\\n115\\t        // 5-bit opcode: bits(14,10).  FMLS=0x04 (bit1=0), FMLA=0x0B (bit1=1).\\n116\\t        uint8_t opcode = bits(machInst, 14, 10);\\n117\\t\\n118\\t        RegIndex vd = (RegIndex)(uint8_t)bits(machInst, 4, 0);\\n119\\t        RegIndex vn = (RegIndex)(uint8_t)bits(machInst, 9, 5);\\n120\\t        RegIndex vm = (RegIndex)(uint8_t)bits(machInst, 20, 16);\\n121\\t\\n122\\t        // opcode & 1: 0=FMLS, 1=FMLA\\n123\\t        // For FMLS only 16-bit elements are valid; 32/64-bit return Unknown.\\n124\\t        switch (size) {\\n125\\t          case 0: // 16-bit elements (half-precision)\\n126\\t            if (opcode & 1) { // FMLA\\n127\\t                if (q)\\n128\\t                    return new FmlaQX<uint16_t>(machInst, vd, vn, vm);\\n129\\t                else\\n130\\t                    return new FmlaDX<uint16_t>(machInst, vd, vn, vm);\\n131\\t            } else { // FMLS\\n132\\t                if (q)\\n133\\t                    return new FmlsQX<uint16_t>(machInst, vd, vn, vm);\\n134\\t                else\\n135\\t                    return new FmlsDX<uint16_t>(machInst, vd, vn, vm);\\n136\\t            }\\n137\\t          case 1: // 32-bit elements (single-precision)\\n138\\t            // FMLS is not defined for single-precision scalar; only FMLA.\\n139\\t            if (opcode & 1) { // FMLA\\n140\\t                if (q)\\n141\\t                    return new FmlaQX<uint32_t>(machInst, vd, vn, vm);\\n142\\t                else\\n143\\t                    return new FmlaDX<uint32_t>(machInst, vd, vn, vm);\\n144\\t            }\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"2e1cecec-4294-4a1e-af23-3cd856d116b1\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:40:01.032Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"1e418c3e-a167-4437-82ba-a50e639996ff\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"2e1cecec-4294-4a1e-af23-3cd856d116b1\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d262177284dcb305f651c557e5566\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Let me add debug output to `decodeFpMulMadd` and see what happens.\\n\\nActually, let me try a completely different approach. Instead of debugging, let me just REBUILD from scratch and see if that helps. Sometimes there are stale object files.\\n\\nActually, let me try a simpler approach. Let me add a debug statement to the `decodeFpMulMadd` function in the SOURCE file (neon64.isa) and rebuild. This will let me know if the function is even being called.\\n\\nBut actually, I think the issue might be something completely different. Let me check the top-level decode again. Looking at the `decode-method.cc.inc`:```cppusing namespace Aarch64;\\n\\n\\n```\\n\\nNow I'm tracing through the top-level decode function for AARCH64 case 1. When the instruction has bits 27:25 equal to 111, it calls `decodeFpAdvSIMD<GenericDecoder>`. I need to verify this is actually being executed for my test instruction `0x1e2e1000`.\\n\\nLooking at the condition chain, bit 27 is 1, bit 25 is 1, bit 26 is 1, and bit 24 is 0 — so this falls through to the else case and correctly invokes `decodeFpAdvSIMD<GenericDecoder>`. The top-level decode path is working as expected.\\n\\nLet me check the actual pattern matching logic. The `isFpMulMadd` pattern is checking specific bits: bit 31 is 0, bit 30 is 0, bit 29 is 0, bit 28 is 1, and bit 27 is 1. I need to verify these bit positions are being extracted correctly from the instruction encoding. I'm seeing that FMLA actually matches the pattern while FMLS doesn't, which seems backwards. Let me double-check the bit extraction logic against the actual instruction encoding to make sure I'm reading the bits correctly. Let me verify the encoding bits for both instructions to confirm the pattern matching. FMLS and FMLA should have the same first 32 bits, which would mean they both match or both don't—but the simulation shows only FMLA matching. Looking more carefully at the bit extraction logic, I need to check how the Python code is actually computing the masked value for FMLS to see where the mismatch is occurring. I'm checking whether FMLS actually matches the bit pattern, and it turns out the simulation result showing \\\"no match\\\" was misleading—the corrected Python code confirms FMLS should match. Now I need to figure out why gem5 isn't recognizing this, so I'm going to add debug output to trace what's happening.\",\"signature\":\"970ada1f7daba797f61336b95251af52faa6fb040d49b0cb105e4b03a5076260\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":935,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":128473},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"810dd858-62e8-47d6-8fcb-621749d15d5e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:40:15.894Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"810dd858-62e8-47d6-8fcb-621749d15d5e\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d262177284dcb305f651c557e5566\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me add debug output to `decodeFpMulMadd` to see if it's being called:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":