\\t        if ((cur_el == EL0 && cpacr_cp10 != 0x3) ||\\n742\\t            (cur_el != EL0 && !(cpacr_cp10 & 0x1)))\\n743\\t            return disabledFault();\\n744\\t    }\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"c553349b-2b9d-45c1-8c39-644e3f31313d\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:01:48.846Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"acc6b7ac-56b8-4fb7-8dc2-316e8232acbc\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"c553349b-2b9d-45c1-8c39-644e3f31313d\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d1d2dddf6b0f124da6d2fc7f3584a\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Line 705-708: `checkFPAdvSIMDEnabled64` checks:\\n```cpp\\nif (((el == EL0 && cpacr.fpen != 0x3) ||\\n    (el == EL1 && !(cpacr.fpen & 0x1))) &&\\n    !ELIsInHost(tc, el))\\n    return advSIMDFPAccessTrap64(EL1);\\n```\\n\\nWait, the error in the backtrace was `UndefinedInstruction`, not `advSIMDFPAccessTrap64`. Let me check what `advSIMDFPAccessTrap64` returns...\\n\\nActually, looking more carefully, the `UndefinedInstruction` might not come from `checkFPAdvSIMDEnabled64`. \\n\\nI need to trace back where `UndefinedInstruction` actually originates—it could be coming from within the `FmlsDX` instruction's execute method itself, maybe through a template macro that validates conditions and generates that fault. Let me look at how `threeEqualRegInstX` handles the `FmlsDX` definition with the `SimdFloatMultAccOp` parameters.\\n\\nLooking at the `FmlsDX` template instantiation, the `eWalkCode` gets set to `simd64EnabledCheckCode`, which chains to `vfp64EnabledCheckCode` and then to `checkFPAdvSIMDEnabled64`. If the FP/SIMD registers aren't enabled, this should trigger the appropriate fault. But the actual error I'm seeing is an `UndefinedInstruction`, not a disabled fault, which suggests something else is going wrong in the execution path.\\n\\nLet me trace through what happens when `FmlsDX<uint16_t>` actually executes. The `fmlsCode` template generates code that calls floating-point library functions like multiplication and addition, but if the FP register bank isn't initialized or accessible in SE mode, that could be throwing the undefined instruction error instead. I need to examine the `threeEqualRegInstX` template more carefully to understand the full execution flow.\",\"signature\":\"cca1bc910b76afb5a1818321a63cf93b3f3b2efd06963615495444d6cf40d13f\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":759,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":55251},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"e0ec6d0a-d8f8-428d-a983-4645950b0999\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:01:58.099Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"e0ec6d0a-d8f8-428d-a983-4645950b0999\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d1d2dddf6b0f124da6d2fc7f3584a\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The `UndefinedInstruction` could come from inside the `FmlsDX` execute body. Let me check the `threeEqualRegInstX` template and the generated execute code:\\n\"}],\"model\