ject/sdc-fuzzing/gem5/build/ARM/gem5.opt\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"uuid\":\"3724a5a2-c585-446e-a03e-18c9fcc22b56\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:08:55.264Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"382132c5-ff81-4bbc-b81c-8f904be2ea9f\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"3724a5a2-c585-446e-a03e-18c9fcc22b56\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d1ed71a371d4e4f1ec1c2750ab3d0\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"The generated exec file is from Apr 2 11:37 and the binary from Apr 2 11:54. So the binary was built after the generated files. The generated files have the template definition for `FmlsDX<Element>::execute`. \\n\\nBut wait - the `exec-ns.cc.inc` file is dated Apr 2 11:37. Let me check when the `neon64.isa` was last modified to see if the ISA compiler regenerated the exec files with the new code.\\n\\nActually, looking at the generated exec file, the template definition at line 247840 looks correct - it uses `rCount=2` (2 uint32_t registers for D-reg) and processes `uint16_t` elements. \\n\\nLet me trace through the FmlsDX instantiation more carefully. For `smallFloatTypes = (\\\"uint16_t\\\", \\\"uint32_t\\\")` and `rCount=2`, the element count calculation gives 4 for 16-bit elements and 2 for 32-bit elements, which should handle both cases correctly.\\n\\nLooking at the generated template for `FmlsDX<Element>::execute`, I can see it's setting up registers for a D-sized register pair with two uint32_t values, and the destination uses a VecRegContainer with uint32_t backing. For `uint16_t` elements, this should map correctly to the 64-bit register space. I see the issue now—for 32-bit elements, eCount becomes 2 and only 2 registers are used, but the template unconditionally zeroes out registers 2 and 3 even when they shouldn't exist. This is fine for 16-bit types but creates a mismatch for 32-bit where only indices 0 and 1 are valid. Let me trace through what happens when Element is 16-bit: with 4 elements per pair of registers and only 2 iterations needed, the second element gets written to the correct position in the first register, and the upper half stays zero as expected for half-precision values. 1*2=2], srcReg2.elements[2] = AA64FpOp2[1]'s upper half, which is the 3rd half-precision element. ✓\\n\\nSo for 16-bit elements with 2 registers:\\n- i=0: elements[0]=H0, elements[1]=H1\\n- i=1: elements[2]=H2, elements[3]=H3\\n- i=2: elements[4]=H4, elements[5]=H5 (but eCount=4, so only i=0,1)\\n- eCount=4, so i=0,1,2,3\\n- elements[0]=H0, elements[1]=H1, elements[2]=H2, elements[3]=H3 ✓\\n\\nThe operand declarations for AA64FpOp1P0, AA64FpOp1P1, AA64FpOp2P0, and AA64FpOp2P1 look right for D-reg. Now I'm wondering where the UndefinedInstruction fault is actually coming from—maybe the real issue is that `FmlsDX` with 16-bit half-precision elements requires the FHM extension, so I should check if there's an extension check missing in the generated code. I'm realizing the `fhm_check` function reads the ID register and checks the FHM bit, returning an undefined instruction fault if it's not set—so `FmlsDX<uint16_t>` should have this check, but the vector variant at line 1935 doesn't include it.\\n\\nLooking at the actual ARM specification, FMLS with half-precision elements is part of the base Floating-point extension, not FHM. FHM is specifically for the faster matrix multiply instructions, so the base FMLS should work without that extension check.\\n\\nBut the `fplibNeg` function might be where the issue lies—I need to check whether it depends on FHM or if it's available with just the base floating-point support. Looking at the ISA compiler's output, I'm noticing that the vector variant of FMLS lacks the FHM check that the scalar variant has, which seems like it might be an unintentional inconsistency rather than a deliberate design choice.\\n\\nBut stepping back, the real issue is that the binary was successfully built with a link error about the undefined `FmlsDX<uint16_t>::execute` symbol, which means the template instantiation never got properly defined in the compiled code. That's why we're getting the undefined instruction error at runtime instead of a linker failure.\\n\\nLet me reconsider what's actually happening with the `UndefinedInstruction` exception being thrown. The instruction decodes to `FmlsDX` correctly, but something in the execute path is triggering the undefined instruction fault. I should check whether the generated code for the element variant is actually being compiled into the binary, since that's what the linker would need for the Q-register variant that the decoder is calling.\\n\\nLooking at the generated execute code around line 247828, the `FmlsElemScX` instantiation does have an actual function body, not just a declaration. So that file is definitely being compiled. But when I look at the non-element `FmlsDX` variant, something's different about how it's being generated or instantiated. The `FmlsDX<uint16_t>` template is declared at line 247972 but never defined anywhere in the generated files, which means the build should fail with a linker error unless the template is explicitly instantiated in the source code. Let me trace back to see where this template is actually supposed to be instantiated or defined.\",\"signature\":\"c537161004979c1e1afd82a770aa3d5ea265eda05e779e411d8e2d5fbe14c4fa\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":522,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":74443},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"2ccef9c1-affe-45ae-a9d8-84f34f2b6cbb\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:09:22.082Z\",\"userTy