correctly adding mask bits for wildcards, but gem5's implementation handles this correctly by only setting mask bits for concrete values. Let me verify the gem5 calculation with bit24='0' instead of '1' to see if that's the actual source of the mismatch. bits15:10=XXXXX = char[16]='X', char[17]='X', char[18]='X', char[19]='X', char[20]='X'\\n\\nWait, I have 22 chars total (indices 0-21):\\n0: '0' (bit31)\\n1: '0' (bit30)\\n2: '0' (bit29)\\n3: '1' (bit28)\\n4: '1' (bit27)\\n5: 'X' (bit26)\\n6: 'X' (bit25)\\n7: '0' (bit24) ← CORRECTED from '1'\\n8: '1' (bit23)\\n9: '0' (bit22)\\n10: 'X' (bit21)\\n11: 'X' (bit20)\\n12: 'X' (bit19)\\n13: 'X' (bit18)\\n14: 'X' (bit17)\\n15: 'X' (bit16)\\n16: 'X' (bit15)\\n17: 'X' (bit14)\\n18: 'X' (bit13)\\n19: 'X' (bit12)\\n20: 'X' (bit11)\\n21: 'X' (bit10)\\n\\nSo the mask and match values are calculated by setting the appropriate bits for each constraint, giving mask=0xf8800000 and match=0x18000000.\\n\\nWhen I verify this against FMLS 0x1e2e1000, the bitwise AND operation produces 0x18000000, which matches the expected value, confirming the pattern is correct.\\n\\nThe issue was that char[7] (bit24) needed to be '0' instead of '1', and I also need to double-check the comment on the op1 field. I'm verifying the constraint ordering in the pattern—bit24 is '0' and bit23 is '1'—and making sure the comment matches the corrected values so it accurately reflects what the pattern expects. Verifying the bit constraints for the corrected pattern against the FMLS instruction 0x1e2e1000, confirming that all specified bits match correctly: bits31:28, 27, 24, 23, and 22 all align as expected.\\n\\nThe corrected pattern should now be applied, though I need to verify the exact character count—counting through the bit assignments from bit31 down to bit10 to ensure the pattern has the right number of elements. I'm mapping out how each character in the string corresponds to bit positions, working through the pattern to identify which bits are fixed ver