e existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"2e9712e2-97a4-49ea-a7c3-afb61615b77b\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:29:20.291Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"105a77ce-08af-425f-acde-53b24e83eecf\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"2e9712e2-97a4-49ea-a7c3-afb61615b77b\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d23a05e81b63728dcdd234b547c28\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Now I can see the full function. The pattern check order is:\\n1. isCryptoAES - unlikely to match FMLS\\n2. isCrypto3SHA - unlikely\\n3. isCrypto2SHA - unlikely\\n4. isAdvSIMDScCopy - unlikely\\n5. isAdvSIMDSc3SameFp16 - UNLIKELY\\n6. isAdvSIMDSc2RegMiscFp16 - unlikely\\n7. isAdvSIMDSc3SameExtra - unlikely\\n8. isAdvSIMDSc2RegMisc - unlikely\\n9. isAdvSIMDSc2Pwise - unlikely\\n\\n\\n10. isAdvSIMDSc3Diff - unlikely\\n11. isAdvSIMDSc3Same - unlikely\\n12. isAdvSIMDScShiftByImm - unlikely\\n13. isAdvSIMDScIndexedElem - unlikely\\n14. isAdvSIMDTblLookup - unlikely\\n15. isAdvSIMDPermute - unlikely\\n16. isAdvSIMDExtract - unlikely\\n17. isAdvSIMDCopy - unlikely\\n18. isAdvSIMD3SameFp16 - unlikely\\n19. isAdvSIMD2RegMiscFp16 - unlikely\\n20. isAdvSIMD3RegExt - unlikely\\n21. isAdvSIMD2RegMisc - unlikely\\n22. isAdvSIMDAcrossLanes - unlikely\\n23. isAdvSIMD3Diff - unlikely\\n24. isAdvSIMD3Same - unlikely (THIS COULD MATCH!)\\n25. isAdvSIMDModImm - unlikely\\n26. isAdvSIMDShiftByImm - unlikely\\n27. isAdvSIMDIndexedElem - unlikely\\n28. isConvFloatPFixedP - NO (bit22=0, needs bit22=1)\\n29. isConvFloatPInt - unlikely\\n30. isFpMulMadd - SHOULD MATCH\\n31. isFpDataProc1Source - unlikely\\n32. isFpCompare - unlikely\\n33. isFpImmediate - unlikely\\n\\nContinuing through the rest of the pattern checks, most are unlikely to match until we reach the floating-point multiplication and addition patterns around step 30, which could potentially match depending on the instruction encoding.\\n\\nNow I'm realizing there are many more patterns between isFpMulMadd and isFpDataProc2Source that I need to examine more carefully. Let me trace through the generated code for the cryptographic and SIMD pattern matching functions to understand what's actually being checked. Looking at the SIMD and floating-point pattern matching functions that follow, I'm seeing a long sequence of checks for various SIMD operations like shift by immediate values, table lookups, permutations, extractions, copies, and floating-point conversions—each with their own pattern definitions that I'm tracing through to understand the bit-level matching logic. I'm looking at the bit pattern definitions for various floating-point and SIMD operations in the ARM ISA decoder, where each pattern specifies which bits must be set or cleared across the instruction encoding to identify a particular instruction type like FP multiplication and addition, comparison operations, and immediate-based variants. I need to figure out why the `isFpMulMadd` pattern isn't matching FMLS instructions, so I'm checking whether any of the earlier patterns like `isAdvSIMDSc3Same` are catching these instructions first. Looking at the bit patterns, `isAdvSIMDSc3Same` has specific constraints on bits 29, 11, and 10 that should distinguish it from FMLS, but I need to verify whether the other patterns might be matching before we even get to the floating-point multiplication check. I'm realizing the NEON functions operate on full 128-bit Q registers, while the scalar FMLS instruction I'm checking uses just the lower 64-bit D register, so they should have different encoding patterns. Let me trace through what makes these instruction formats distinct.\\n\\nI'm checking if any of the other isAdvSIMD patterns might actually be matching FMLS instead, since my pattern correctly identifies it but something else in the chain is returning Unknown64. Let me verify what bit23 actually contains in the FMLS encoding. I'm realizing I need to actually test the isFpMulMadd pattern in a debugger rather than doing more manual bit analysis. Let me also reconsider the instruction encoding more carefully — I'm checking what the actual bit patterns are for FMLS variants across different bit positions to make sure I'm not confusing which instruction is which. I'm checking which patterns might be matching FMLS before the expected handler. Looking at the opcode bits for various SIMD operations like FMLS, FMLA, and FCADD, they all fall within the `isAdvSIMDSc3Same` pattern's wildcard space, so I need to verify if any of those earlier patterns are catching FMLS instead. Continuing through the rest of the instruction pattern definitions...\\n\\nI notice the FCADD instruction at 0x1e945000 doesn't match the `isFpMulMadd` pattern either, so it's not interfering. Now I need to actually test this with a debugger to see what's happening.\\n\\nWait, I just realized the panic message is about an unknown instruction at 0x1e2e1000 — not just the \\\"unknown\\\" string. I should check what fault gem5 throws when StaticInst::isUnknown() returns true to understand if this is actually a different error than what I thought.\\n\\nLooking at the fault definitions, when isUnknown() is true, gem5 creates an UndefinedInstruction fault rather than returning an Unknown instruction. So if the decode is returning Unknown64, the fault should be UndefinedInstruction. But the panic at the top level suggests something else is happening — maybe the instruction is being decoded as something that invokes the UndefinedInstruction fault directly.\\n\\nLet me trace through what's happening at execution time with this specific instruction encoding.\\n\\nThe decode appears to