fore reaching line 2911\\n\\nWait - let me look at the switch structure more carefully. Looking at the code again:\\n\\n\\nThe outer switch is on DEBUGSTEP, and the inner case checks if bits 27 through 25 equal 0x7 (the `else` branch at line 2911). The `machInst` parameter passed to `decodeInst` is of type `ExtMachInst`, but I need to understand what this type actually contains when passed from the `switch (AARCH64)` block.\\n\\nLooking at the header macros, `machInst` is the object itself, not a reference, and `AARCH64` is extracted from it via the bitfield accessor. So the parameter is passed by value as a 64-bit ExtMachInst value, which should work correctly. But I'm noticing the top-level function signature doesn't match what I'd expect from looking at how it's being called with the bitfield expression. I'm realizing the switch statement uses `bits()` to extract bitfields from `machInst`, and this works because `ExtMachInst` is a BitUnion64 that supports bitfield extraction operators. But there's a key distinction here: when calling `decodeFpAdvSIMD<GenericDecoder>(machInst)` in the `decode-method.cc.inc` file, the decoder functions live in the `Aarch64` namespace, so I need to understand how that namespace resolution works with the bitfield extraction.\\n\\nThe `isFpMulMadd` pattern is defined in `decoder-ns.cc.inc` under the `ArmISAInst` namespace, but `decodeFpAdvSIMD` is defined under `Aarch64`. The generated code uses `using namespace Aarch64` to access the decoder functions, so the pattern should be available in that scope. Let me try calling `isFpMulMadd` directly with some debug output to see what's actually happening.\\n\\nI need to verify whether `decodeFpAdvSIMD` is being invoked at all—the fact that my debug message isn't printing suggests either the function isn't being called, the stderr output is being buffered, or the debug string isn't present in the binary. Let me check if stderr is actually making it to the output or if it's being redirected somewhere else.\\n\\nLooking at the backtrace, the panic is being called from `UndefinedInstruction::invoke`, which itself gets called from `advancePC`. But that's the execution path, not the decode path—I need to trace where the decode is actually happening. The question is whether gem5 decodes instructions eagerly when they're fetched or lazily during execution. Let me search through the AtomicSimpleCPU code to find the actual decode call.\\n\\nExamining the fetch logic...\",\"signature\":\"bc46a71b88199a358941058b9f89dc142d3670710b89a188cbafa51e2f61477c\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":186,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":162004},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"9ea5ebc4-7247-4a4c-8faa-caf07b04d21c\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:25:28.289Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"9ea5ebc4-7247-4a4c-8faa-caf07b04d21c\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d30b30f863410629d06f8272229d5\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The string is in the binary but not printed! `decodeInst` is called but the bit27:25=111 path is never reached. Let me trace where the decode actually happens:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":186,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":162004},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"603597a9-ea85-45fd-9d8d-d031b79e6a51\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:25:29.251Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-proje