with my mask and match values to see where the logic is breaking down. I see the issue now—the mask is capturing bits that should be wildcards. The problem is that when the character is a wildcard, I'm still setting the corresponding bit in the mask, which incorrectly constrains those positions. I need to modify the logic so that wildcards don't contribute to the mask at all, while still handling the bit extraction correctly for both masked and non-masked values. I see the issue now—when I apply the mask to extract just the constrained bits, the result isn't aligned to bit 0, so comparing it directly to the match value won't work. I need to shift the masked result right to align it properly before comparing.\\n\\nActually, looking at the gem5 implementation more carefully, the mask and match values are pre-aligned so that the comparison `(value & mask) == match` works directly without any shifting needed.\\n\\nLet me trace through with concrete bit positions to verify this logic holds. I'm mapping out how each constraint in the pattern corresponds to actual bit positions, then building up the mask and match values by iterating through the characters and setting the appropriate bits based on whether each position should be forced high, forced low, or left as a wildcard. I see the issue now—the match value needs to be shifted to align with the mask positions, so when I OR together the individual bit constraints, I'm actually building up the correct value in the right bit positions rather than always starting from bit 0. Continuing to verify the pattern for bit 27, then moving on to bit 24 which should be a wildcard 'X', and now working through bit 23 which needs to be set to 1.\\n\\nFinishing the calculation for bit 22 as a wildcard, then summing all the mask values to get 0xf9c00000 and the match value to 0x18800000. But wait—I'm realizing I made an error earlier about which bits should be constrained versus wildcards. Let me reconsider the corrected pattern from the