rough the movk instruction, then combined with the previous value through the add operation to get the final w9 value of 0xff228c00.\\n\\nNow I need to decode the machine instruction in w0 by masking it with 0xff228c00 and comparing against a match value, but my initial Python simulation suggests this pattern check might not match the expected FMLS instruction. Let me look at the second check to see if there's a pattern I'm missing.\\n\\nActually, I'm realizing the first check starting at address 0x100848084 is almost certainly the `isCryptoAES` check based on the constants involved, not FMLS. I should be looking further into the function where the FMLS pattern matching should actually occur.\\n\\nLooking at the constants in the second check, I'm calculating what w10 and w9 would be: w10 gets 0x5e00800 from the immediate values, and w9 becomes 0x5e008c00 after adding the offset, which then gets ANDed with w0 in the instruction. I'm tracing through the assembly instructions to verify the binary encoding—the `movk` instruction at address 0x1008480b8 loads 0x5e00 into bits 31:16 of w11, giving 0x5e000000, and I need to confirm how the subsequent `mov` instruction at 0x10084809c constructs the final value in w11. I'm working through the bit manipulation to decode which cryptographic instruction this is—the `movk` instruction loads 0x5e28 into bits 31:16 of w10, giving 0x5e280800, and the mask calculation confirms this is `isCrypto2SHA` for AES operations. I'm tracing through the bit pattern more carefully now. Looking at the assembly instructions, the mask constant 0x5e280800 is constructed from the immediate values, but when I work through the `movk` instruction with lsl #16, I'm getting a different bit arrangement than expected—I need to reconsider how the bits are actually being positioned. Now I'm verifying the `movk` instruction works correctly—it loads the immediate 0x5e28 into bits 31:16 of w10, which gives 0x5e288000, and the AND operation against the instruction bits isolates those same bits for comparison.\\n\\nLooking at the generated code structure, each bitPatternMatcher in the generated decoder maps to a specific mask-and-match constant pair, so I need to identify which pattern constant corresponds to the FMLS instruction in the actual disassembly. Rather than tracing through all the comparison instructions, I should just disassemble the `decodeFpMulMadd` function directly to see what's actually happening.\",\"signature\":\"ab2b3252affe831f8b676eef2e96aa197b7ae11d40128b9da35159d286271ed2\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":2561,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":113295},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"8ebe1a6e-98e1-4b31-9299-c23c5cd31a68\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:34:24.695Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"8ebe1a6e-98e1-4b31-9299-c23c5cd31a68\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d24af8b02e8efbee41ef09d6e9373\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me disassemble `decodeFpMulMadd` to verify the binary has the correct logic:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":2561,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":113295},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"973aa8bf-7430-405f-8e9d-c6e5620c47db\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:34:25.160Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\