ng long)machInst, (unsigned)((machInst >> 32) & 0xFFFFFFFF));\\n82136\\t        if (isCryptoAES(machInst)) return decodeCryptoAES(machInst);\\n82137\\t        if (isCrypto3SHA(machInst)) return decodeCryptoThreeRegSHA(machInst);\\n82138\\t        if (isCrypto2SHA(machInst)) return decodeCryptoTwoRegSHA(machInst);\\n82139\\t        if (isAdvSIMDScCopy(machInst)) return decodeNeonScCopy(machInst);\\n82140\\t        if (isAdvSIMDSc3SameFp16(machInst)) return decodeNeonSc3SameFp16(machInst);\\n82141\\t        if (isAdvSIMDSc2RegMiscFp16(machInst)) return decodeNeonSc2RegMiscFp16(machInst);\\n82142\\t        if (isAdvSIMDSc3SameExtra(machInst)) return decodeNeonSc3SameExtra(machInst);\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"9b154c82-6e12-4085-9c47-3b80b569d7fd\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:50:40.696Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"ba73d600-a760-4fac-b048-3ed88e9b5dd9\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"9b154c82-6e12-4085-9c47-3b80b569d7fd\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d28a13f3de0ec176859754568d024\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"The debug print IS in the generated code at the beginning of `decodeFpAdvSIMD`. But it never appears before the panic. This means `decodeFpAdvSIMD` is NEVER called. The instruction `0x1e2e1000` must be reaching `Unknown64` through a DIFFERENT path.\\n\\nLet me think about this differently. What if the instruction is NOT reaching `decodeFpAdvSIMD` at all? What if `decodeFpAdvSIMD` only handles `bits27:25=111` but the instruction reaches a DIFFERENT handler?\\n\\nWait - from the decode-method.cc.inc at line 2910-2915:```cpp\\n\\n\\nelse {    // bit 27:25=111    switch(decoderFlavor){    default:        return decodeFpAdvSIMD<GenericDecoder>(machInst);    }}\\n```\\n\\nThis means: if `bit27:25=111` AND none of the other conditions match, then call `decodeFpAdvSIMD`. But what if ONE of the other conditions MATCHES first?\\n\\nThe `if-else` chain is:```cpp\\nif (bits(machInst, 27) == 0x0) { ... }\\nelse if (bits(machInst, 25) == 0) { ...\\n```\\n\\nNow I need to trace through the actual conditional logic more carefully. The check for `bits27:25=111` comes after several other conditions, so one of those earlier branches must be matching first and returning a different handler.\\n\\nLet me work through the bit analysis for the instruction: bits 27-25 are all 1, and bit 26 is also 1, which means the `if (bits(machInst, 26) == 0)` check fails, so execution should reach the final `else` clause where I return the `decodeFpAdvSIMD` handler. bit 25 is 1, so this check also fails. Since bit 24 is 0, not 1, the third condition fails too, which means we correctly fall through to the final else block and return `decodeFpAdvSIMD`. But the debug output isn't appearing, which suggests either the build didn't actually use the new binary, scons ran from a different directory than expected, or the object files weren't rebuilt despite the source changes.\\n\\nLet me verify by touching the file to force a rebuild and checking the resulting binary with objdump.\",\"signature\":\"e07d0ccaad038197315098b5647271e11d96cc35a1442dbae5da82838c0e757f\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":8,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":145144},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"