46-ebd4030c2649\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d2a733cdac923d89c5e4b445d0a20\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"The \\\"DEBUG Unknown path\\\" is NOT printed, but the entry debug WAS printed. So the execution path is:\\n1. `decodeInst` is called with `machInst=0x1e2e1000`2. The entry debug fires\\n3. ...something happens...\\n4. The instruction ends up at the Unknown fault\\n\\nBut the \\\"DEBUG Unknown path\\\" in `decode-method.cc.inc` is NOT fired.\\n\\nWait - maybe `decodeInst` calls a cached version, or maybe there's a different `decodeInst` being called. Let me check the call stack again:```\\n5   gem5.opt  REDACTED + 0\\n\\n\\n```\\n\\nThis `onUDelay` function is suspicious - it's not the normal decode path. Let me look at what this function does.\\n\\nActually, let me look at this more carefully. The backtrace shows:\\n```\\n5   gem5.opt  REDACTED + 0\\n6   gem5.opt  REDACTED + 248\\n7   gem5.opt  REDACTED\\n\\n```\\n\\nSo the crash is happening in `UndefinedInstruction::invoke`, which means the instruction decode succeeded but the execution failed. The issue might be that `decodeInst` is being called, but then something in the cache or execution path is returning an unknown instruction instead of the properly decoded one. I'm seeing the full backtrace now—the undefined instruction exception is triggering a call to `onUDelay`, which seems odd at first, but that function is actually designed to handle various delay and synchronization operations in the Linux ISA layer, so it makes sense as part of the exception handling mechanism. I'm looking at the `UndefinedInstruction::invoke` method to understand how it handles instruction faults in the simulator. The method checks if the system is running in full simulation mode, and if so, delegates to the ARM fault handler.\\n\\nThen there's a debug assertion that ensures either the `unknown` flag is set or the mnemonic is defined, followed by a cast to access the ARM-specific instruction details. If the instruction is disabled, it panics with a message; otherwise it proceeds with another panic check.\\n\\nWait, I'm noticing something odd here—the panic function itself appears to be calling `UndefinedInstruction::invoke`, which would create a circular dependency where the panic handler is triggering the same fault it's trying to handle.\\n\\nLet me look at the actual binary to see what's really going on at that panic address.\",\"signature\":\"24bdb7a9b63e724d830c34ad583fdaf81a3b0f5788bd8781c0553374d2f0a7c4\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":252,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":155343},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"9de172f1-4231-4202-acd5-0cc462a23b2e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:58:45.442Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"9de172f1-4231-4202-acd5-0cc462a23b2e\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d2a733cdac923d89c5e4b445d0a20\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me check the binary at the `onUDelay` address:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":252,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":155343},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"5b4ff723-5017-4e3d-9c37-0274be58d1b0\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:58:46.041Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4