93\\t                                      // Unknown::unknown(([], {}))\\n794\\t                                      return new Unknown(machInst);\\n795\\t                                      break;\\n796\\t                                    }\\n797\\t                                  GEM5_UNREACHABLE;\\n798\\t                                \\n799\\t                                case 0x0: \\n800\\t                                  switch (IS_MISC) {\\n801\\t                                    \\n802\\t                                    case 0x0:   \\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"71e4f34b-b6f3-4894-b832-db7a9e3d544e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:43:26.382Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"ca891740-8568-4966-8228-2e0339c89f77\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"71e4f34b-b6f3-4894-b832-db7a9e3d544e\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d34feec5972c915916867a6afaca9\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Now I see the full structure:\\n\\n```\\nswitch (AARCH64):\\n  case 0x0:     // COND_CODE cases\\n    case 0xf:\\n      // ArmUnconditional (bits31:28=0xF)\\n      ...\\n      return new Unknown(machInst);  // fall-through from all non-matching paths\\n      break;\\n    default:     // COND_CODE != 0xf\\n      switch (ENCODING):\\n        case 0x0:\\n          switch (SEVEN_AND_FOUR):\\n            case 0x1:\\n              // Multiply/multiply-accumulate instructions\\n              ...\\n              return new Unknown(machInst);\\n\\n\\n```\\n\\nThe else branch handles the Multiply/Accumulate encodings when SEVEN_AND_FOUR equals 0x1. For the FMLS instruction with COND_CODE=0x1, it should take this path, but I'm noticing something odd in the instruction encoding—the bits don't seem to match what I'd expect for this instruction type.\\n\\nLooking more carefully at the bit pattern: bits 7 and 4 are both 0, which would make SEVEN_AND_FOUR = 0, not 1. That means FMLS is actually matching case 0x0 instead of case 0x1, which contradicts what I initially thought about the encoding scheme. Looking at the bit pattern more carefully, I need to map out which bits correspond to which positions in the instruction encoding to understand why it's not matching the expected case. I'm realizing there's a mismatch between my manual decoding and what the bit pattern function should produce—the `bitPatternMatcher` template in the Python code defines the expected bit positions differently than how I've been interpreting them, so I need to reconcile how the function actually maps bits to the instruction encoding versus my step-by-step extraction. Now I'm mapping out the remaining bits from the instruction encoding, tracing through the bitfields to extract op1, op2, and the condition code, then working backwards from the actual instruction value to verify each field extraction. The switch evaluates op1 & 0xf7, which gives 0x68, and since this value isn't defined in any case statement, execution falls through to line 291 where the function returns a new Unknown instruction. This confirms that FMLS isn't being decoded correctly by the AARCH64 decoder path. Now I need to check whether the AARCH64 path is even being invoked.\\n\\nLooking at the AARCH64 switch structure, there's only a case 0x0 defined and no case 0x1. For FMLS with COND_CODE=0x1, it takes the default COND_CODE path, but SEVEN_AND_FOUR is 0 for this instruction, so it falls into case 0x0 of the SEVEN_AND_FOUR switch instead.\\n\\nNow I'm tracing through what happens in the IS_MISC switch. Since bit 24 is 0x1 (1) and bits 23 are 0x2 (2), the IS_MISC value would be 0 AND 2, which equals 0.\\n\\nSo IS_MISC is 0, meaning this instruction falls into the first case of the IS_MISC switch. I need to look at what's at line 802 to understand what decoding path this follows.\",\"signature\":\"d45f2f7b6a6003951d96bba313c1793545e3ce7c3fd157c910430194ff5dc067\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":871,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":65994},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"856d5726-3dbc-4f3b-90c3-e1d715eb3da4\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:43:48.576Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"856d5726-3dbc-4f3b-90c3-e1d715eb3da4\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d34feec5972c915916867a6afaca9\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Now I understand the AARCH64=0 path structure. FMLS (COND_CODE=0x1 != 0xf) falls to `default:` → `switch(ENCODING)` → `switch(SEVEN_AND_FOUR)`. Let me trace through: `0x1e2e1000` has bits 7=0 and 4=0, so SEVEN_AND_FOUR=0. Let me trace the IS_MISC path.\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":871,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":65994},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"4a94b2b3-f5de-41ee-8be5-8fd0bf30c340\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:43:52.219Z\",\"userType\":\"exte