 `moreBytes` function in decoder.cc to see what `pc.aarch64()` and `pc.thumb()` return. Or better, let me add debug in `decodeInst` to show ALL the fields that matter.\\n\\nActually, I already have that debug. From the output:\\n```\\nDBG decodeInst ENTRY: mach=0x1e2e1000 THUMB=0 AARCH64=0\\n\\n\\n```\\n\\nThis tells me that when decodeInst is called, both THUMB and AARCH64 are 0. The THUMB=0 makes sense, but AARCH64=0 is wrong for ARM64 code. Since these values come from the instruction bits, I need to figure out which bit in the 64-bit machInst is actually being used to determine the AARCH64 state. Let me look at the decoder code to see how it's extracting this information from the instruction.\\n\\nLooking at the moreBytes function, it's setting emi.aarch64 directly from pc.aarch64(). The debug output confirms that when decodeInst is called, AARCH64 is indeed 0. I could add debug output to moreBytes to trace where this flag is getting set, but the real question is whether the PC is being initialized correctly as AArch64 when the program starts, or if there's something else happening during the decoding process.\\n\\nThe solution would be to either fix how the PC initializes in AArch64 mode or add a fallback check in the decoder that examines the instruction encoding itself. Since the AARCH64 bit is determined by the PSTATE.nRW bit in the CPSR, I need to verify whether the PC state is being set correctly when the workload begins.\",\"signature\":\"ccd9b761c5e1f305b558ff0e8ce1c127e67dc1c4d3f03a5f2543cb3117b28667\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":96,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":71043},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"44aa5754-5461-45aa-b28b-f632c8be308a\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:45:29.748Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"session