\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"3500291b-e115-46a7-80fb-7571f6bf3abe\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_eksou9hl0pjm_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"40\\t\\n41\\tBareMetalWorkload::BareMetalWorkload(const Params &p) : SEWorkload(p)\\n42\\t{}\\n43\\t\\n44\\tvoid\\n45\\tBareMetalWorkload::initState()\\n46\\t{\\n47\\t    SEWorkload::initState();\\n48\\t\\n49\\t    // Get entry point from the first process via ThreadContext\\n50\\t    ThreadContext *tc = system->threads[0];\\n51\\t    Process *proc = tc->getProcessPtr();\\n52\\t\\n53\\t    // CRITICAL: Call the Process subclass's initState() explicitly\\n54\\t    // This ensures ArmBareMetalProcess64::initState() is called which maps pages\\n55\\t    proc->initState();\\n56\\t\\n57\\t    // Get entry after process initState (in case it changes)\\n58\\t    Addr entry = proc->objFile->entryPoint();\\n59\\t\\n60\\t    // Set PC via ArmPCState\\n61\\t    ArmISA::PCState pc;\\n62\\t    pc.pc(entry);\\n63\\t    tc->pcState(pc);\\n64\\t\\n65\\t    // Activate first thread, suspend others\\n66\\t    for (auto *tc: system->threads) {\\n67\\t        if (tc->contextId() == 0) {\\n68\\t            tc->activate();\\n69\\t        } else {\\n70\\t            tc->suspend();\\n71\\t        }\\n72\\t    }\\n73\\t}\\n74\\t\\n75\\t} // namespace ArmISA\\n76\\t\\n77\\tnamespace\\n78\\t{\\n79\\t\\n80\\t/**\\n81\\t * BareMetalLoader — matches ARM64/ARM bare-metal ELFs (no OSABI = SYSV or ELF\\n82\\t * with OSABI set to something other than Linux/FreeBSD) and instantiates\\n83\\t * ArmBareMetalProcess instead of ArmProcess64/ArmLinuxProcess64.\\n84\\t *\\n85\\t * The loader is registered via static initialization so it participates in\\n86\\t * Process::tryLoaders().  It only matches when:\\n87\\t *   - Architecture is Arm64, Arm, or Thumb\\n88\\t *   - Operating system is Unknown (not Linux / FreeBSD / etc.)\\n89\\t *   - No ELF interpreter is present (static binaries only)\\n90\\t */\\n91\\tclass BareMetalLoader : public Process::Loader\\n92\\t{\\n93\\t  public:\\n94\\t    Process *\\n95\\t    load(const ProcessParams &params, loader::ObjectFile *obj) override\\n96\\t    {\\n97\\t        auto arch = obj->getArch();\\n98\\t        auto opsys = obj->getOpSys();\\n99\\t\\n100\\t        // Only handle ARM architectures\\n101\\t        if (arch != loader::Arm && arch != loader::Thumb &&\\n102\\t            arch != loader::Arm64) {\\n103\\t            return nullptr;\\n104\\t        }\\n105\\t\\n106\\t        // Only handle truly bare-metal binaries (no OS, or explicit bare-metal)\\n107\\t        // If it's Linux, FreeBSD, or other OS — let the appropriate loader handle it\\n108\\t        if (opsys != loader::UnknownOpSys)\\n109\\t            return nullptr;\\n110\\t\\n111\\t        // Bare-metal ELF should have no interpreter (static binary)\\n112\\t        if (obj->getInterpreter())\\n113\\t            return nullptr;\\n114\\t\\n115\\t        // Create the appropriate ArmBareMetalProcess based on word width\\n116\\t        if (arch == loader::Arm64)\\n117\\t            return new ArmISA::ArmBareMetalProcess64(params, obj, arch);\\n118\\t        else\\n119\\t            return new ArmISA::ArmBareMetalProcess32(params, obj, arch);\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"d17b4514-951f-4658-a2c1-fd700bdf6f3c\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:46:01.971Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"3500291b-e115-46a7-80fb-7571f6bf3abe\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"d17b4514-951f-4658-a2c1-fd700bdf6f3c\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d359afd17e56a15e31d27ff9bc331\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Line 61-63:\\n```cpp\\nArmISA::PCState pc;\\npc.pc(entry);\\ntc->pcState(pc);\\n```\\n\\nThe PC is set with just `pc.pc(entry)`. But `ArmISA::PCState pc` creates a PCState on the stack. What are the initial flag values? Let me check the PCState constructor.\",\"signature\":\"0cfb01f5b9ec24852ddc5b2f07f64bd75de0ec0599e19a363ccaf6b286fe686b\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":914,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":74576},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"e3a964da-8a0f-4820-b775-28122525d4bc\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:46:10.688Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"e3a964da-8a0f-4820-b775-28122525d4bc\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d359afd17e56a15e31d27ff9bc331\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Found it. Line 62: `pc.pc(entry)` - but PCState flags are uninitialized. Let me check the default flag state.\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":914,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":74576},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"1d6bbf5f-f7dd-4e7b-b369-13f25f89acf6\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:46:11.480Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"1d6bbf5f-f7dd-4e7b-b369-13f25f89acf6\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d359afd17e56a15e31d27ff9bc331\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_function_za062e2il1om_1\",\"name\":\"Read\",\"input\":{\"file_path\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5/src/arch/arm/pcstate.hh\",\"offset\":78,\"limit\":50}}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":\"tool_use\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":914,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":74576,\"output_tokens\":170,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"887d0cd6-038f-45c2-99e4-37345db940b4\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:46:12.232Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"887d0cd6-038f-45c2-99e4-37345db940b4\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_za062e2il1om_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"78\\t        AArch64Bit = (1 << 2)\\n79\\t    };\\n80\\t\\n81\\t    uint8_t flags = 0;\\n82\\t    uint8_t nextFlags = 0;\\n83\\t    uint8_t _itstate = 0;\\n84\\t    uint8_t _nextItstate = 0;\\n85\\t    uint8_t _size = 0;\\n86\\t    bool _illegalExec = false;\\n87\\t\\n88\\t    // Software Step flags\\n89\\t    bool _debugStep = false;\\n90\\t    bool _stepped = false;\\n91\\t\\n92\\t  public:\\n93\\t    void\\n94\\t    set(Addr val) override\\n95\\t    {\\n96\\t        Base::set(val);\\n97\\t        npc(val + (thumb() ? 2 : 4));\\n98\\t    }\\n99\\t\\n100\\t    PCState(const PCState &other) : Base(other),\\n101\\t        flags(other.flags), nextFlags(other.nextFlags),\\n102\\t        _itstate(other._itstate), _nextItstate(other._nextItstate),\\n103\\t        _size(other._size), _illegalExec(other._illegalExec),\\n104\\t        _debugStep(other._debugStep), _stepped(other._stepped)\\n105\\t    {}\\n106\\t    PCState &operator=(const PCState &other) = default;\\n107\\t\\n108\\t    PCState() {}\\n109\\t    explicit PCState(Addr val) { set(val); }\\n110\\t\\n111\\t    PCStateBase *clone() const override { return new PCState(*this); }\\n112\\t\\n113\\t    void\\n114\\t    update(const PCStateBase &other) override\\n115\\t    {\\n116\\t        Base::update(other);\\n117\\t        auto &pcstate = other.as<PCState>();\\n118\\t        flags = pcstate.flags;\\n119\\t        nextFlags = pcstate.nextFlags;\\n120\\t        _itstate = pcstate._itstate;\\n121\\t        _nextItstate = pcstate._nextItstate;\\n122\\t        _size = pcstate._size;\\n123\\t        _illegalExec = pcstate._illegalExec;\\n124\\t        _debugStep = pcstate._debugStep;\\n125\\t        _stepped = pcstate._stepped;\\n126\\t    }\\n127\\t\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"c5655971-159b-432c-8ddc-822e76c991be\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:46:12.356Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"887d0cd6-038f-45c2-99e4-37345db940b4\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"c5655971-159b-432c-8ddc-822e76c991be\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d35a41269aca672bf6b4527b41c73\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Line 81: `uint8_t flags = 0;` - default value is 0. So `pc.flags = 0`, which means:- `AArch64Bit = (1 << 2) = 0x04`\\n- `pc.flags & AArch64Bit = 0x00 & 0x04 = 0`\\n- `pc.aarch64() = false` (AARCH64=0)So the PC is initialized with AARCH64=0, which is wrong for ARM64 binaries.But how did the first instruction (FMLS = 0x1e2e1000) get decoded correctly before? Looking at the output:\\n\\n\\n`DBG decodeInst ENTRY: mach=0x1e2e1000 THUMB=0 AARCH64=0` - THUMB=0, AARCH64=0\\n\\nWith AARCH64=0, the code takes the ARM mode path instead of AARCH64, which is incorrect for an ARM64 binary. This should have caused a panic, but the error says the instruction attempted to execute as an unknown instruction.\\n\\nThe real issue is that the bare-metal workload's PC is being initialized with AARCH64=0, so when decodeInst runs, it goes down the ARM decode path instead of the AARCH64 decode path, finds no match for the instruction, and returns Unknown. I need to fix the PC initialization to properly set the AARCH64 flag based on the binary's architecture.\",\"signature\":\"658542ed235f9e714af730094cf7050e6718475e58d977b1bd449dc3ff5d8a78\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":730,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":75446},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"1aa10e24-ef7a-40ac-8524-366950fe280b\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:46:22.280Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"1aa10e24-ef7a-40ac-8524-366950fe280b\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d35a41269aca672bf6b4527b41c73\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"ROOT CAUSE FOUND. `flags = 0` means `aarch64() = false`. The bare-metal workload initializes PC without setting the AArch64 bit. Let me check how ArmBareMetalProcess64 detects ARM64 and fixes this.\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":730,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":75446},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"7debd772-41c2-428b-a38b-7bd3a6dfc2c1\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:46:24.328Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"7debd772-41c2-428b-a38b-7bd3a6dfc2c1\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d35a41269aca672bf6b4527b41c73\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_function_g09m96o68vzs_1\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"grep -rn \\\"ArmBareMetal