orking through the bit positions systematically. Now I'm checking the instruction against the remaining patterns—verifying that bits 23-21 match the expected values and examining how the opcode and operand fields are structured for this particular instruction encoding. Now I'm checking the isFpCompare pattern against the instruction bits, and I'm seeing it fail at bit 15 where the expected value is '1' but the instruction has '0'. Let me verify this against the actual ARM instruction encoding to make sure I have the right opcode breakdown. I'm checking the isFpCondCompare pattern now, looking at how bits 20-15 map to the expected values. The first few bits match (bit 20 is 0 as expected), but I'm noticing the pattern for bits 19-15 is '1' when the actual instruction has different values, so this might be where the mismatch occurs.\\n\\nRather than continuing to manually verify each pattern, I should look at the actual decode function or test case to see what's really happening. Let me trace through the FMADD instruction encoding and compare it against the isFpDataProc pattern to understand which bits actually need to match for this instruction class. I'm analyzing the bit patterns for different instruction types—FpDataProc, FpDataProc2Source, and FpDataProc1Source—and checking whether the FMADD instruction matches the FpDataProc2Source pattern by comparing the opcode bits systematically. Continuing to verify the remaining bits against the pattern—bits 14 and 10-3 all match their expected values, but bit 15 is the issue: the pattern expects '1' while the actual instruction has '0', so this doesn't match the FpDataProc2Source pattern either.\\n\\nLet me check the FpDataProc pattern instead, which requires bit 15 to be '1' and bits 3-0 to be '0b0001', but this instruction has '0' in bit 15, so that doesn't match either.\\n\\nMoving on to FpDataProc1Source, which looks more promising since it doesn't constrain bit 15. I'm verifying each bit against the pattern, and so far bits 31, 29, 28-24, 23, 22, 20-18, and 17 are all matching up correctly. Now I'm checking the remaining bits 16-15 and the opcode fields to see if the entire instruction matches the FpDataProc pattern. Now checking the remaining bits through bit 3, and they all match 'X' patterns as expected. This means the FMADD instruction at 0x1e204020 actually matches the isFpDataProc1Source pattern, but the user is saying it's failing to decode—let me trace through what's actually happening in the decode function. I'm checking the bit ranges against the pattern template. The FMADD instruction has '0' at bit 18, but the pattern expects '1' there, so it fails to match. Let me verify the exact bit positions and the corresponding pattern values to understand where the mismatch occurs. 0 (bit 23)\\n- 'X' at bit 22\\n- 'X' at bit 21\\n- 'X' at bit 20\\n- '1' at bit 19\\n- '1' at bit 18 ← MISMATCH!\\n\\nSo FMADD doesn't match isFpDataProc1Source because bit 18 is '0' but the pattern requires '1'.\\n\\nNow checking isFpCompare: the bits match up through bit 20, but bit 15 has a '0' when the pattern expects a '1', so this instruction doesn't match either.\\n\\nMoving on to isFpImmediate, where bits 31 through 21 all align with the pattern as well.\\n\\nNow checking isFpCondCompare: the first few bits match up correctly, but then bit 15 has a '0' when the pattern expects a '1', so this one also fails.\\n\\nMoving on to isFpDataProc2Source, which is showing similar mismatches at the bit level.\\n\\nContinuing through isFpCondSel and isFpDataProc patterns, both are failing at bit 15 as well.\\n\\nSo FMADD doesn't actually match any of the decoder patterns currently defined, which means the instruction decoder has a gap and needs to be updated.\\n\\nThe real problem is that the isFpDataProc1Source pattern is too restrictive—it requires bit 18 to be '1', but FMADD and FMLA variants have bit 18 set to '0'. Looking at the ARMv8 reference manual, these instructions are all part of the FP multiply-add category with different opcodes, and they all fall into this same bit pattern space where bit 18 distinguishes between the multiply-add/subtract variants and the single-operation variants.\\n\\nThe key insight is that bit 15 (the o1 field) separates the multiply-add instructions from the others: when o1=0, we get FMADD, FMSUB, FNMADD, and FNMSUB, and the decoder logic needs to account for this distinction rather than requiring bit 18 to be '1'.\\n\\nLet me reconsider the pattern matching for isFpDataProc1Source—checking whether FMADD at 0x1e204020 actually matches it, and if not, which pattern it should route through instead. I see the issue now—`isFpDataProc` matches FMADD correctly based on the bit pattern, so the routing should work. But the user reported that FMADD fails to decode, which means either the bit pattern matching is broken or there's something wrong with the actual test case they're running. Let me reconsider whether the pattern I identified is actually what's being used in the current code. Let me trace through the FMADD instruction encoding more carefully by looking at the actual binary representation. I need to verify each bit position matches the pattern requirements for the multiply-add variant specifically. Now I'm checking the isFpDataProc pattern against the instruction encoding, verifying each bit position matches the expected values for the opcode and operand fields. Continuing through the bit comparison for isFpCompare—the first 15 bits all match the pattern, but bit 15 has a mismatch where the instruction has 0 instead of the expected 1, so this comparison fails.\\n\\nNow checking isFpCondSel with a similar bit pattern...\\n\\nChecking isFpDataProc1Source now... I'm continuing to verify the bit pattern match for the remaining fields, and it looks like there's a mismatch at bit 18 where the actual instruction has 0 but the pattern expects 1, so this doesn't align with the isFpDataProc1Source instruction either.\\n\\nLet me check the other instruction patterns like isFpDataProc and isFpDataProc2Source to see if there's a better match. The isFpDataProc2Source pattern seems to have a similar issue at bit 15, but isFpDataProc matches perfectly through all the bit positions.\\n\\nThis is strange though—if FMADD is matching isFpDataProc, it should be routed to decodeFpDataProc, which should handle it correctly. But the user is reporting it's failing to decode. Either there's something else going on, or maybe the user is asking me to implement support for FMADD as a new feature rather than fix an existing bug. Let me reconsider what they're actually asking for.\\n\\nLooking at their description again, they're saying FMADD, FMLA, and FMOV aren't being routed correctly to the decode function. For FMADD specifically, I've verified it matches the isFpDataProc pattern and should route there, but I need to double-check the routing order in the main decode function to see if something else might be intercepting it first.\\n\\nThe routing checks happen sequentially—each pattern is tested in order—so FMADD should definitely reach decodeFpDataProc if it matches isFpDataProc. Let me look more carefully at the actual bit patterns in the code to see if there's a discrepancy between what I'm analyzing and what's really there. I'm continuing to map out the bit pattern for this instruction, breaking down the remaining fields and their values to verify the encoding matches what I'm seeing in the instruction encoding table.\\n\\nNow I'm checking the full 32-bit instruction against the expected pattern for the FMADD variant, working through each bit position systematically to confirm the opcode and operand fields align correctly.\\n\\nWait, I think I've been looking at the wrong bit pattern—I need to reconsider which instruction encoding I'm actually examining. Let me pull up the complete isFpDataProc pattern definition from the file to see the full context of what pattern I'm working with.\",\"signature\":\"8c1beee