ut is not a terminal, disabling listeners.\\nsrc/arch/arm/faults.cc:785: panic: Attempted to execute unknown instruction (inst 0x1e2e1000)\\nMemory Usage: 409214368 KBytes\\nProgram aborted at tick 0\\n--- BEGIN LIBC BACKTRACE ---\\n0   gem5.opt                            0x0000000102209058 _ZN4gem515print_backtraceEv + 44\\n1   gem5.opt                            0x000000010221c7f0 _ZN4gem512abortHandlerEi + 68\\n2   libsystem_platform.dylib            0x0000000191e08624 _sigtramp + 56\\n3   libsystem_pthread.dylib             0x0000000191dce88c pthread_kill + 296\\n4   libsystem_c.dylib                   0x0000000191cd7c60 abort + 124\\n5   gem5.opt                            0x0000000100982b7c REDACTED + 0\\n6   gem5.opt                            0x0000000100dd95d8 REDACTED + 248\\n7   gem5.opt                            0x0000000100d0bf14 REDACTED + 184\\n8   gem5.opt                            0x0000000100cf75fc _ZN4gem515AtomicSimpleCPU4tickEv + 1316\\n9   gem5.opt                            0x00000001022152e0 _ZN4gem510EventQueue10serviceOneEv + 244\\n10  gem5.opt                            0x000000010222ebb4 _ZN4gem59doSimLoopEPNS_10EventQueueE + 280\\n11  gem5.opt                            0x000000010222e5bc _ZN4gem58simulateEy + 496\\n12  gem5.opt                            0x0000000100a171bc REDACTED + 88\\n13  gem5.opt                            0x0000000100a17158 REDACTED + 24\\n14  gem5.opt                            0x00000001009ad34c REDACTED + 4068\\n15  Python                              0x00000001068f9f04 cfunction_call + 72\\n16  Python                              0x0000000106892518 _PyObject_Call + 116\\n17  Python                              0x00000001069c77a0 _PyEval_EvalFrameDefault + 24752\\n18  Python                              0x00000001069c11f4 PyEval_EvalCode + 248\\n19  Python                              0x00000001069bc190 builtin_exec + 376\\n20  Python                              0x00000001068923ec PyObject_Vectorcall + 88\\n21  Python                              0x00000001069c70b4 _PyEval_EvalFrameDefault + 22980\\n22  Python                              0x00000001069c13ec _PyEval_Vector + 268\\n23  gem5.opt                            0x0000000100a02d24 REDACTED + 68\\n24  gem5.opt                            0x000000010221ec88 main + 284\\n25  dyld                                0x0000000191a2eb98 start + 6076\\n--- END LIBC BACKTRACE ---\\nFor more info on how to address this issue, please visit https://www.gem5.org/documentation/general_docs/common-errors/\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"70774241-aa68-4d5c-836a-a5f15f233ca5\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"2b4c5740-784a-499f-98ec-d3de9135726a\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d08d7846a6c512c622558e45bb5bd\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Now I found the actual issue! The panic is:\\n```\\npanic: Attempted to execute unknown instruction (inst 0x1e2e1000)\\n```\\n\\nLet me decode this instruction:\\n0x1e2e1000 = 0b00011110001011100001000000000000\\n\\nBit 31:24 = 00011110 = 0x1e\\nBit 23:21 = 010 = 0x2\\nBit 20:16 = 00001 = 0x01\\nBit 15 = 0\\nSo op21 = 0100 = 0x4\\n\\n\\nI'm working through the bit matching against the isFpDataProc1Source pattern to see where it fails.\\n\\nMatching against the full 22-bit pattern from position 31 down to 10, I get 0001111001010001000000, but the pattern expects 00011110001000XXXXXXXX10000. The mismatch occurs at bits 19:18, where the instruction has 01 but the pattern requires 10, so this instruction doesn't match the isFpDataProc1Source pattern. I'm checking this against the isFpDataProc2Source pattern with the bits 0001111001010001000000, but finding a mismatch at bits 19:18 where the value is 01 instead of the expected 11, so this pattern doesn't match either.\\n\\nNow I'm verifying the isFpDataProc pattern, which covers the range 31:10, and the bits align perfectly with the expected 22-bit sequence, so the FMADD instruction is correctly routing to the decode function.\\n\\nHowever, I'm noticing that the decode function calculates a switch value from bits 23:21 and 15, giving 0x4 for this instruction, which should map to a valid case for the FMADD Dd instruction. The panic is occurring before we even reach that switch statement though—it's being triggered at tick 0, which is suspicious and suggests the issue might be happening during initialization rather than execution.\\n\\nLooking more closely at the panic message and the call stack, I'm seeing a reference to `onUDelay` and `UndefinedInstruction`, which makes me think the instruction 0x1e2e1000 is hitting the undefined instruction handler rather than being recognized as a valid FMADD instruction. I need to trace through the bit pattern more carefully to see where the instruction is being misclassified.\\n\\nExamining the FMADD instruction encoding, I can verify that 0x1e2e1000 matches the pattern for FMADD Dd with specific register fields, so the instruction itself is valid. The panic must be coming from somewhere else in the pipeline. I'm checking the bit patterns for both instructions and neither matches—FMADD has 01 in bits 19:18 while FMLA has 10, so these are definitely distinct encodings. I need to reconsider what the decoder is actually doing here.\\n\\nLet me trace through the routing: for 0x1e2e1000, the top-level decode confirms it should go to `decodeFpAdvSIMD` based on the instruction class bits, and then the isFpDataProc check passes, routing it to `decodeFpDataProc`. Within that function, the switch statement evaluates to 0x4, which maps to the FMADD Dd case and should instantiate the correct instruction object.\\n\\nBut looking at the panic message more carefully—\\\"Attempted to execute unknown instruction (inst 0x1e2e1000)\\\"—it seems the instruction is actually being decoded as Unknown64 rather than FMADDD, even though the pattern matching should work. Let me verify whether FMADDD is actually defined in the fp.isa format file, since that could explain why the instruction isn't being recognized despite passing the pattern checks.\",\"signature\":\"9722c24d58e43c9fac455eb433ec1d4e1892043b2dc3c40f20de8a26cbf0cfbb\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1787,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":63458},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"76aac87d-6819-40fb-a354-81ada107fae5\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:35:34.890Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"76aac87d-6819-40fb-a354-81ada107fae5\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d08d7846a6c512c622558e45bb5bd\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Found it. `test_neon.elf` crashes with `inst 0x1e2e1000` - an FMLA instruction. Let me decode it and trace the routing.\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1787,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":63458},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"9dfaec9a-6981-4d30-8372-b1ba9dc54878\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:35:36.422Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"prou