cher so that FMLA-like instructions with bit 21 set and bits 15:10 equal to 0b011010 get correctly routed to the NEON decoder instead of the scalar FP decoder. I need to refine the isFpDataProc2Source pattern to exclude these specific bit patterns, since isAdvSIMDSc2RegMisc should catch them but isn't matching correctly.\\n\\nLooking at the isAdvSIMDSc2RegMisc pattern, the op2 field expects bits[22:19] to be exactly 0b0100, but FMLA has bits[22:19] = 0b0010, which is why it's not being caught.\\n\\nThe solution is to modify isFpDataProc2Source to exclude the FMLA encoding by requiring bit[21] = 0 instead of making it a don't-care. This prevents FMLA from being incorrectly classified as a scalar floating-point multiply operation.\\n\\nI need to verify that no legitimate FpDataProc2Source instructions have bit[21] = 1. Looking at the actual ARM instruction encodings, the scalar FP multiply and divide instructions all have bit[21] = 0, confirming this fix is safe and won't break valid instructions.\\n\\nNow I'm tracing through what happens with FMLA specifically—when bit[21] = 1 and bits[15:10] = 0b011010, this creates the FMLA encoding, but I need to check what other valid scalar FP instructions might also have these bit patterns to ensure my check won't accidentally reject legitimate instructions.\\n\\nLet me look at the actual test cases and artifact files to see what real instructions are causing the collision, rather than working through all the theoretical combinations.\",\"signature\":\"c9c04c9336d2ccb9bcf21889e2dd1e288400f25047f05cd887bf53ab42b39b50\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":2096,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":66423},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"ac1da1d0-073f-4b5b-919d-c07ab40aeb81\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:36:53.173Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/