f9b485ec610bb647d468daa\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Good, my first fix is already in `decodeFpDataProc1Source`. But I need to ALSO add the guard to `decodeFpDataProc2Source` AND add a new pattern before `isFpDataProc1Source` to catch FMLA/FMLS.\\n\\nWait, actually there's a better approach. Let me reconsider.\\n\\nThe current decoder in `decodeFpAdvSIMD`:\\n1. Tries all the NEON patterns first\\n2. Then tries scalar FP patterns\\n\\nFor `0x1e2e1000`:\\n- All NEON patterns fail because `bits[23:22]=00` (isAdvSIMDSc2RegMisc requires `bits[24:23]=01`)\\n\\n\\n- The scalar FP patterns then intercept it incorrectly\\n\\nThe real problem is that FMLA/FMLS has `bits[24:23]=10` which doesn't match the NEON pattern for single-register operations, but it does match the scalar FP decoder because both groups have overlapping bit patterns in the remaining fields. So I need to add a specific guard to prevent FMLA/FMLS from being caught by the scalar FP decoder, either by adding an explicit pattern or by checking the bits directly in the decode function itself.\\n\\nMy approach is to reject FMLA/FMLS within `decodeFpDataProc2Source` since that's where the false match happens, then add a new pattern `isFpMulMadd` to catch FMLA/FMLS before they reach the scalar FP decoder and route them to the correct NEON handler instead. I'm realizing the switch statement uses the opcode field directly, so FMLS with opcode 0x1B would trigger the default case and return Unknown, which explains why the instruction isn't being handled properly in decodeFpDataProc2Source. Now I'm thinking through where to intercept this—whether to add a guard in the decode function itself or create a dedicated pattern matcher to catch these NEON instructions before they hit the scalar path. Looking at the ARM encoding specs more carefully, FMLA and FMLS do have distinct opcodes that should be routed to the same decoder, so the cleanest approach is to create a dedicated pattern matcher `isFpMulMadd` that captures both instructions and route them to `decodeFpMulMadd`. I need to check whether that decode function already exists in neon64.isa or if I need to implement it.\",\"signature\":\"9772abd155b0013566b5527b58785826283b21d7ee285cbb6182e40339c7f560\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":79241},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"fc8bab9c-165b-4264-8c28-67da28a1faf6\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:42:27.916Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"fc8bab9c-165b-4264-8c28-67da28a1faf6\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d0a855f9b485ec610bb647d468daa\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The fix needs two changes: (1) add guard to `decodeFpDataProc2Source`, and (2) add a new pattern `isFpMulMadd` to catch FMLA/FMLS before the FP path. Let me check if `decodeFpMulMadd` already exists in neon64.isa.\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":79241},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"b36acf59-ea9a-481f-ac3d-647317ed400f\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:42:29.216Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"b36acf59-ea9a-481f-ac3d-647317ed400f\",