st)\\n102\\t    {\\n103\\t        uint8_t u_a_opcode = bits(machInst, 29) << 4 | // u\\n104\\t                             bits(machInst, 23) << 3 | // a\\n105\\t                             bits(machInst, 13, 11);   // opcode\\n106\\t\\n107\\t        RegIndex vd = (RegIndex) (uint8_t) bits(machInst, 4, 0);\\n108\\t        RegIndex vn = (RegIndex) (uint8_t) bits(machInst, 9, 5);\\n109\\t        RegIndex vm = (RegIndex) (uint8_t) bits(machInst, 20, 16);\\n110\\t\\n111\\t        uint8_t q = bits(machInst, 30);\\n112\\t\\n113\\t        switch (u_a_opcode) {\\n114\\t          case 0b00000:\\n115\\t            if (q)\\n116\\t                return new FmaxnmQX<uint16_t>(machInst, vd, vn, vm);\\n117\\t            else\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"35cede31-86b2-4a4b-a55a-1434f6d36128\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:42:43.621Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"bd40d82d-491a-4e6f-a478-83920464aad0\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"cbdb0ff6-5969-4cce-a636-5b0b5ab1eb22\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_o6zi825eteqn_2\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"    3565 /Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5/src/arch/arm/isa/formats/neon64.isa\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"uuid\":\"d764e10a-748c-4fee-81e2-80d190e722b7\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:42:43.630Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"cbdb0ff6-5969-4cce-a636-5b0b5ab1eb22\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"d764e10a-748c-4fee-81e2-80d190e722b7\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d0aa39e2c0ccbd9bf930f96a0cd33\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Now I need to:\\n\\n1. Add declaration of `decodeFpMulMadd` to neon64.isa declarations (after line 93, before line 94)\\n2. Add `decodeFpMulMadd` implementation in neon64.isa decoder section (after line 99)\\n3. Add `isFpMulMadd` pattern in aarch64.isa (after `isFpDataProc2Source`)\\n4. Add call in `decodeFpAdvSIMD` before `isFpDataProc1Source`\\n5. Add guard in `decodeFpDataProc2Source`\\n6. Remove the wrong fix from `decodeFpDataProc1Source`\\n\\n\\n7. The real problem is that FMLS with `switchVal=0x02` matches `isFpDataProc2Source` and gets decoded as FADD, which is incorrect. FMLA/FMLS need to route to the NEON decoder instead, but they're currently falling through to the wrong scalar floating-point decoder.\\n\\nThe solution involves creating a dedicated pattern `isFpMulMadd` that specifically catches FMLA/FMLS instructions and routes them to a new `decodeFpMulMadd` function that properly delegates to the NEON decoder. I also need to add a guard in `decodeFpDataProc2Source` to prevent FMLS from being misrouted there.\\n\\nNow I'm realizing the key difference between these instruction types: FMLA/FMLS have `bits[22:19]=0010` (o=1, L=0), while the 32-bit NEON variants have `bits[22:19]=0100` (o=1, L=1). The scalar FMLA/FMLS pattern I'm creating needs to specifically target the `o=1, L=0` case to distinguish it from other FMLS-like instructions.\\n\\nFor the decoder function, I'm implementing a switch that handles the different operand size combinations for FMLA and FMLS across half-precision, single-precision, and double-precision formats, then delegates to the appropriate Neon instruction class based on the size field. I'm mapping out the opcode values for scalar FMLS instructions across different precision levels, and I realize the current opcode check is incomplete—it should specifically handle `0x19` and `0x1B` for the half and single/double variants respectively. I also need to refine the condition by breaking it into separate checks for the opcode bits rather than using a combined range comparison.\\n\\nNow I'm setting up a decoder function for the scalar floating-point multiply-add and multiply-subtract instructions, extracting the opcode from bits 15:10 and determining the size based on the type field from bits 23:22, then mapping those to the appropriate Neon instruction classes. I see the switch statement is handling different opcode cases for the scalar multiply-add and multiply-subtract operations, mapping them to Neon instruction classes with their respective type parameters.\\n\\nNow I'm thinking about the existing code that might need adjustment—the condition I added to check for bit21 and the opcode range should correctly reject the FMLA/FMLS cases without affecting other instructions, since FADD/FMUL won't match those conditions anyway.\\n\\nLet me verify that FMADD/FMSUB are properly routed through `isFpDataProc` to `decodeFpDataProc` in the main decoder. Looking at the instruction encoding, FMADD has op1=1 with bits 15:10=000000, while FMLA and FMLS have op1=1 with different bit patterns. The `isFpDataProc` pattern allows op1=1X, so both should match correctly.\\n\\nMy plan is to create a new pattern matcher `isFpMulMadd` that specifically targets the FMLA/FMLS encoding, implement the corresponding decode function to route them properly, and add guards to prevent false matches in other decode paths. I need to add the forward declaration and then build out the decoder functions with their corresponding patterns. I'm refining the condition to check if bit21 is set and the opcode range falls within 0x19 to 0x1B for the multiply-add/subtract instructions, then removing the old check from the source decoder. Now I'll implement all the necessary changes across the codebase.\",\"signature\":\"de1c467ddb736db17bb058076f90d308dbf4faacb24ef7cb13df17e301ddcd4c\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":405,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":79626},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"3c8f5397-920b-450c-afc8-52f510b87246\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:43:07.804Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"3c8f5397-920b-450c-afc8-52f510b87246\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d0aa39e2c0ccbd9bf930f96a0cd33\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Now I have the full picture. Two changes needed:\\n1. Add a new `isFpMulMadd` pattern + `decodeFpMulMadd` in neon64.isa to catch FMLA/FMLS before FP routing\\n2. Add guard in `decodeFpDataProc2Source` to reject FMLS\\n3. Remove the incorrect guard from `decodeFpDataProc1Source`\\n\\nLet me also check what instruction classes are available in neon64.isa.\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":405,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":79626},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"9a6d9155-b122-4a76-9e51-e12d402d57b7\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:43:10.159Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint