:\"a30ac64b-81ba-4c30-8c17-b012924cffda\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"dd0217f2-a70b-40b1-8457-481c0c0b7dfb\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d0ac3a0f6f27b1121d6a2bc191822\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"These are vector forms, not scalar forms. For the scalar FMLA/FMLS instructions, I need to find or create the appropriate classes.\\n\\nLet me check if there are scalar FP instruction templates in the neon64.isa file that handle scalar forms.Actually, looking at the ARM ISA, the scalar FMLA/FMLS instructions have the same encoding as vector forms. They differ only in the Q bit (bit 30): Q=0 means scalar, Q=1 means vector. The decoder dispatches to the same `FmlaDX`/`FmlaQX` and `FmlsDX`/`FmlsQX` classes based on Q.\\n\\n\\nSo using `FmlaDX`/`FmlaQX` for scalar FMLA should work correctly—the DX variant handles scalar double-precision when Q=0, while the QX variant handles vector operations. I'm verifying that the FMLA scalar instruction correctly maps to the `FmlaDX` template, which handles the `Sd = Sd + Sn * Sm` operation, while confirming that `FmlsDX` serves as the scalar counterpart for FMLS with the same accumulator-based multiplication semantics.\\n\\nNow I'm realizing that gem5 uses templates rather than separate scalar/vector classes, so I need to check how the existing decoder handles this to ensure consistency with the architecture.\\n\\nLet me look at how the vector FMLA instructions are decoded to understand the pattern I'm working with. Looking at the NEON instruction encoding more carefully, the U bit in the instruction actually determines whether it's FMLA (U=0) or FMLS (U=1), with the opcode field specifying the operation type. So in the scalar case, I need to extract the U bit and opcode from the instruction encoding to route correctly between these two operations. I see that `decodeNeonUThreeFpReg` handles the dispatch for FMLA and FMLS based on the U bit, so I need to verify whether this same function works for scalar forms or if I need a separate decoder. Let me check how scalar FMUL is currently handled in `decodeFpDataProc2Source` to understand the pattern for scalar floating-point operations.\\n\\nActually, I'm realizing the real issue here: when bit21=1 in the FMLS encoding, it incorrectly matches the `isFpDataProc2Source` pattern, causing the instruction to be dispatched to the wrong decoder. The solution is to catch FMLA/FMLS instructions with a dedicated pattern that recognizes the bit21=1 condition, then route them to the correct NEON decoder that can properly handle scalar forms. I need to verify what instruction classes are actually available in the neon64 templates to properly handle the scalar floating-point multiply-accumulate operations.\",\"signature\":\"82e9f67aa8237c22e9afdc5836b47d91df828fbf9c298d3947cffda2660a2606\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":80990},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"e40129de-b16b-413b-b0c2-bf815088b3f7\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:43:33.609Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"e40129de-b16b-413b-b0c2-bf815088b3f7\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d0ac3a0f6f27b1121d6a2bc191822\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Good - `FmlaDX`/