10\\n\\nSo the upper byte is 0x1E, which confirms the instruction matches the required pattern. Now I need to verify the FMLA variant as well.\\n\\nFor FMLA, bits 20:16 = 11001 (0x19), but the switch value becomes 0x03, which maps to FNMSUB rather than FMLA. This reveals that the issue isn't limited to FMLS alone—FMLA has the same routing problem.\\n\\nSo both instructions share the same root cause: they get incorrectly routed to the wrong instruction handler because their switch values collide with existing scalar FP instructions. The fix needs to handle both FMLS and FMLA by detecting when bit21=1 with bits20:16 in the range 0x19-0x1F, which catches both FMLS (0x1B) and FMLA (0x19).\\n\\nNow I need to write the actual code to implement this fix. Now I'm adding the routing check for the NEON FMLA/FMLS pattern, then updating the decode functions to properly handle these cases across both the scalar and SIMD paths.\",\"signature\":\"438da840a6d354b671fa07e6454a71a5caef46611f25e401ae620d351409ac91\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1838,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":85896},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"25ee8341-61e1-4fc2-8651-c5695aa86bb0\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:48:16.335Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"25ee8341-61e1-4fc2-8651-c5695aa86bb0\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d0b40349e61334fd23b0d25310552\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Now I have the full picture. Let me verify my `switchVal` calculation is correct (my earlier Python had a bug):\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",