pattern to route these instructions to the correct NEON decoder, then add guards to both decodeFpDataProc and decodeFpDataProc2Source to properly handle the FMLA and FMLS cases.\\n\\nLooking at the switch statement in decodeFpDataProc, I notice that case 0x02 correctly identifies FNMSUB, but the FMLA and FMLS instructions with opcode 0x19 aren't being caught by any case—they just fall through to the default Unknown64 handler, which masks the real problem.\\n\\nThe real issue is that FMLA and FMLS are matching the wrong instruction patterns entirely: they match isFpDataProc1Source (which routes to decodeFpDataProc1Source) and isFpDataProc2Source (which routes to decodeFpDataProc2Source) when they should be handled elsewhere.\\n\\nNow I need to trace through what the NEON 3-same decoder expects for these instructions. Looking at the opcode mapping in the NEON decoder, FMLA (opcode 0x19) and FMLS (opcode 0x09) are actually handled there when the size field is less than 2, but the current routing is sending them down the wrong path entirely. Looking at the opcode mapping for these instructions, FMLA and FMLS both need to route to the `decodeNeon3Same` decoder with their specific u values (1 for FMLA, 0 for FMLS), which will then correctly dispatch to the right instantiation functions. Looking at the instruction encoding for the scalar variant, I'm checking the opcode bits for FMLA which should be 0x19, but the switch statement only extracts bits 15:12, which gives 0x1 instead of the full opcode value I need. I'm realizing the switch statement's opcode extraction is giving me the wrong value—it extracts 0x1 from bits 15:12, but the actual opcode 0x19 requires bits 20:16, so the case 0x1 handler won't correctly distinguish between FMADD, FMSUB, and FMLA since they all map to different values in the lower bits.\\n\\nThis suggests either these instructions are intentionally separated into different decode paths, or there's a mismatch in how the opcodes are being parsed. Looking at the actual instruction encoding, FMLA has a different structure than the scalar FP instructions—it's part of the AdvSIMD profile rather than the scalar FP data processing profile, which means they're handled by different decoders entirely.\\n\\nFor the scalar versions, FMLA and FMLS follow the 31-bit encoding with bit 21 set to 1 and specific opcode bits at positions 20:16, while FMSUB uses a different opcode pattern at those same bit positions, confirming they're all distinct operations despite sharing similar encoding schemes.\\n\\nIn the NEON three-same decoder, these instructions map to different switch cases based on their opcode values and whether they're scalar or vector forms. The scalar FMLA and FMLS encodings with Q=0 route to different case handlers than their vector counterparts, with the scalar forms being encoded at 0x19 and 0x09 respectively.\\n\\nNow I'm ready to implement the decoder function that will handle these cases, along with a few supporting modifications.\",\"signature\":\"de7d117eb903d53f94f180d90567843575be5cfcfb708b5763e2cb0942e0b340\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":820,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":87740},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"76a98951-aaa4-4f1d-99a4-bebb8915d795\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:49:44.091Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"76a98951-aaa4-4f1d-99a4-bebb8915d795\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d0c02e15794271df5bb4048477025\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Now I have the complete picture. Let me implement the fix:\\n\\n1. **Re