FMLS instruction.\\n\\nNow I'm realizing the distinction between scalar and vector/neon variants: the scalar half-precision FMLS instruction doesn't exist in gem5's implementation, while the vector variant does. The scalar FMLA half-precision variant does exist, but only the vector form of FMLS is available.\\n\\nLooking at the full scalar FMLS encoding, it appears this instruction simply isn't defined for half-precision at all—only single and double precision variants are available.\\n\\nNow I'm checking the actual instruction encoding for the scalar FMLS case and tracing through the switch statement in the decoder to understand why FMLS maps to a different case than FMLA.\\n\\nLooking at the encoding details, I see that FMLS Hd,Hn,Hm uses a different opcode structure than the vector variant, with bit29 determining whether we're doing a fused multiply-add or multiply-subtract operation. The full scalar half-precision instruction isn't actually encoded in this particular switch case.\\n\\nNow I'm checking the actual FMLS encoding for scalar half-precision registers, which gives me 0x1E3E8000, and I'm working through the bit extraction to verify the opcode field maps correctly to the instruction format. The scalar FMLS half-precision instruction is reserved according to the ARM specification, but I'm noticing the FMLS variant does appear in the half-precision decoder for the NEON case. Let me verify which instruction set variant this actually corresponds to—scalar versus NEON encoding are quite different, so I need to reconsider the structure here.\\n\\nLooking at the NeonSc3SameFp16 pattern for bit23, it's checking for '1', which means for scalar FMLS at 0x1E3E8000, bit23 equals 0. This means the scalar half-precision variant doesn't match the NEON decoder pattern, so I need to check the regular Neon3Same pattern instead.\\n\\nFor the Neon3Same variant, bit29 needs to be 0 for scalar operations, and bit23 needs to be 0 as well, but I'm realizing I should verify what