generating isn't matching what gem5 expects for this CPU type—gem5's `UndefinedInstruction` could be firing because the instruction IS technically defined but triggers a condition that makes it undefined in this context, like an invalid register encoding or operand combination. Looking at the stack trace, the crash happens during execution when `advancePC` is called after an undefined instruction exception, not during decoding itself. So the instruction was decoded successfully, but when the CPU tries to execute it, gem5 throws an undefined instruction exception. This means either the instruction requires a feature or extension that's not enabled in my config, or gem5's implementation of these instructions treats them as undefined on this particular CPU model.\\n\\nThe real issue is likely that gem5 checks whether the FPLS extension (which includes FMLS and FMLA) is available on the CPU, and since I'm using AtomicSimpleCPU with minimal configuration, that extension probably isn't enabled, causing the exception to fire when the instruction attempts to execute. I should verify the ARM architecture extensions available in my configuration and check gem5's source code to understand how it validates floating-point instruction availability at runtime.\",\"signature\":\"5c3850f9c8cb54aef054942f68d158cd7804722b6ccc20d33de236d5fbbee81b\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1318,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":42781},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"bf278a40-ffc0-42ee-85f9-aa43867161a6\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:00:15.187Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"bf278a40-ffc0-42ee-85f9-aa43867161a6\",\"isSid