tween what the generated code says and what I'm actually counting in the pattern itself.\\n\\nI think the issue is that my Python script is miscalculating the mask—it might be using a formula like `pos = 31 - i` which would shift everything incorrectly, when it should be `pos = msb - i` where msb is 31 and i ranges from 0 to 21.\\n\\nThe actual mask should be `0xf9c00000` with match `0x18800000`, and testing with FMLS instruction `0x1e2e1000` should give `{(0x1e2e1000 & 0xf9c00000) == 0x18800000}` which evaluates to True. But the Python script is returning False, so I need to trace through where the calculation is going wrong.\\n\\nLooking at the corrected pattern with fixed bit values for positions 24, 23, and 22, the mask stays `0xf9c00000` and match becomes `0x10800000`, which still produces `False` when I test it against the FMLS instruction. Something's fundamentally broken in how the bit manipulation is working here.\\n\\nLet me manually verify what the instruction bits actually are at those positions. Breaking down `0x1e2e1000` in binary, I can see the bits at positions 31 through 10 don't align with what I'm expecting—the top nibble is `0x1` but the next section should match a different pattern, and position 23 keeps showing `0` when I need it to be `1`.\\n\\nNow I'm realizing the issue: for the bitPattern to match the value, I need to apply the mask `0xf9c00000` correctly. Looking at the mask bits, position 24 is set to 1 and position 23 is also set to 1, but the actual instruction has 0 at position 23, so they won't match. My calculation was correct before—the script just has a bug in how it's comparing the masked values.\\n\\nLooking back at the script output, I can see the corrected mask and match values are being computed properly, but then the comparison is failing because the masked instruction value doesn't actually equal what the match should be. The mask and match formulas are right, but the final verification logic has an error—it's not accounting for the fact that the instruction needs to be masked and then compared to the match value. I'm verifying the bit positions against the pattern characters—when i=8 I need pos=23 to get bit 23=1, and when i=9 I need pos=22 to get bit 22=0, which matches the correct mask of 0xf9c00000.\\n\\nNow I'm realizing the issue: my corrected script was accidentally overwriting mask2 with the same value as the original, so I need to recalculate with the actual fixed pattern to see what's really happening. So bit 23 is set correctly in match2, but the instruction at bit 23 is actually 0, which means (0x1e2e1000 & 0xf9c00000) = 0x18000000 doesn't equal 0x18800000 — the pattern doesn't match the instruction at that bit position. But wait, my earlier manual verification showed bit 23 should be 1...\\n\\nLet me just check the binary representation directly: 0x1e2e1000 in hex breaks down to 0001 1110 0010 1110 0001 0000 0000 0000 in binary, and if I look at the 24th bit position from the right, that's where the mismatch is.\\n\\nActually, I need to clarify how gem5 counts bits — in their convention, bit 31 is the most significant bit, so bit 23 is actually the 24th bit from the right, which is in the lower nibble of the middle byte. I'm realizing my manual bit extraction was completely wrong, so let me write a Python script to verify the bit positions directly and understand how the bitPatternMatcher actually works in gem5.\",\"signature\":\"e9ed9aa82c88df3249815167b35b68f46b355884ace9070501e2663713887e83\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1033,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":86449},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"5d412a45-716d-4667-83e2-c4c6e1faf524\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:17:03.699Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-