ther any of the earlier patterns like `isAdvSIMDSc3Same` are catching these instructions first. Looking at the bit patterns, `isAdvSIMDSc3Same` has specific constraints on bits 29, 11, and 10 that should distinguish it from FMLS, but I need to verify whether the other patterns might be matching before we even get to the floating-point multiplication check. I'm realizing the NEON functions operate on full 128-bit Q registers, while the scalar FMLS instruction I'm checking uses just the lower 64-bit D register, so they should have different encoding patterns. Let me trace through what makes these instruction formats distinct.\\n\\nI'm checking if any of the other isAdvSIMD patterns might actually be matching FMLS instead, since my pattern correctly identifies it but something else in the chain is returning Unknown64. Let me verify what bit23 actually contains in the FMLS encoding. I'm realizing I need to actually test the isFpMulMadd pattern in a debugger rather than doing more manual bit analysis. Let me also reconsider the instruction encoding more carefully — I'm checking what the actual bit patterns are for FMLS variants across different bit positions to make sure I'm not confusing which instruction is which. I'm checking which patterns might be matching FMLS before the expected handler. Looking at the opcode bits for various SIMD operations like FMLS, FMLA, and FCADD, they all fall within the `isAdvSIMDSc3Same` pattern's wildcard space, so I need to verify if any of those earlier patterns are catching FMLS instead. Continuing through the rest of the instruction pattern definitions...\\n\\nI notice the FCADD instruction at 0x1e945000 doesn't match the `isFpMulMadd` pattern either, so it's not interfering. Now I need to actually test this with a debugger to see what's happening.\\n\\nWait, I just realized the panic message is about an unknown instruction at 0x1e2e1000 — not just the \\\"unknown\\\" string. I should check what fault gem5 throws when StaticInst::isUnknown() returns true to understand if this is actually a different error than what I thought.\\n\\nLooking at the fault definitions, when isUnknown() is true, gem5 creates an UndefinedInstruction fault rather than returning an Unknown instruction. So if the decode is returning Unknown64, the fault should be UndefinedInstruction. But the panic at the top level suggests something else is happening — maybe the instruction is being decoded as something that invokes the UndefinedInstruction fault directly.\\n\\nLet me trace through what's happening at execution time with this specific instruction encoding.\\n\\nThe decode appears to work (no panic during decode), but then the execute() method triggers UndefinedInstruction. I've already confirmed the execute methods for both FmlsDX instantiations exist in the binary, so that's not the issue. Maybe the problem is that decodeFpMulMadd isn't returning an FmlsDX instruction at all — perhaps it's returning an Unknown64 somewhere in the switch case, and then the execute() method for Unknown64 is what's throwing the UndefinedInstruction fault.\\n\\nLet me trace through the decode logic: for instruction 0x1e2e1000, the size field extracts to 0, which maps to 16-bit elements. The opcode is 0x04, and checking the opcode & 1 bit to determine whether it's FMLA or FMLS gives me 0, so it should be taking the FMLS branch.\\n\\nNow I'm verifying the register indices and Q bit—vd, vn, and vm all extract correctly, and since q=0, it should instantiate FmlsDX with uint16_t. The decoded instruction looks right, but there's still something off. Let me double-check the opcode extraction logic in the decoder to make sure I'm reading bits 14-10 correctly. I need to reconsider the bit numbering here. The formula `bits(machInst, 14, 10)` is extracting bits from position 14 down to 10 inclusive, which gives me 5 bits total. For the instruction value 0x1e2e1000, I should verify what those specific bits actually contain rather than assuming the calculation is wrong. I'm realizing the bit extraction logic is correct—bits 14 through 10 do yield 0x04—but the actual ARM encoding specifies a 5-bit opcode field, so I need to reconsider whether I'm reading the right bits from the instruction encoding.\\n\\nLet me trace through what the NEON scalar three-same pattern matcher expects for FMLS, since that's what the generated decoder is using instead of the scalar floating-point path I was focusing on.\\n\\nLooking at the pattern for `isAdvSIMDSc3Same`, it specifies that bit 29 must be 1 and bits 11:10 must be 00. For the FMLS instruction 0x1e2e1000, I'm verifying the binary representation to see if it matches these constraints.\\n\\nChecking each bit position against the pattern requirements, FMLS satisfies all the conditions for `isAdvSIMDSc3Same`, which means it's being routed to the wrong decoder function. The scalar FMLS should go through `decodeFpMulMadd` instead. Now I need to check what `isFpDataProc2Source` expects—it has wildcards in the critical positions where FMLS differs, so FMLS would match that pattern too and get decoded through the wrong path.\\n\\nLet me trace through what `decodeFpDataProc2Source` actually does with FMLS to understand why it's returning an unknown instruction.\",\"signature\":\"2714f0a801541a192e1e719ea87a01eef6e2843c4ca5fe38a355689627404260\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1211,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":106286},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"bcd2be1a-fca7-40a7-b39a-18a3870ba0ed\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:30:09.834Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"bcd2be1a-fca7-40a7-b39a-18a3870ba0ed\",\"isSidech