ult\",\"content\":\"55:    typedef uint32_t MachInst;\\n57:    typedef uint16_t vmid_t;\\n59:    BitUnion64(ExtMachInst)\\n203:    EndBitUnion(ExtMachInst)\\n234:    typedef int RegContextParam;\\n235:    typedef int RegContextVal;\\n393:     * Instruction decoder fault codes in ExtMachInst.\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"uuid\":\"5f7b36de-4109-4391-a224-a20fa134a30b\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:35:56.432Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"b6e9c729-9a4f-4fdf-b311-ad609d8ec547\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"5f7b36de-4109-4391-a224-a20fa134a30b\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d252ec136d48c4b2b0c4a3507ea0a\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"`MachInst = uint32_t`! So `MachInst` is a 32-bit unsigned integer, NOT a 64-bit. But the disassembly showed `ubfx x24, x0, #30, #8` extracting bits 37:30 from a 64-bit register...This doesn't make sense if the compiler knows `MachInst` is 32-bit.Let me reconsider. The `bitPatternMatcher` takes `MachInst` (uint32_t) as a parameter. The template computes:- `mask = 0xf9c00000` (a 32-bit value)- `match = 0x18000000` (a 32-bit value)When these 32-bit values are stored and used in the generated lambda, they're implicitly converted to whatever type the function uses. \\n\\nIf the lambda parameter `T` is `MachInst` (uint32_t), then the mask and match would be zero-extended to 64 bits. But in the actual runtime check, the instruction gets sign-extended before being masked, which could explain the discrepancy between the expected and actual behavior. The mask `0xf9c00000` should only set bits in the lower 32 bits, so bit 30 would always be 0 after masking regardless of sign extension.\\n\\nThe real issue might be that `MachInst` is actually a bitfield-backed type rather than a plain `uint32_t`, which would mean bit extraction operations behave differently than expected.\",\"signature\":\"d1d5f5ea544f42a5b7d4f5986ee50d2a58d49bffdb765a5e65614b3779cf29ae\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":208,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":121201},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"09a18bc7-1779-47ed-86bf-d653af3de69f\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:36:07.812Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"09a18bc7-1779-47ed-86bf-d653af3de69f\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d252ec136d48c4b2b0c4a3507ea0a\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"`MachInst = uint32_t`! But the disassembly showed `ubfx x24, x0, #30, #8` (bits 37:30). Let me check if `ExtMachInst` is different:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":208,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":121201},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"55b6af34-e9f1-418e-a63f-179e44c08e7a\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:36:09.551Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"55b6af34-e9f1-418e-a63f-179e44c08e7a\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d252ec136d48c4b2b0c4a3507ea0a\",\"type\":\"message\"