 what mask value is actually being used in the comparison instruction. I'm tracing through the immediate values being loaded into the registers: `w10` gets `0xff208c00` and `w11` gets `0x4e28800`, which combine to create the mask `0xff3fffff`. But when I check what the actual instruction bits are and apply this mask, I'm getting a mismatch with the expected value `0x1e008000`, so something's off with my calculation.\\n\\nLooking more carefully at the bit ranges, I realize the mask might not be covering the full 22-bit instruction encoding correctly—the bits 31-10 should be laid out differently than I initially thought. I'm shifting my approach and looking at the actual disassembly from the compiled `isFpMulMadd` check to understand how the bit masking and matching actually works in the binary, since the mask extraction doesn't align with my manual calculations. Looking at the bit ranges for the pattern, I'm mapping out how each character in the mask corresponds to specific instruction bits—from the upper opcode bits down through the mode and operation encoding, with each grouping representing different aspects of the ARM instruction format. I'm working through the bit pattern extraction for the mask and match values, checking which bits need to be set based on the character assignments, then verifying the calculation against the binary representation to ensure the arithmetic is correct. I'm looking at how the mask and match values are actually used in the disassembly—the immediate value loaded into `w10` gets shifted left by 16 bits, which explains why the final value differs from what I initially calculated. I'm verifying the bit positions in the instruction encoding—the upper byte should be `0x1e` for bits 31-28, which confirms that bits 31-24 are correct. The lower byte `0x80` covers bits 15-8, so my mask of `0xff3fff80` is properly isolating those bit ranges.\\n\\nBut I'm realizing the instruction format in `ExtMachInst` might not be straightforward—the `Bitfield<31,28>` definitions could be creating a union with the `uint32_t instBits` field, which would mean the upper bits are being interpreted differently than I thought.\\n\\nLooking at how the bitfields are actually laid out, there's a gap from bits 31-0 where only certain bitfields are explicitly defined, while `instBits` takes up the full 32-bit range as a raw field. When I extract a specific bitfield like `Bitfield<31,28>`, the bitfield backend should just return the correct bits from `instBits` without shifting, so `emi.encoding` at bits 31-28 would give me `0x1e` directly from the instruction `0x1e2e1000`. Let me verify this is actually how the bitfield extraction works in the gem5 code.\",\"signature\":\"54927974c0567e346131034258c47abc15ec93f64b3a0b38d505c3355ee6736f\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":245,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":126840},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"d8f895bb-636f-4d53-8a7f-421df3c140fe\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:39:23.055Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"d8f895bb-636f-4d53-8a7f-421df3c140fe\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d25e0af8d3e1e8c565c5974911435\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me verify how gem5's BitField actually extracts bits from the underlying storage:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":245,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":126840},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"4adc946b-6fbd-4ac0-b5aa-13