ry might have been built from a PREVIOUS build that didn't include the debug print. Let me check when the binary was last modified vs the source files.\\n\\ngem5.opt: Apr 2 12:47\\ndecoder-ns.cc.inc: Apr 2 12:47\\ninst-constrs-3.o: Apr 2 12:47\\n\\nAll the same time. But the string \\\"DEBUG decodeFpAdvSIMD\\\" is in the binary (from `strings`). \\n\\nThe binary definitely contains that debug string, so the function must be present somewhere. The fact that it's not being called suggests the problem might be upstream in how `decodeFpAdvSIMD` gets invoked - maybe through a decode cache or a different code path that I haven't traced yet. Let me check if there's a caching mechanism interfering with the call. `decodeInst` uses a cache keyed by the full `ExtMachInst`. But `ExtMachInst` is 64-bit, and the instruction 0x1e2e1000 only fills bits 31:0. The upper bits (63:32) might contain decoder state, making the cache key different every time.\\n\\nBUT - `decodeFpAdvSIMD` is NOT being called at all. The debug print would appear if it WAS being called. So the cache is returning a cached unknown instruction BEFORE calling `decodeFpAdvSIMD`.\\n\\nActually, I think I see it now. The decode cache might be returning a cached `Unknown64` instruction for this particular `ExtMachInst` value, which means `decodeFpAdvSIMD` never gets invoked in the first place. If the cache key is based on the full 64-bit `ExtMachInst`, then the upper bits would change between runs, so the cache should miss on the first call. That would mean `decodeInst` should invoke the decode chain and eventually call `decodeFpAdvSIMD`, which would print the debug message. Since I'm not seeing that message, either the cache is hitting unexpectedly or `decodeFpAdvSIMD` isn't being reached at all. Rather than keep digging into the cache logic, I should just check what instruction is actually being returned by adding debug output in the base `decodeInst` function to see what gets returned.\\n\\nActually, let me reconsider where the unknown instruction error is coming from. I initially thought it was a decode-time panic, but the fault occurs at execution time. If the decode had failed, the instruction wouldn't execute at all. The panic is happening in the fault invoke function, which means an instruction was successfully decoded but then faulted when it tried to execute. So the decode is working—`decodeFpMulMadd` is being called and returning `FmlsDX`—but the `Unknown64` instruction is being created somewhere in that function. The issue must be in the `decodeFpMulMadd` logic itself, not in whether the decode path is being reached.\\n\\nWait, but the debug output shows that `decodeFpAdvSIMD` is never being called at all, which means either the instruction isn't reaching the FP/AdvSIMD decode path or there's something wrong with how the instruction is being fetched in the first place.\",\"signature\":\"24a4245b4f1de2243da1ddd8c0c43f5633895a0d62e8196a53550e0141d8fd11\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":47,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":143030},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"b7a15906-f8ad-4b76-988d-59aac788e655\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:49:49.143Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"b7a15906-f8ad-4b76-988d-59aac788e655\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d2856c741a9f0ae3938f833e6c7cd\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me check what the AtomicSimpleCPU does when fetching and decoding:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tok