stX(\\\"fmls\\\", \\\"FmlsQX\\\", \\\"SimdFloatMultAccOp\\\", floatTypes, 4,\\n                   fmlsCode, True)\\n\\n\\n```\\n\\nThese DON'T have `extra_check=fhm_check`! They use the same `fmlsCode` (which calls `fplibMulAdd`) but WITHOUT the FHM extension check. This means `FmlsDX` and `FmlsQX` will attempt to use `fplibMulAdd<uint16_t>` for half-precision floats without verifying the CPU actually supports the FHM extension, which could cause undefined behavior or incorrect results.\\n\\nLet me trace through the instruction variants more carefully. Looking at the line numbers and what each instruction should be checking, I need to distinguish between the element-wise variants (FmlsElemDX, FmlsElemQX) which should have the extension check, versus the standard variants (FmlsDX, FmlsQX) which might not need it depending on the architecture version.\\n\\n```\\n\\npython\\nthreeEqualRegInstX(\\\"fmls\\\", \\\"FmlsElemDX\\\", \\\"SimdFloatMultAccOp\\\",\\n                   smallFloatTypes, 2, fmlsCode, True, byElem=True)\\nthreeEqualRegInstX(\\\"fmls\\\", \\\"FmlsElemQX\\\", \\\"SimdFloatMultAccOp\\\", floatTypes,\\n                   4, fmlsCode, True, byElem=True)\\nthreeEqualRegInstX(\\\"fmls\\\", \\\"FmlsElemScX\\\", \\\"SimdFloatMultAccOp\\\", floatTypes,\\n                   4, fmlsCode, True, byElem=True, scalar=True,\\n                   nepSrc=\\\"vd\\\")\\n# FMLS (vector)\\nthreeEqualRegInstX(\\\"fmls\\\", \\\"FmlsDX\\\", \\\"SimdFloatMultAccOp\\\", smallFloatTypes,\\n                   2, fmlsCode, True)\\nthreeEqualRegInstX(\\\"fmls\\\", \\\"FmlsQX\\\", \\\"SimdFloatMultAccOp\\\", floatTypes, 4,\\n                   fmlsCode, True)\\n```\\n\\nI see the pattern now—the `_ScX` variants are scalar operations with different element counts and a special `nepSrc` parameter, while the `_DX` and `_QX` variants handle the vector cases with their respective element sizes. I'm realizing the line numbers might not align with what I'm seeing in the code—the grep outpu