ser\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_ocbc9x50ze0h_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"770\\tUndefinedInstruction::invoke(ThreadContext *tc, const StaticInstPtr &inst)\\n771\\t{\\n772\\t    if (FullSystem) {\\n773\\t        ArmFault::invoke(tc, inst);\\n774\\t        return;\\n775\\t    }\\n776\\t\\n777\\t    // If the mnemonic isn't defined this has to be an unknown instruction.\\n778\\t    assert(unknown || mnemonic != NULL);\\n779\\t    auto arm_inst = static_cast<ArmStaticInst *>(inst.get());\\n780\\t    if (disabled) {\\n781\\t        panic(\\\"Attempted to execute disabled instruction \\\"\\n782\\t                \\\"'%s' (inst 0x%08x)\\\", mnemonic, arm_inst->encoding());\\n783\\t    } else if (unknown) {\\n784\\t        panic(\\\"Attempted to execute unknown instruction (inst 0x%08x)\\\",\\n785\\t              arm_inst->encoding());\\n786\\t    } else {\\n787\\t        panic(\\\"Attempted to execute unimplemented instruction \\\"\\n788\\t                \\\"'%s' (inst 0x%08x)\\\", mnemonic, arm_inst->encoding());\\n789\\t    }\\n790\\t}\\n791\\t\\n792\\tbool\\n793\\tUndefinedInstruction::routeToHyp(ThreadContext *tc) const\\n794\\t{\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"7e630fa4-0998-4e9b-91a2-ead9baff4478\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:31:17.424Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"85813f39-cafa-4fed-a0cc-1fff6d355cd9\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"7e630fa4-0998-4e9b-91a2-ead9baff4478\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d24159293f5647850063e9c8d5c66\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"OK so the panic message \\\"Attempted to execute unknown instruction (inst 0x%08x)\\\" comes from the `UndefinedInstruction` exception handler, specifically when `unknown=true`. This means that the decode DOES return a `StaticInstPtr`, but it's marked as \\\"unknown\\\".\\n\\nLet me trace back: `Unknown64` is the class that gets `unknown=true`. It's created when the decoder can't match any pattern. The decode path is:1. `decodeInst(machInst)` in `decode-method.cc.inc`\\n2. Routes to `decodeFpAdvSIMD<GenericDecoder>(machInst)`3. `decodeFpAdvSIMD` checks patterns in order...\\n\\n\\n4. At some point, it falls through and returns `new Unknown64(machInst)`, so the issue is that one of the patterns I'm checking is matching when it shouldn't be, causing the decode to skip the correct handler and fall through to Unknown64 instead. Let me carefully check what `isFpDataProc2Source` actually matches for this instruction encoding. So FMLS is definitely matching `isFpDataProc2Source` and routing to `decodeFpDataProc2Source`, which then returns an `Unknown64` instance based on the opcode mismatch. The problem is that `isFpMulMadd` is checking its conditions in the wrong order—it's evaluating after `isFpDataProc2Source` when it should be catching the case first.\\n\\nActually, wait. Looking at the generated code, `isFpMulMadd` is being checked before `isFpDataProc2Source`, so it should fire first. Let me trace through why it's not matching FMLS instead.\\n\\nGoing through the bit constraints: bits 31-28 are 0001 (passes), bits 27-25 are 111 (passes), bit 24 is 0 (passes with 'X'), bit 23 is 0 (passes with 'X'), bit 22 is 0 (passes with 'X'), and bit 21 is 1 (passes with 'X'). Everything checks out, so `isFpMulMadd` should be matching and returning `decodeFpMulMadd`.\\n\\nBut the error message suggests an `UndefinedInstruction` fault is being triggered instead. I'm wondering if the issue isn't with the decode path itself, but rather with how the instruction execution is being handled afterward. The `UndefinedInstruction::invoke` function in the faults module seems to be involved in the error handling, so maybe the problem lies in the execution phase rather than the initial decode.\\n\\nLooking at the error message more carefully—it's panicking about an \\\"unknown instruction\\\" rather than an \\\"unimplemented instruction\\\"—which means the decode is returning an `Unknown64` object. So either `isFpMulMadd` isn't matching correctly, or something else is returning `Unknown64` before we even get to execute. I should add some debug output to trace which pattern is actually being matched and where the `Unknown64` is coming from. I'm tracing through the bit pattern matching logic for the FMLS instruction, checking each bit position against the expected values in the pattern matcher. Continuing through the remaining bit positions, most are don't-care values that pass automatically, with bit 23 failing the check since the pattern expects 1 but the instruction has 0. I'm realizing the actual mask only constrains the specified bits—bits 31, 30, 29, 28, 27, and 24—while the other positions like 23 and 22 aren't actually checked, so I need to recalculate what the match value should be based on this corrected understanding. I need to double-check which bits are actually specified in the pattern—the earlier analysis showed bit 24 as specified (expecting 1), but I should verify this is correct before moving forward with the bit matching logic. Continuing through the bit analysis, I'm verifying each specified position: bit 28 matches correctly at 1, bit 27 matches at 1, and now checking bit 24 which should be 0 but the instruction has 1, causing a mismatch that breaks the entire check.\\n\\nWait, I'm second-guessing myself—let me recount the character positions more carefully since my earlier trace was also showing a mismatch on bit 24, and I need to verify I'm reading the bit positions correctly. I keep making mistakes counting bit positions in my head, so let me write a Python script to verify this once and for all.\",\"signature\":\"280681151062e73d997a0be88b7bdf52a46f710dbe8f77865c639f10ea9b6069\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":255,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":110060},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"20817eaf-c1d5-46c5-85a0-3d0c923dbe8d\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:31:43.620Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"20817eaf-c1d5-46c5-85a0-3d0c923dbe8d\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d24159293f5647850063e9c8d5c66\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me write a definitive Python verification:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":255,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":110060},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"edb10037-c65d-4fcf-9331-4b4565cae70a\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:31:43.620Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"edb10037-c65d-4fcf-9331-4b4565cae70a\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d24159293f5647850063e9c8d5c66\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_function_3p5gwv9vweud_1\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"python3 << 'PYEOF'\\n# VERIFY gem5 bitPatternMatcher for isFpMulMadd\\n\\n# Pattern from current file (22 chars):\\n# '0','0','0','1','1','X','X','0','0','0','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X'\\n# char[0]->bit31, char[1]->bit30, ..., char[21]->bit10\\n\\n# CORRECT gem5 logic: recursion from msb to lsb\\ndef gem5_match(msb, lsb, chars):\\n    if msb == lsb:\\n        c = chars[0]\\n        return (1 << msb) if c == '1' else 0\\n    c = chars[0]\\n    first = (1 << msb) if c == '1' else 0\\n    return first | gem5_match(msb - 1, lsb, chars[1:])\\n\\ndef gem5_mask(msb, lsb, chars):\\n    if msb == lsb:\\n        c = chars[0]\\n        return (1 << msb) if c not in ('X','x') else 0\\n    c = chars[0]\\n    first = (1 << msb) if c not in ('X','x') else 0\\n    return first | gem5_mask(msb - 1, lsb, chars[1:])\\n\\nchars = ['0','0','0','1','1','X','X','0','0','0','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X']\\nmask = gem5_mask(31, 10, chars)\\nmatch = gem5_match(31, 10, chars)\\n\\nprint(f\\\"Pattern: {''.join(chars)}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"mask   : 0x{mask:08x}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"match  : 0x{match:08x}\\\")\\n\\n# Show which bits are constrained\\nprint(\\\"\\\\nConstrained bits:\\\")\\nfor i, c in enumerate(chars):\\n    if c != 'X':\\n        pos = 31 - i\\n        print(f\\\"  char[{i:2d}] -> bit {pos:2d}: {c}\\\")\\n\\n# Test\\nfmls = 0x1e2e1000\\nresult = (fmls & mask) == match\\nprint(f\\\"\\\\nFMLS 0x{fmls:08x}: {'MATCHES' if result else 'NO MATCH'}\\\")\\nif not result:\\n    print(f\\\"  fmls & mask = 0x{fmls & mask:08x}\\\")\\n    print(f\\\"  match       = 0x{match:08x}\\\")\\n    for i, c in enumerate(chars):\\n        if c != 'X':\\n            pos = 31 - i\\n            inst_bit = (fmls >> pos) & 1\\n            expected = 1 if c == '1' else 0\\n            if inst_bit != expected:\\n                print(f\\\"  MISMATCH at bit {pos}: inst={inst_bit}, pattern={c}\\\")\\nPYEOF\"}}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":\"tool_use\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":255,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":110060,\"output_tokens\":1613,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"b5034322-6bd3-4c3d-b9b0-0755b442a6fd\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:31:54.577Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"b5034322-6bd3-4c3d-b9b0-0755b442a6fd\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_3p5gwv9vweud_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"Pattern: 00011XX000XXXXXXXXXXXX\\nmask   : 0xf9c00000\\nmatch  : 0x18000000\\n\\nConstrained bits:\\n  char[ 0] -> bit 31: 0\\n  char[ 1] -> bit 30: 0\\n  char[ 2] -> bit 29: 0\\n  char[ 3] -> bit 28: 1\\n  char[ 4] -> bit 27: 1\\n  char[ 7] -> bit 24: 0\\n  char[ 8] -> bit 23: 0\\n  char[ 9] -> bit 22: 0\\n\\nFMLS 0x1e2e1000: MATCHES\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"uuid\":\"6e719a9f-9a08-43d4-a7a0-0dbc3153c144\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:31:54.709Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"b5034322-6bd3-4c3d-b9b0-0755b442a6fd\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",