f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"45a5e1ac-8625-4b6f-929d-317d1446da5b\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d254eed50fae56d2f4091af0552e8\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"`BitUnion64(ExtMachInst)` means ExtMachInst is a 64-bit type. But `MachInst` is `uint32_t`. And the `bitPatternMatcher` uses `MachInst`. Let me check if `ExtMachInst` is the same as `MachInst` for pattern matching purposes, or if there's a conversion.\\n\\nLooking at the `isFpMulMadd` pattern:\\n```cpp\\ninline constexpr auto isFpMulMadd = bitPatternMatcher<\\n    MachInst,\\n    31, 10,\\n    '0','0','0','1','1','X','X','0','0','0','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X'>();\\n```\\n\\nThe template parameter is `MachInst` (uint32_t). But `ExtMachInst` is 64-bit. When calling `isFpMulMadd(machInst)`, the `machInst` is an `ExtMachInst`. Does the pattern matcher handle this by implicitly converting to `MachInst`? Or does it use the full `ExtMachInst`?\\n\\nLooking at the `bitPatternMatcher` template:\\n```cpp\\ntemplate<typename T, int msb, int lsb, char curr, char... rest>\\nconstexpr auto bitPatternMatcher() {\\n```\\n\\nWhere `T = MachInst` (uint32_t). The lambda is `auto ret = [](T value) constexpr { return (value & mask) == match; }`. When `value` is `ExtMachInst` (64-bit), does it implicitly convert to `MachInst` (uint32_t)?\\n\\nYES! In C++, when you pass a larger integer type to a function/template expecting a smaller integer type, the value is implicitly converted. `ExtMachInst` (64-bit) → `MachInst` (uint32_t) would take the lower 32 bits.\\n\\n\\nWhen `ExtMachInst` (64-bit) is implicitly converted to `MachInst` (uint32_t), only the lower 32 bits are kept. For FMLS `0x1e2e1000`, the instruction is stored in the lower 32 bits of `ExtMachInst`, so this conversion should work correctly. But wait—if the instruction encoding is in the upper 32 bits, then the lower bits might be metadata or other fields, which would explain why the conversion is losing the actual instruction data.\\n\\nLet me trace through how `decodeInst` actually receives the `ExtMachInst` value to see where the instruction bits are positioned.\",\"signature\":\"4db98f8931a925e002933e20604d82f10007f261bfaf2f8203957e256b60674a\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":178,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":122159},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"1fa42391-e423-4ea2-a69e-a5e6520194fe\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:36:45.598Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"1fa42391-e423-4ea2-a69e-a5e6520194fe\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d254eed50fae56d2f4091af0552e8\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"`ExtMachInst` is 64-bit! The instruction encoding is in the **upper 32 bits**. Let me verify:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":178,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":122159},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"052834c3-c91e-4b62-987f-d834711382a4\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:36:46.960Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"052834c3-c91e-4b62-987f-d834711382a4\",\"isSidecha