rn match: {result}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"  fmls & mask = 0x{fmls & mask:08x}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"  match        = 0x{match:08x}\\\")\\nPYEOF\"}}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":\"tool_use\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1288,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":123102,\"output_tokens\":1714,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"b683565a-11ed-48fb-a592-12c4faa50297\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:37:44.282Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"b683565a-11ed-48fb-a592-12c4faa50297\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_8sxzg3w5vp3l_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"FMLS 0x1e2e1000\\n  bit 31: 0\\n  bit 30: 0\\n  bit 29: 0\\n  bit 28: 1\\n  bit 27: 1\\n  bit 26: 1\\n  bit 25: 1\\n  bit 24: 0\\n  bit 23: 0\\n  bit 22: 0\\n\\nPattern char[8] -> bit 23: expects '0'\\nFMLS bit23: 0\\nMatch: True\\n\\nFull pattern match: True\\n  fmls & mask = 0x18000000\\n  match        = 0x18000000\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"uuid\":\"b4ce85d4-c265-4985-bc8c-4b987dd4b266\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:37:44.436Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"b683565a-11ed-48fb-a592-12c4faa50297\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"b4ce85d4-c265-4985-bc8c-4b987dd4b266\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d25988de71a5ac89461ee7471ed68\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"OK so the pattern DOES match. My earlier analysis was correct: bit23 of FMLS is 0, and the pattern has '0' at char[8] which maps to bit23. And bit24 of FMLS is 0, and the pattern has '0' at char[7] which maps to bit24.So the pattern is correct and should match FMLS. But the test is still failing.This is very confusing. Let me take a completely different approach and just add debug output to the generated decoder to see what's actually happening.Actually, let me try yet another approach. Instead of trying to understand why the pattern doesn't work, let me add `isFpMulMadd` to the `decodeFpDataProc2Source` function. Since that function IS being called and handles switchVal=0x01 (FMLS), I can add a check there to catch FMLS before it falls through to FDIV.Actually wait, I can't modify `decodeFpDataProc2Source` in the .isa file because it would affect scalar FP data processing instructions. But I CAN add a guard in `isFpDataProc2Source` to EXCLUDE FMLS/FMLA patterns, so that they fall through to `isFpMulMadd`.Let me look at what `isFpDataProc2Source` checks:```cpp\\n\\n\\nisFpDataProc2Source(machInst) = check bits 31, 30, 29, 28, 27, 26, 25 against specific values, with wildcard bits at 23:10. For FMLS at 0x1e2e1000, the check needs to verify bit31=0, bit30=0, bit29=0, bit28=1, bit27=1, bit26=1, and bit25=1.\\n\\nSince the pattern shows all 'X' for those wildcard bits, FMLS passes the check and gets routed to `decodeFpDataProc2Source`. To fix this, I could modify `isFpDataProc2Source` to specifically exclude FMLS and FMLA by checking that bit23 is 0 or that bits21:20 don't match the FMLS/FMLA pattern, but I need to verify this won't break other instructions. I'm seeing that bit23 being 0 is what separates FMLS from other instructions in this space—if I add a guard checking bit23==1, it would exclude FMLS while keeping FADD, FDIV, and FMUL intact. I should verify this won't accidentally block other valid scalar FP instructions that legitimately use the same encoding. I'm verifying the instruction encoding pattern holds across FMLS, FMLA, FMADD, and FMSUB — they all have bit23=1 while scalar FP instructions like FADD, FDIV, FMUL, FMAX, FMIN, FMAXNM, and FMINNM all have bit23=0, so this single bit can reliably distinguish between them.\\n\\nBut wait, I need to reconsider the bit numbering — in ARM64 ISA documentation, bits are numbered from MSB to LSB as 31 down to 0, so bit 23 refers to the 24th bit from the left in the 32-bit instruction encoding, which is the 8th bit of byte 2. I'm realizing the bit numbering is consistent across both definitions—the 24th bit (0-indexed) is indeed bit 23, which is always 1 in FMLS, and this same bit pattern holds across all the FMLS variants I'm checking.\\n\\nNow I'm verifying that FMLA, FMADD, FMSUB, FNMADD, and FNMSUB all follow the same pattern with bit23=1, while FADD, FDIV, and FMUL all have bit23=0, so I can confidently use bit23 as a distinguishing feature.\\n\\nThe approach is to add a guard to `isFpDataProc2Source` that requires bit23=0, which will exclude all the multiply-add/subtract variants and route them correctly to `isFpMulMadd` instead. I'm writing the guard condition to check this bit.\\n\\nChecking if this creates any conflicts with NEON instructions that use the same encoding space... Looking at the FP data processing instruction encodings, I'm checking whether adding bit23=1 as a requirement for `isFpMulMadd` would inadvertently match other instruction types like the scalar FP variants of FMLS and FMLA.\\n\\nIt turns out scalar FP instructions already have bit15 set to 0, which is part of the pattern check, and they can't be confused with the AdvSIMD versions since they use different register types. So the approach should work: set `bit23=1` in `isFpMulMadd` to distinguish FMLS and FMLA from scalar FP instructions, while `isFpDataProc2Source` already correctly routes to its decode function since it doesn't check bit23.\\n\\nBut I need to reconsider the actual requirement here—`isFpMulMadd` should match FMLS and FMLA instructions, and the constraint is that `isFpDataProc2Source` must not match these same instructions. The current `isFpDataProc2Source` pattern doesn't have a guard on bit23, so I need to figure out what's preventing FMLS and FMLA from matching it currently. Looking at the mask calculation, when I OR together all those bit extractions, I get 0xF9C00000, which confirms the mask is correct. Now I'm trying to trace through the actual value being checked against this mask.\\n\\nWait, I think I found the issue—the instruction encoding might be in the upper 32 bits of ExtMachInst rather than the lower bits. Let me check how the decoder actually reads and stores the instruction.\",\"signature\":\"77ca246b89bedbc8436905fe4fa36a90d80872efd1250c4f4cc1c1999ed5c603\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":828,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":124410},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"340de482-2af6-4d1f-9152-ff12f12aa76b\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:38:16.802Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"340de482-2af6-4d1f-9152-ff12f12aa76b\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d25988de71a5ac89461ee7471ed68\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me check how `