 to uint32_t and ItE to uint16_t\\n\\nLooking at the symbol names more carefully: `FmlsDXIjE` decodes to the uint32_t variant, while `FmlsDXItE` is the uint16_t one - the 'I' indicates an integer type, with 'j' being unsigned int and 't' being unsigned short. I'm realizing the compiler backtrace shows `UndefinedInstruction::invoke` calling `ArmStaticInst::invoke`, which then checks a disabled flag or unknown instruction flag. The issue is that `new FmlsDX<uint16_t>` is being created in `decodeFpMulMadd`, but this instruction object must have `unknown=true` or `disabled=true` set, triggering the panic even though the execute method exists in the binary. The panic message indicates the unknown flag is set, but `FmlsDX` shouldn't have that since it's a real instruction. Let me trace through the actual execution path: if `isFpMulMadd` matches and `decodeFpMulMadd` creates the instruction, then `execute()` gets called and should pass the `checkFPAdvSIMDEnabled64()` check in AtomicSimpleCPU, but something in that chain is setting the unknown flag or bypass flag instead.\\n\\nLooking at the backtrace more carefully, the crash happens in the undefined instruction handler at tick 0, which means the instruction was decoded but marked as unimplemented or unknown. If the decode were working correctly and returning a valid instruction, we'd see the fault from `checkFPAdvSIMDEnabled64()` inside `execute()` instead, or an actual arithmetic panic. Since we're seeing the undefined instruction panic, the instruction object itself must have been created with the wrong type or an uninitialized flag.\\n\\nNow I'm realizing the real issue: if `decodeFpMulMadd` were being called and working correctly, it would either succeed or hit the FHM check for half-precision variants. But we're crashing immediately at tick 0 with an undefined instruction panic, which suggests the instruction was decoded but immediately flagged as unknown. This points to `isFpMulMadd` not actually matching in the binary, even though our Python simulation says it should—either the binary wasn't rebuilt with our changes, or there's something else filtering it out before `isFpMulMadd` gets checked.\\n\\nLet me trace through the full decode path in `decodeFpAdvSIMD` to see if any of the earlier pattern checks are catching the instruction instead. I'm working through the bit patterns for the remaining functions to verify whether any of them match the FMLS instruction encoding, systematically checking each predicate against the binary representation. Continuing through the remaining bit patterns: the immediate encoding requires bit 25 to be 1, which doesn't match our instruction, so that's ruled out. The conditional compare and second source operand patterns also fail their bit checks. This means the FMLS instruction would only match the multiply-add predicate, routing it to the decode function for further processing.\\n\\nThe decode function extracts the vector length, element size, and opcode fields from the instruction encoding. Now I need to check what `switch (size)` does with a size value of 0, since that's what the decoder would pass.\\n\\nLooking at the switch statement, when size equals 0 (16-bit elements), the code checks the opcode's least significant bit to determine whether to execute FMLA or FMLS. Since the opcode is 0x04, the LSB is 0, so it routes to the FMLS path. With Q=0 (scalar mode), it instantiates a new FmlsDX instruction with uint16_t elements.\\n\\nThis should work correctly, so there must be something else causing the mismatch. I'm wondering if the ISA parser is generating code with a slightly different mask or match value than what the C++ template produces—maybe an off-by-one error in how it's interpreting the template parameters or character array.\\n\\nLet me check the generated decoder-ns.cc.inc file to see what the actual compiled values are. If the mask is something like 0x18 instead of 0x18000000, the matcher would accept any instruction with those top bits set. I can use a simple test instruction like 0x00000000 to verify whether the pattern is actually matching correctly.\",\"signature\":\"96171eb90135adddc7327513d9ae08f7f8cb0238680ba850d662ba9d16ac8623\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":239,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":111592},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"de203655-81f1-43ea-b695-71b5085e4a9c\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:33:04.824Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"de203655-81f1-43ea-b695-71b5085e4a9c\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d24653dbb6f82c5e0f4a94b6435ff\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me check what `isFpMulMadd` is producing in the actual generated C++ code. Let me add a test instruction that should NEVER match:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":239,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":111592},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"28e83575-dd04-40dd-a6f9-ed9685ac1d9e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:33:05.948Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"28e83575-dd04-40dd-a6f9-ed9685ac1d9e\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d24653dbb6f82c5e0f4a94b6435ff\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\