sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"8096d024-12c7-4243-821d-64a3056a6673\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"7deaf8ed-cc1c-41c0-bfde-51c981e7e320\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d2988f1ad155e13debd1118de7b40\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"`aarch64` is `Bitfield<34>` which accesses bit 34 of the 64-bit ExtMachInst. Since `emi` is 0x00000000_1e2e1000 (upper=0, lower=0x1e2e1000):\\n- `emi.aarch64` reads bit 34 of `emi` = bit 34 of 0x00000000_1e2e1000 = **0** (the upper 32 bits are 0, so bit 34 of the whole 64-bit value is in the upper dword).\\n\\nSo `emi.aarch64 = 0`, which means `AARCH64=0` in the switch statement!\\n \\nBut wait, that means `case 0x1` wouldn't match at all—I'd actually fall through to the default case that returns a new `Unknown(machInst)`. Let me trace through the switch statement more carefully to see where the panic is actually coming from. I'm tracing through the switch statement on the `AARCH64` bit field—when it's 0, execution jumps to the default case that returns a new `Unknown(machInst)`, but when it's 1, it should fall through to case 0x1. Let me verify that the switch is actually reading from `machInst.aarch64` correctly, since the macro definition shows `AARCH64` maps to `machInst.aarch64`.\\n\\nThe issue is becoming clearer: if the panic message shows \\\"unknown instruction\\\" with the lower 32 bits of the ExtMachInst value, but the actual ExtMachInst being decoded is just those lower bits with zeros above, then the `aarch64` field would be 0, routing to the default case instead of the AARCH64 case. Yet the panic message format suggests it came from the UndefinedInstruction handler, which means something else is going wrong here. I'm wondering if `decodeInst` is returning an `Unknown` object that's then being invoked, which would trigger the panic. Let me verify what `machInst.instBits` contains when the 32-bit instruction is stored—specifically whether the value is actually 0x1e2e1000 or if there's something else going on with how the bits are being interpreted. Looking at the panic message format and how `encoding()` extracts the instruction bits, I'm realizing the issue might be that `decodeInst` is returning an `Unknown` object instead of an `UndefinedInstruction` fault, which would explain why we're seeing the \\\"unknown instruction\\\" message rather than a panic about an undefined instruction. Looking at the panic locations in faults.cc, I see that \\\"unknown instruction\\\" and \\\"unimplemented instruction\\\" are both panic messages triggered from different paths in the UndefinedInstruction handler. The \\\"unimplemented\\\" case means the instruction decoded successfully but isn't implemented, while \\\"unknown\\\" means either the mnemonic isn't defined or the `unknown` flag is set—so if the instruction decodes to `Unknown`, that's likely what's triggering the \\\"unknown\\\" panic. I should check whether the `Unknown` class is a subclass of `UndefinedInstruction` and how it gets constructed to understand the full flow.\",\"signature\":\"36b5739fd698df9bf597c75c1a93f007aa05a71eadf6350d713699e0bfa53dc8\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":260,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":148992},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"c8e6b8a9-8f5f-4bfb-945e-f80e5668698a\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:54:52.890Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\