{\\n849\\t                                                              return new EorsRegPclr(machInst, rd,\\n850\\t                                                                                               rn, rm, imm5,\\n851\\t                                                                                               type);\\n852\\t                                                          } else\\n853\\t                                           {\\n854\\t                                                              return new EorRegCc(machInst, rd,\\n855\\t                                                                                            rn, rm, imm5, type);\\n856\\t                                                          }\\n857\\t                                                      } else {\\n858\\t                                                          return new EorReg(machInst, rd, rn,\\n859\\t                                                                                       rm, imm5, type);\\n860\\t                                                      }\\n861\\t                                                  } else {\\n862\\t                                                      if (setCc) {\\n863\\t                                                          return new EorRegRegCc(machInst, rd,\\n864\\t                                                                                            rn, rm, rs, type);\\n865\\t                                                      } else {\\n866\\t                                                          return new EorRegReg(machInst, rd,\\n867\\t                                                                                          rn, rm, rs, type);\\n868\\t                                                      }\\n869\\t                                                  }\\n870\\t                                                  break;\\n871\\t                                          \\n872\\t                                                case 0x2:\\n873\\t                                                  if (immShift) {\\n874\\t                                                      if (setCc) {\\n875\\t                                                          \\n876\\t                                                          if (rd == int_reg::Pc) {\\n877\\t                                                              return new SubsRegPclr(machInst, rd,\\n878\\t                                                                                               rn, rm, imm5,\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"6bc9ac32-f731-4d0c-a6b3-a79d04d78002\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:43:52.952Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"21f9a0c0-fff4-41fb-9d82-2cc0ffe324ca\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"6bc9ac32-f731-4d0c-a6b3-a79d04d78002\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d35194e7904e9a741827f9354ca14\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"This is the `ArmDataProcReg` decode path. It uses OPCODE (bits 24:21). FMLS has OPCODE = bits 24:21 = 0x1 = 1. That matches `case 0x1:` which is EOR-type instructions. None of these would match FMLS.\\n\\nSo FMLS falls through to `default: return new Unknown(machInst)`. This is the AARCH64=0 path.\\n\\nBut this is WRONG. FMLS is an AARCH64 instruction! The `case 0x1:` (AARCH64=1) block at line 2868 exists for this.\\n\\n\\nThe real issue is that `pc.aarch64()` is returning 0 when it should return 1 for AARCH64 instructions. The PC state isn't being set correctly in bare-metal mode. Looking at how the decode function processes instructions, it checks whether the instruction is THUMB and sets the size accordingly, but something in that logic isn't recognizing AARCH64 properly.\\n\\nActually, I think I see the root cause now. The ELF is compiled for AARCH64, but the gem5 decoder is in AARCH32 mode. The THUMB and AARCH64 flags come directly from the CPU's PC state, and if the CPU is in AARCH32 mode, it will decode and execute everything as 32-bit ARM instructions regardless of what the ELF specifies.\\n\\nBut wait—the SEWorkload initialization should set the CPU to AARCH64 mode for ARM64 binaries, so `pc.aarch64()` should return 1. Let me add some debug output to verify what's actually happening there, and also double-check whether COND_CODE is even the right field to look at in AARCH64 mode, since that field might be interpreted differently depending on the execution state. Looking at the decode-method structure, I see that AARCH64=0 and AARCH64=1 are separate decode paths, but the generated code suggests they might be conditionally compiled together. The real issue is that my decoder is hitting the AARCH64=0 path instead of AARCH64=1 because `pc.aarch64()` is returning 0 when it should return 1. I'm wondering if the bare-metal configuration is properly initializing the CPU into AARCH64 mode, or if there's a mismatch between what the binary expects and how gem5 is actually executing it. I'm reconsidering the bit layout for ARM32 instructions—when COND_CODE is 0x1, that represents the EQ (equal) condition, and I need to verify how that maps to the actual instruction encoding in the THUMB mode path. I'm realizing the confusion: the `switch (COND_CODE)` on line 30 is nested within `case 0x0:` of the AARCH64 switch, which means it's specifically for ARM32 decoding. When AARCH64=0, COND_CODE (bits 31:28) determines the condition flags, with case 0xf being unconditional instructions. For my instruction 0x1e2e1000 where COND_CODE=0x1, this should route to the conditional execution path, but I need to understand where the actual decode is failing.\\n\\nLooking at the conditional instruction handler around line 298, it seems like it's checking specific condition combinations, but I'm realizing there might be a structural issue—perhaps this entire nested structure is wrapped inside a conditional that checks AARCH64 first, which would mean these decode paths are only valid under certain conditions. I'm realizing the `switch (THUMB)` is actually at depth 7 in the output, not 8 as I thought earlier. Let me trace through the structure more carefully - the outer switches for DEBUGSTEP, ILLEGALEXEC, and DECODERFAULT are all at depth 4, and then THUMB sits at depth 7, which means there's another nesting level I'm missing in between.\\n\\nLooking at the AARCH64 switch specifically, it's at depth 9, and I need to figure out whether it has its own `case 0x0:` block or if the COND_CODE switch is nested inside the AARCH64 case directly.\\n\\nActually, looking at the indentation levels, `case 0x0:` is indented the same as the AARCH64 switch itself, so they're at the same nesting depth—this means the COND_CODE switch is definitely inside the `case 0x0:` block of the AARCH64 switch.\\n\\nSo the decode path for AARCH64=0 goes through COND_CODE, and for AARCH64=1 it uses the FP decode function instead. My instruction is getting incorrectly decoded as AARCH64=0 when it should be AARCH64=1, so I need to figure out why the AARCH64 bit is being set to 0 in the first place.\\n\\nLet me trace back to where `emi.aarch64` gets set in the moreBytes method—it's pulling from `pc.aarch64()`, which is a CPU state flag. I'm wondering if the bare-metal configuration is properly initializing the CPU into AARCH64 mode for the test binary, or if there's a mismatch in how the PC state is being set up.\",\"signature\":\"0d2f121f9694a0b92cda91fbcdbde67ae7a167643cd1083fd230c734e07e5738\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1204,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":66777},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"1d3457f3-2d49-4461-b704-a2688cb3b267\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:44:29.602Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"1d3457f3-2d49-4461-b704-a2688cb3b267\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d35194e7904e9a741827f9354ca14\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"I see the AARCH64=0 path only has `case 0xf:` (unconditional) in COND_CODE. COND_CODE=0x1 falls to `default:` → returns `Unknown`. But FMLS is AARCH64, so it should take `case 0x1:` (AARCH64=1). The problem is `pc.aarch64()` is returning 0 instead of 1. Let me check the PC state.\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1204,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":66777},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"2f48ed6b-62cc-410f-a659-7d950afb06fe\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:44:32.404Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"2f48ed6b-62cc-410f-a659-7d950afb06fe\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\