chain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d07433392e0b7e7324316e96ac739\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Now I understand the issue. Let me analyze:\\n\\n1. `isFpDataProc1Source` pattern (lines 3694-3701):\\n   - Bits 31:29 = X,0,X (op0)  \\n   - Bits 28:24 = 1,1,1 (fixed)\\n   - Bits 23:22 = 0,X (op1)\\n   - Bits 21:19 = X,1,X (op2)\\n   - Bits 18:10 = X,X,X,X,1,0,0,0,0 (op3)\\n   \\n   This means bit[18] = 1. The pattern covers opcodes 0-31 but requires that bit to be set, so FMOV with opcode 0 (where bit[18]=0) falls outside this pattern and won't match.\\n\\n2. `isFpDataProc` pattern (lines 3748-3755):\\n   - Bits 31:29 = X,0,X (op0)\\n   - Bits 28:24 = 1,1,1 (fixed)\\n   - Bits 23:22 = 1,X (op1)\\n   - Bits 21:10 = X,X,X,X,X,X,X,X,X,X,X,X (op2, op3 all X)\\n   \\n   This requires bit[23] = 1 and bit[21] = 1. Since FMADD/FMSUB have bit[21] = 0, they won't match this pattern either.\\n\\n3. The decode flow checks `isFpDataProc1Source` first (bit[18]=1), then `isFpDataProc` (bit[23]=1), so FMADD won't be caught by either check.\\n\\nThe root issue is that these floating-point multiply-add instructions fall into a gap: FMADD needs bit[18]=0 (excluded from the first pattern) and bit[23]=0 (excluded from the second pattern), so they slip through without matching any decode pattern. Looking at the ARM64 encoding specs more carefully, I'm trying to map out which bits correspond to which instruction categories. The floating-point data processing instructions seem to be scattered across different bit patterns, and I'm realizing my initial breakdown might not be capturing the full picture—I need to reconsider how these instruction types are actually distributed across the encoding space. I'm seeing the rest of the bit field definitions for the remaining instruction categories. Looking at the full picture, each instruction class has a distinct pattern: `isFpDataProc1Source` requires bit 18 to be set, `isFpDataProc` requires bit 23 to be set, and the multiply-add variants have bit 23 cleared with bit 21 also cleared. There's no overlap between these patterns, which means I need to create a dedicated decoder for the multiply-add instructions.\\n\\nNow I'm mapping out the specific instruction encoding for these cases—looking at how the opcode bits (20-15) and register fields (rn, rm, ra) combine to distinguish between FMADD, FMSUB, FNMADD, and FNMSUB variants for both single and double precision formats. I'm working through the bit encoding for the multiply-add and negate variants—the switch value calculation uses bits from the instruction encoding to distinguish between the different floating-point operations, and I need to carefully map out which bits correspond to the opcode versus the register fields. I need to map out the instruction encoding structure more carefully—the opcode bits are at positions 23:21 and 15, but I need to account for the MUL/DOT bit interaction and figure out exactly how the opcode value gets encoded in those fields versus the multiplier bits. Looking at the remaining instruction categories for the Coproc=0x1e case, I can see the structure for FP miscellaneous operations, comparison, immediate, conditional compare, conditional select, and the multiply-add variants that use a 3-register scalar format.\\n\\nNow examining the actual opcode bit assignments for these categories—specifically how bits 23:21 and 15 map to the different floating-point operations, and comparing the bit patterns between FMUL and FMLA to understand the encoding scheme.\\n\\nThe key difference appears to be bit 15, where FMUL has it set to 0 and FMLA has it set to 1, suggesting this bit distinguishes between multiply and multiply-accumulate variants. Looking at the FP Multiply-add category, the opcode values 0-15 likely encode the specific operation types, with FMADD, FMSUB, FNMADD, and FNMSUB being the primary variants. I'm seeing the encoding pattern now — the switch value combines bits from different positions in the instruction encoding, so I need to trace back how this maps to the actual FMADD instruction bits to understand why the switch values are 0x0 through 0x7 for different operand sizes. Looking at the FMLA instruction format, I can see that when the opcode bits are 0010 (decimal 2), it corresponds to FMLA, and since bit 15 is set, the switchVal would be 0x5 (combining the opcode value with the control bit). I'm working through the bit layout more carefully now. Let me extract the opcode value from bits 23, 22, 21, and 15, then compute the switchVal by shifting and combining these bits in the way the code does.\\n\\nLooking at the specific cases for FMADD and FMLA variants, I can see that FMADD uses switchVal values 0x0 through 0x7, while FMLA should fall into the same range since it's also a 3-operand FP operation. The pattern suggests both instruction types share the same switchVal encoding, just with different bit patterns in the opcode field.\\n\\nThe real question is whether the isFpDataProc pattern's requirement for bit[23]=1 is actually correct for FMADD/FMLA, or if those instructions might have bit[23]=0 instead. I should verify this against the actual ARM64 instruction encoding to see if the pattern is too restrictive. Looking at the instruction encoding more carefully, the 32-bit instruction format has bits 31:10 defining the major opcode, with the top 3 bits determining the category. For these floating-point operations, bits 31-29 equal 001, which matches the pattern I'm seeing in the decoder.\\n\\nNow I'm examining how the isFpDataProc pattern works—it looks like the bitPatternMatcher uses specific field positions (op0 through op3) to match against the instruction encoding, with wildcards allowing flexibility in certain bit positions while others must match exactly. Now I'm decoding the specific bit patterns for the FMADD instruction: bits 31-29 are 001, bits 28-24 are fixed at 11111, bits 23-21 are 000 for the multiply-add category, and bit 20 is the Rm register selector. Continuing through the bit field assignments, I'm mapping out how the remaining fields encode the register specifiers and instruction variants—bit 15 serves as the M bit that distinguishes between FMUL and FMLA variants, while the opcode field and register operands fill in the rest of the instruction encoding. Looking at the pattern for `isFpDataProc1Source`, I need bits 15 and 21 to be 0 and 1 respectively, but FMADD has both as 0, so it doesn't match. For `isFpDataProc`, the pattern requires bits 23 and 21 to be 1 and 0, but again FMADD has bit 23 as 0 instead of 1.\\n\\nSo FMADD is falling through all the patterns and hitting the Unknown64 error, which means there's a gap in the decoder. I need to create a new pattern called `isFpMultiplyAdd` that captures this specific bit combination: bits 31-29 as 001, bits 28-24 as 11111, bits 23-21 as 000, and bit 15 as 0.\\n\\nThe other instruction categories I should verify are `isFpDataProc2Source` (FMUL, FMLA, FDIV, FADD, FSUB with bit 15 determining which), and `isFpCondSel` (FCSEL with bit 15 as the condition flag).\\n\\nFor the 4-register FMLA variant that the issue mentions, the encoding shifts to a 128-bit instruction format with M=1 and additional opcode bits, so I need to examine how those parameters get encoded in the instruction structure.\\n\\nNow looking at the scalar floating-point multiply-add instructions more carefully. For the 32-bit variants like FMADD and FMSUB, the opcode field spans bits 23, 22, and 21, with bits 15, 14-10, and 9-5 forming the register specifiers. The 64-bit variants follow a similar pattern but distinguish themselves through the opcode values—FMADD uses opcode=000 while FMSUB uses opcode=001, and they both share the same control bit structure.\\n\\nMoving to FMLA and FMLS, these are the scalar floating-point fused multiply-add instructions that encode differently. They use bits 23 and 21 for the