its 31:10 with op0 being either 00 or 01, op1 always 11, and op1 bits 20:19 being 10 or 11, with op3 as don't-care.\\n\\nLet me trace through what `isFpDataProc1Source` expects: it requires bits 31:29 as X0111, then op0 as either 000 or 011, op1 as 0X, op2 as X10X, and op3 with bit 18 set to 1. For my instruction 0x1e2e1000, that's 00011110 in binary, and checking against these constraints shows the bit positions don't align properly. I'm verifying that all the constraints check out—op0, op1, and op2 all match their required values, and op3's bit 18 is indeed set to 1, confirming this instruction falls into the `isFpDataProc1Source` category.\\n\\nNow I need to trace what happens inside `decodeFpDataProc1Source` for this instruction—it extracts the opcode bits and type, then maps those to specific floating-point operations like FMOV, FABS, FNEG, or FSQRT depending on the combination. Continuing through the remaining opcode cases, I'm seeing FCVT operations that handle type 1 and type 3 conversions, mapping to the appropriate instruction variants like FcvtFpDFpS and FcvtFpHFpS.\\n\\nNow I'm checking the opcode 0xD case with type=0 for FRECPE in single precision, but there's a mismatch in the encoding comments that I need to verify more carefully. I need to map out the bit positions correctly—bits 23-22 represent the type field, bit 21 is part of the opcode, and bits 20-15 make up the opcode extension. So I'm extracting type as bits 23:22 (which gives 0 for this instruction), opcode as bits 20:15 (which gives 0x25), and then combining the opcode with bit 21 to create a 6-bit switch value. Now I need to check what operation case 0x25 corresponds to in the switch statement. So the root cause is clear: bit 21 being 1 routes this instruction to the wrong decoder, and once there, the opcode bits don't match any of the defined cases in the switch statement, causing it to return an Unknown instruction error.\\n\\nThe solution is to fix the pattern matcher so that FMLA-like instructions with bit 21 set and bits 15:10 equal to 0b011010 get correctly routed to the NEON decoder instead of the scalar FP decoder. I need to refine the isFpDataProc2Source pattern to exclude these specific bit patterns, since isAdvSIMDSc2RegMisc should catch them but isn't matching correctly.\\n\\nLooking at the isAdvSIMDSc2RegMisc pattern, the op2 field expects bits[22:19] to be exactly 0b0100, but FMLA has bits[22:19] = 0b0010, which is why it's not being caught.\\n\\nThe solution is to modify isFpDataProc2Source to exclude the FMLA encoding by requiring bit[21] = 0 instead of making it a don't-care. This prevents FMLA from being incorrectly classified as a scalar floating-point multiply operation.\\n\\nI need to verify that no legitimate FpDataProc2Source instructions have bit[21] = 1. Looking at the actual ARM instruction encodings, the scalar FP multiply and divide instructions all have bit[21] = 0, confirming this fix is safe and won't break valid instructions.\\n\\nNow I'm tracing through what happens with FMLA specifically—when bit[21] = 1 and bits[15:10] = 0b011010, this creates the FMLA encoding, but I need to check what other valid scalar FP instructions might also have these bit patterns to ensure my check won't accidentally reject legitimate instructions.\\n\\nLet me look at the actual test cases and artifact files to see what real instructions are causing the collision, rather than working through all the theoretical combinations.\",\"signature\":\"c9c04c9336d2ccb9bcf21889e2dd1e288400f25047f05cd887bf53ab42b39b50\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":2096,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":66423},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"ac1da1d0-073f-4b5b-919d-c07ab40aeb81\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:36:53.173Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/ge