sc in test_cases:\\n    result = (inst & mask) == match\\n    print(f\\\"  0x{inst:08x} ({desc}): {'MATCH' if result else 'no match'}\\\")\\nPYEOF\"}}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":\"tool_use\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":85887,\"output_tokens\":583,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"fc8c9c57-fcd2-46cf-b764-ff5471d5e644\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:14:15.354Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"fc8c9c57-fcd2-46cf-b764-ff5471d5e644\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_4pfc80o1euzq_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"mask=0xf9c00000, match=0x19800000\\n  0x1e2e1000 (FMLS Sd,Sn,Sm): no match\\n  0x1e2a1000 (FMLS variant): no match\\n  0x1e3a1000 (FMLA Sd,Sn,Sm): no match\\n  0x1e220000 (FADD Sd,Sn,Sm (should not match)): no match\\n  0x1e220800 (FDIV Sd,Sn,Sm): no match\\n  0x1e604000 (FMUL Sd,Sn,Sm): no match\\n  0x1e7a0000 (FMSUB Sd,Sn,Sm (should match!)): no match\\n  0x1e7a0800 (FMADD Sd,Sn,Sm (should match!)): no match\\n  0x1e7a1000 (FNMADD Sd,Sn,Sm): no match\\n  0x1e7a1800 (FNMSUB Sd,Sn,Sm): no match\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"uuid\":\"6667ad46-0a15-47c3-9731-3400111d3785\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:14:15.489Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"fc8c9c57-fcd2-46cf-b764-ff5471d5e644\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"6667ad46-0a15-47c3-9731-3400111d3785\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d2017a8ee8cd73112430232aeeabc\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Still no match! But the mask and match are the same as before. The issue is with my Python implementation. Let me check - the bit_pattern function returns:\\nmask=0xf9c00000, match=0x19800000\\n\\nBut earlier when I had the wrong `'0'` for bit23, I also got mask=0xf9c00000, match=0x19000000.\\n\\nWait, the mask is the same! 0xf9c00000. That's because in my bit_pattern function, for 'X' I set all bits of that position in the mask (1 << pos). \\n\\nI'm seeing the issue now - the mask doesn't care about specific bits for 'X' characters, only the fixed bits matter. The match value is different because I set bit23 to 1 instead of 0, but since the mask ignores that bit anyway, it has no effect on the comparison. I need to figure out what mask is actually being used here.\\n\\nLet me trace through the character mapping more carefully. For the pattern `'0','0','0','1','1','X','X','1','1','0','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X'`, I'm going through each character and setting the corresponding bit in the mask based on its position - the fixed bits like '1' and '0' get their values stored in the match, while 'X' just sets the mask bit without changing the match. Continuing to set bits for the remaining positions...\\n\\nNow I'm verifying the mask and match values against the Python output, but there's a discrepancy—the mask matches but the match value shows 0x19000000 when I calculated 0x19800000, so let me trace back through the character positions to find where the mismatch is. I'm recounting the character positions in the mask string to find where the discrepancy is. Looking at positions 7 and 8 more carefully—they should correspond to bits 24 and 23 respectively, so when I set those bits in the mask and match values, I need to verify my Python code is correctly handling the 'X' at position 7 and the '1' at position 8. I'm checking my character array against the actual file contents to verify the mask value—comparing each element from position 7 onward to see where the discrepancy is coming from.\\n\\nNow I'm tracing through the bit operations: when I OR the mask with the shifted value and then set the match bit based on whether the character is '1', I'm getting 0x00800000 for this position, but my Python output shows the match as 0x19800000 instead of the expected 0x19000000.\\n\\nLooking at the bit positions more carefully, I see the issue—bit 24 should be 0 based on the instruction format, but my pattern has it marked as 'X' (don't care), which means the mask won't catch this mismatch.\\n\\nFor FMLS specifically, bits 24 and 26 are both 0, while bit 25 is 1. My current pattern uses 'X' for all three positions, which is too permissive—it would incorrectly match instructions where these bits have different values. I need to tighten the pattern to enforce the exact bit values that FMLS requires.\\n\\nThe key constraint I'm missing is bit 24: it must be 0, not don't-care. For FMLA, the instruction format has bit 24 set to 1 instead. So I need to adjust the pattern to require bit 24 = 0, which means the character at that position should be '0', not 'X'.\\n\\nNow I'm checking which instruction category each instruction belongs to by looking at the opcode ranges. FMLS and FMLA fall into the 000-011 range, while FMSUB, FMADD, FNMADD, and FNMSUB are in the 100-111 range, which confirms the pattern needs to distinguish between these groups based on bit 24.\\n\\nBut when I examine the actual instruction bits more closely, I'm seeing that the