nstruction needs to be masked and then compared to the match value. I'm verifying the bit positions against the pattern characters—when i=8 I need pos=23 to get bit 23=1, and when i=9 I need pos=22 to get bit 22=0, which matches the correct mask of 0xf9c00000.\\n\\nNow I'm realizing the issue: my corrected script was accidentally overwriting mask2 with the same value as the original, so I need to recalculate with the actual fixed pattern to see what's really happening. So bit 23 is set correctly in match2, but the instruction at bit 23 is actually 0, which means (0x1e2e1000 & 0xf9c00000) = 0x18000000 doesn't equal 0x18800000 — the pattern doesn't match the instruction at that bit position. But wait, my earlier manual verification showed bit 23 should be 1...\\n\\nLet me just check the binary representation directly: 0x1e2e1000 in hex breaks down to 0001 1110 0010 1110 0001 0000 0000 0000 in binary, and if I look at the 24th bit position from the right, that's where the mismatch is.\\n\\nActually, I need to clarify how gem5 counts bits — in their convention, bit 31 is the most significant bit, so bit 23 is actually the 24th bit from the right, which is in the lower nibble of the middle byte. I'm realizing my manual bit extraction was completely wrong, so let me write a Python script to verify the bit positions directly and understand how the bitPatternMatcher actually works in gem5.\",\"signature\":\"e9ed9aa82c88df3249815167b35b68f46b355884ace9070501e2663713887e83\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1033,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":86449},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"5d412a45-716d-4667-83e2-c4c6e1faf524\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:17:03.699Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version