dvSIMDExtract - unlikely\\n17. isAdvSIMDCopy - unlikely\\n18. isAdvSIMD3SameFp16 - unlikely\\n19. isAdvSIMD2RegMiscFp16 - unlikely\\n20. isAdvSIMD3RegExt - unlikely\\n21. isAdvSIMD2RegMisc - unlikely\\n22. isAdvSIMDAcrossLanes - unlikely\\n23. isAdvSIMD3Diff - unlikely\\n24. isAdvSIMD3Same - unlikely (THIS COULD MATCH!)\\n25. isAdvSIMDModImm - unlikely\\n26. isAdvSIMDShiftByImm - unlikely\\n27. isAdvSIMDIndexedElem - unlikely\\n28. isConvFloatPFixedP - NO (bit22=0, needs bit22=1)\\n29. isConvFloatPInt - unlikely\\n30. isFpMulMadd - SHOULD MATCH\\n31. isFpDataProc1Source - unlikely\\n32. isFpCompare - unlikely\\n33. isFpImmediate - unlikely\\n\\nContinuing through the rest of the pattern checks, most are unlikely to match until we reach the floating-point multiplication and addition patterns around step 30, which could potentially match depending on the instruction encoding.\\n\\nNow I'm realizing there are many more patterns between isFpMulMadd and isFpDataProc2Source that I need to examine more carefully. Let me trace through the generated code for the cryptographic and SIMD pattern matching functions to understand what's actually being checked. Looking at the SIMD and floating-point pattern matching functions that follow, I'm seeing a long sequence of checks for various SIMD operations like shift by immediate values, table lookups, permutations, extractions, copies, and floating-point conversions—each with their own pattern definitions that I'm tracing through to understand the bit-level matching logic. I'm looking at the bit pattern definitions for various floating-point and SIMD operations in the ARM ISA decoder, where each pattern specifies which bits must be set or cleared across the instruction encoding to identify a particular instruction type like FP multiplication and addition, comparison operations, and immediate-based variants. I need to figure out why the `isFpMulMadd` pattern isn't matching FMLS instructions, so I'm checking whether any of the earlier 