ng on a much wider value than I initially thought.\\n\\nLooking at the actual code, when the pattern extractor tries to apply a 22-character pattern starting at position 31 down to position 10, it's using bit positions that would normally be out of bounds for a 32-bit ExtMachInst. The generated code is trying to extract bits 37 through 30, which confirms that either ExtMachInst is actually 64 bits wide, or there's something else going on with how the bitfield backend is interpreting these positions. Looking at the disassembly more carefully, I see it's extracting bits 30-37 from x0, but ExtMachInst should only be 32 bits. Let me trace through what the actual bit positions mean in the ARM64 encoding versus what the mask computation would produce, since there's a mismatch between what the code is trying to extract and the actual width of the instruction field. I think I'm misunderstanding how the bitfield backend is working here. Let me look at what the `bits()` function actually does in the generated code, and check whether `MachInst` is 64-bit even though `ExtMachInst` might be 32-bit. The ARM64 ISA specification says instructions are 32-bit, but I should verify what gem5 actually uses for its internal type definitions.\",\"signature\":\"a626be88ba42c4b02b9fc573302ab39b1592f5cc789dd6f3773f998cc2084a58\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":825,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":117030},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"92730d13-d8f2-475f-9e87-2f6251b2397f\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:34:50.816Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"92730d13-d8f2-475f-9e87-2f6251b2397f\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":