ntUuid\":\"45a5e1ac-8625-4b6f-929d-317d1446da5b\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d254eed50fae56d2f4091af0552e8\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"`BitUnion64(ExtMachInst)` means ExtMachInst is a 64-bit type. But `MachInst` is `uint32_t`. And the `bitPatternMatcher` uses `MachInst`. Let me check if `ExtMachInst` is the same as `MachInst` for pattern matching purposes, or if there's a conversion.\\n\\nLooking at the `isFpMulMadd` pattern:\\n```cpp\\ninline constexpr auto isFpMulMadd = bitPatternMatcher<\\n    MachInst,\\n    31, 10,\\n    '0','0','0','1','1','X','X','0','0','0','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X'>();\\n```\\n\\nThe template parameter is `MachInst` (uint32_t). But `ExtMachInst` is 64-bit. When calling `isFpMulMadd(machInst)`, the `machInst` is an `ExtMachInst`. Does the pattern matcher handle this by implicitly converting to `MachInst`? Or does it use the full `ExtMachInst`?\\n\\nLooking at the `bitPatternMatcher` template:\\n```cpp\\ntemplate<typename T, int msb, int lsb, char curr, char... rest>\\nconstexpr auto bitPatternMatcher() {\\n```\\n\\nWhere `T = MachInst` (uint32_t). The lambda is `auto ret = [](T value) constexpr { return (value & mask) == match; }`. When `value` is `ExtMachInst` (64-bit), does it implicitly convert to `MachInst` (uint32_t)?\\n\\nYES! In C++, when you pass a larger integer type to a function/template expecting a smaller integer type, the value is implicitly converted. `ExtMachInst` (64-bit) → `MachInst` (uint32_t) would take the lower 32 bits.\\n\\n\\nWhen `ExtMachInst` (64-bit) is implicitly converted to `MachInst` (uint32_t), only the lower 32 bits are kept. For FMLS `0x1e2e1000`, the instruction is stored in the lower 32 bits of `ExtMachInst`, so this conversion should work correctly. But wait—if the instruction encoding is in the upper 32 bits, then the lower bits might be metadata or other fields,