is actually `ExtMachInst` (64-bit), the pattern matcher treats it as `uint32_t`. The key question is whether the comparison `(value & mask) == match` works correctly when `value` is the upper bits of `ExtMachInst`. If `ExtMachInst` stores the 32-bit instruction in bits 31:0 of its 64-bit storage, then `machInst & 0xFFFFFFFF` gives the instruction, but the pattern matcher might be extracting bits differently than expected.\\n\\nLet me trace through the C++ template expansion for `bitPatternMatcher<MachInst, 31, 10, ...>` where `MachInst` is `uint32_t`. The template computes a mask and match value for bits 31:10, and when evaluating `(value & mask) == match`, if `value` is `ExtMachInst` cast to `uint32_t`, it should extract bits 31:0 of that value.\\n\\nThe real question is how `MachInst` resolves when `bitPatternMatcher` is instantiated in the `ArmISAInst` namespace — it should find `ArmISA::MachInst` which is `uint32_t`, so the mask calculation `0xf9c00000` and match value `0x18000000` should be correct for bits 31:28 and 27 being specified.\\n\\nBut I'm realizing the instruction in `ExtMachInst` might actually be stored at a different bit position than I assumed. Let me trace through the decoder to see how `decodeInst` constructs the `ExtMachInst` value from the raw instruction data. Now I'm mapping out which bits of the extracted instruction correspond to the pattern—the condition code, instruction type, and various opcode fields—and verifying how they align with the bitfield definitions. in ExtMachInst. These are named aliases for specific bit ranges.\\n\\nBut `decoderFault` and `illegalExecution` are at bits 63:62 and 61. Then `debugStep` at bit 60. Then various SVE/FPSCR fields at bits 59:37. Then `thumb` at bit 36, `bigThumb` at 35, `aarch64` at 34. Then `sevenAndFour` at 33, `isMisc` at 32.\\n\\nSo bits 31:0 are reserved for `instBits` (the actual 32-bit instruction). And bits 63:32 are used for decoder state.\\n\\nThen named bitfields for specific bits:\\n- `Bitfield<27, 25> encoding` at bits 27:25 of the 64-bit ExtMachInst\\n\\nSo if `inst` is stored in bits 31:0, then `encoding` extracts bits 27:25 from those lower bits, which is correct. But I should check the order—the BitUnion definitions go from higher to lower bits, starting with `Bitfield<63, 62> decoderFault` at the top.\\n\\nNow I'm tracing through the specific fields: the SVE vector length occupies bits 59:56, FPSCR flags at bits 41:37, and mode selection bits at 36:34. The instruction fields start appearing at bit 33 with the sevenAndFour flag, then various encoding and opcode fields in the 27:0 range. Let me look at what comes after those top-level fields to see the complete picture. I'm mapping out how the instruction encoding bits correspond to the ARM64 instruction format, verifying that bit 31 through bit 15 are positioned correctly for the opcode, register indices, and various control fields. I'm noticing there's a mismatch between where `Bitfield<31, 28>` is defined in the 32-bit ARM types and where it would actually appear in the `ExtMachInst` bit union, so I need to figure out if there's an `inst31_28` field or if those upper bits are handled differently in the extended machine instruction format. I need to track down where `Bitfield` is actually defined to understand what happens when a bitfield's range extends beyond the storage size of the parent type.\",\"signature\":\"46e05081f9598ab8a249ac8bf64e69df38be9b1142a1b8be5d09c7e1d91b16ba\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":287,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":132595},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"477ab98b-4812-404e-b443-dc60268301aa\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:43:19.120Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.