,\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"3314204e-3443-498d-930d-454378a7341d\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_xgzp936qmfca_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"518\\t}\\n519\\t\\n520\\ttemplate<typename T, int pos, char curr_bit>\\n521\\tconstexpr auto\\n522\\tdontCareMask()\\n523\\t{\\n524\\t    if constexpr (curr_bit != 'X' && curr_bit != 'x') {\\n525\\t        return static_cast<T>(1) << pos;\\n526\\t    } else {\\n527\\t        return 0;\\n528\\t    }\\n529\\t}\\n530\\t\\n531\\ttemplate<typename T, int msb, int lsb, char curr, char... rest>\\n532\\tconstexpr auto\\n533\\tdontCareMask()\\n534\\t{\\n535\\t    if constexpr (msb < lsb) {\\n536\\t        return 0;\\n537\\t    } else if constexpr (msb == lsb) {\\n538\\t        return dontCareMask<T, msb, curr>();\\n539\\t    } else {\\n540\\t        return dontCareMask<T, msb, curr>() |\\n541\\t               dontCareMask<T, msb-1, lsb, rest...>();\\n542\\t    }\\n543\\t}\\n544\\t\\n545\\ttemplate<typename T, int pos, char curr_bit>\\n546\\tconstexpr auto\\n547\\tbitMatch()\\n548\\t{\\n549\\t    if constexpr (curr_bit == '1') {\\n550\\t        return 1ULL << pos;\\n551\\t    } else {\\n552\\t        return 0;\\n553\\t    }\\n554\\t}\\n555\\t\\n556\\ttemplate<typename T, int msb, int lsb, char curr, char... rest>\\n557\\tconstexpr auto\\n558\\tbitMatch()\\n559\\t{\\n560\\t    if constexpr (msb < lsb) {\\n561\\t        return 0;\\n562\\t    } else if constexpr (msb == lsb) {\\n563\\t        return bitMatch<T, msb, curr>();\\n564\\t    } else {\\n565\\t        return bitMatch<T, msb, curr>() |\\n566\\t               bitMatch<T, msb-1, lsb, rest...>();\\n567\\t    }\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"0ed866e3-3e48-4d1f-9ec2-2b470c82445a\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:18:31.337Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"3314204e-3443-498d-930d-454378a7341d\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"0ed866e3-3e48-4d1f-9ec2-2b470c82445a\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d2117d15579139fa92a510bd5bb7f\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Now I understand the gem5 C++ implementation:\\n\\nFor `dontCareMask<T, msb, lsb, curr, rest...>`:\\n- If `msb == lsb`: calls `dontCareMask<T, msb, curr>()` which:\\n  - if curr != 'X': returns `(1) << pos`  ← this SETS the bit in the mask\\n  - if curr == 'X': returns 0  ← this does NOT set the bit\\n\\n- If `msb > lsb`: returns `dontCareMask<T, msb, curr>() | dontCareMask<T, msb-1, lsb, rest...>()`\\n  - So it recursively processes from msb down to lsb\\n  \\nNow looking at the concrete example with `dontCareMask<T, 31, 10, '0','0'>`: it recursively evaluates each position, and for the literal '0' characters, it returns the bit shifted to that position, building up the mask as it goes.\\n\\nWait, I'm seeing the issue now—this function builds a mask for bits that are *specified* (0 or 1), not for don't-care bits. That's the opposite of what I'd expect for a don't-care mask. Let me reconsider the overall logic here. The function accumulates specified bits into the mask and match value, so that at runtime the check `(value & mask) == match` works correctly—it enforces that any explicitly specified bit must match, while unspecified bits (X) are ignored. This approach is more efficient than the alternative of only masking the X positions.\\n\\nNow I need to verify this with a Python simulation of the bitPatternMatcher template to make sure I understand how the parameters map to the actual bit positions.\",\"signature\":\"588a0bdaeef5ccd6b5cb255494b0c63b110d072d55f4827cbae7b22d717148bd\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":626,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":91089},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"f43ecfad-55c9-42a6-8234-af7b141824d4\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:18:41.670Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"f43ecfad-55c9-42a6-8234-af7b141824d4\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d2117d15579139fa92a510bd5bb7f\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Now I understand gem5's C++ logic. Let me write the correct Python simulation:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":626,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":91089},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"53ea4f58-5dac-44a6-9223-ec4cc11e487d\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:18:42.113Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"53ea4f58-5dac-44a6-9223-ec4cc11e487d\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d2117d15579139fa92a510bd5bb7f\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_function_zg6zibdcvyxr_1\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"python3 << 'PYEOF'\\n# gem5 bitPatternMatcher logic:\\n# dontCareMask: if curr!='X', returns (1 << pos), else 0. Recurses msb->lsb.\\n# bitMatch: if curr='1', returns (1 << pos), else 0. Recurses msb->lsb.\\n# mask = bits that ARE specified (not X), match = value of specified bits.\\n# At runtime: (val & mask) == match\\n\\ndef gem5_bitPatternMatcher(msb, lsb, *chars):\\n    def recurse(m_pos, lsb_inner, remaining):\\n        if m_pos < lsb_inner:\\n            return 0\\n        elif m_pos == lsb_inner:\\n            c = remaining[0]\\n            if c not in ('X', 'x'):\\n                return (1 << m_pos)\\n            return 0\\n        else:\\n            c = remaining[0]\\n            first = (1 << m_pos) if c not in ('X', 'x') else 0\\n            return first | recurse(m_pos - 1, lsb_inner, remaining[1:])\\n\\n    def recurse_match(m_pos, lsb_inner, remaining):\\n        if m_pos < lsb_inner:\\n            return 0\\n        elif m_pos == lsb_inner:\\n            c = remaining[0]\\n            if c == '1':\\n                return (1 << m_pos)\\n            return 0\\n        else:\\n            c = remaining[0]\\n            first = (1 << m_pos) if c == '1' else 0\\n            return first | recurse_match(m_pos - 1, lsb_inner, remaining[1:])\\n\\n    mask = recurse(msb, lsb, chars)\\n    match = recurse_match(msb, lsb, chars)\\n    return lambda val: (val & mask) == match, mask, match\\n\\nprint(\\\"=== Testing gem5 logic against KNOWN WORKING pattern ===\\\")\\n# isConvFloatPFixedP: 'X','0','X','1','1','1','0','0','1','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X'\\n# Expected: bit31=X,30=0,29=X,28=1,27=1,26=1,25=0,24=0,23=1,22=X,...\\nknown_chars = ['X','0','X','1','1','1','0','0','1','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X']\\n# Test with FMLS: bit31=0,30=0,29=0,28=1,27=1,26=1,25=1,24=0,23=0,22=0\\n# Expected: should NOT match (bit25=1 vs pattern bit25=0, bit23=0 vs pattern bit23=1)\\nm, mask, match = gem5_bitPatternMatcher(31, 10, *known_chars)\\nprint(f\\\"isConvFloatPFixedP: mask=0x{mask:08x}, match=0x{match:08x}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"  FMLS 0x1e2e1000: {m(0x1e2e1000)} (expected False)\\\")\\ninst_test = 0x1e230000  # bit25=0, bit24=0, bit23=1\\nprint(f\\\"  test 0x{inst_test:08x}: {m(inst_test)} (expected True)\\\")\\n\\nprint(\\\"\\\\n=== Current pattern in file ===\\\")\\n# Pattern: '0','0','0','1','1','X','X','0','1','0','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X'\\n# Bits:     31  30  29  28  27  26  25  24  23  22  21  20  19  18  17  16  15  14  13  12  11  10\\nchars_cur = ['0','0','0','1','1','X','X','0','1','0','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X','X']\\nm, mask, match = gem5_bitPatternMatcher(31, 10, *chars_cur)\\nprint(f\\\"mask=0x{mask:08x}, match=0x{match:08x}\\\")\\nfor inst, desc in [(0x1e2e1000,\\\"FMLS\\\"),(0x1e3a1000,\\\"FMLA\\\"),(0x1e220000,\\\"FADD\\\"),(0x1e7a0800,\\\"FMADD\\\"),(0x1e7a0000,\\\"FMSUB\\\")]:\\n    print(f\\\"  0x{inst:08x} ({desc}): {'MATCH' if m(inst) else 'no match'}\\\")\\nPYEOF\"}}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":\"tool_use\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":626,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":91089,\"output_tokens\":1486,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"69550ced-a635-409c-b6e5-2458ffb3ce9f\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:19:01.961Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\"