\\"mask=0x{mask:08x}\\\")\\nprint(f\\\"match=0x{match:08x}\\\")\\n\\ntests = [\\n    (0x1e2e1000, \\\"FMLS 0x1e2e1000\\\", True),\\n    (0x1e3a1000, \\\"FMLA 0x1e3a1000\\\", True),\\n    (0x1e220000, \\\"FADD 0x1e220000\\\", False),\\n    (0x1e220800, \\\"FDIV 0x1e220800\\\", False),\\n    (0x1e604000, \\\"FMUL 0x1e604000\\\", False),\\n    (0x1e7a0000, \\\"FMSUB 0x1e7a0000\\\", True),\\n    (0x1e7a0800, \\\"FMADD 0x1e7a0800\\\", True),\\n    (0x1e7a1000, \\\"FNMADD 0x1e7a1000\\\", True),\\n    (0x1e7a1800, \\\"FNMSUB 0x1e7a1800\\\", True),\\n]\\nprint()\\nfor inst, desc, expected in tests:\\n    result = (inst & mask) == match\\n    status = \\\"PASS\\\" if result == expected else \\\"FAIL\\\"\\n    print(f\\\"  0x{inst:08x} ({desc}): {'MATCH' if result else 'no match'} [{status}]\\\")\\nPYEOF\"}}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":\"tool_use\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":377,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":96512,\"output_tokens\":1379,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"3640d68b-ed08-4e04-b57e-f8a0700bf96e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:23:07.741Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"3640d68b-ed08-4e04-b57e-f8a0700bf96e\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_rir7daj7ko9i_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"mask=0xf9c00000\\nmatch=0x18000000\\n\\n  0x1e2e1000 (FMLS 0x1e2e1000): MATCH [PASS]\\n  0x1e3a1000 (FMLA 0x1e3a1000): MATCH [PASS]\\n  0x1e220000 (FADD 0x1e220000): MATCH [FAIL]\\n  0x1e220800 (FDIV 0x1e220800): MATCH [FAIL]\\n  0x1e604000 (FMUL 0x1e604000): no match [PASS]\\n  0x1e7a0000 (FMSUB 0x1e7a0000): no match [FAIL]\\n  0x1e7a0800 (FMADD 0x1e7a0800): no match [FAIL]\\n  0x1e7a1000 (FNMADD 0x1e7a1000): no match [FAIL]\\n  0x1e7a1800 (FNMSUB 0x1e7a1800): no match [FAIL]\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"uuid\":\"50c20295-9f6e-485e-bd25-fa8380e6cee5\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:23:07.878Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"3640d68b-ed08-4e04-b57e-f8a0700bf96e\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"50c20295-9f6e-485e-bd25-fa8380e6cee5\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d222c9c5cfbc195ecffadbb2cde1a\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"OK! So the simulation now shows:\\n- FMLS and FMLA MATCH correctly\\n- FADD and FDIV MATCH (but I expected no match) - let me check why\\n- FMUL doesn't match\\n- FMADD/FMSUB/FNMADD/FNMSUB don't match (but I expected them to match)\\n\\nWait, I was wrong about FADD and FDIV. Let me check:\\n- FADD 0x1e220000: 0001_1110_0010_0010_0000_0000_0000_0000\\n  - Bits: 31:28=0001, 27:1, 26:1, 25:0, 24:0, 23:0, 22:0, 21:0, 20:0\\n  - Pattern: bit31=0,30=0,29=0,28=1,27=1, bit24=0, bit23=0, bit22=0\\n  - All match! So FADD DOES match the pattern.\\n\\nBut that can't be right—the FADD instruction should have a different opcode and shouldn't match this pattern. Let me verify the exact binary representation of FADD to see where the mismatch actually is. I need to look up the actual ARM64 encoding for FADD more carefully. According to the ARM Reference Manual, FADD (scalar) is encoded as 0001_1110_0010_0001_0_0000_0000, but my test instruction 0x1e220000 has a different opcode field at bits 15:12 and 10, so it's not actually FADD—I need to recalculate what instruction this encoding really is. I'm realizing the constraint I'm applying with bits 24, 23, and 22 equal to 0 matches all three instruction types I'm testing, which means my pattern isn't specific enough to distinguish between them. I need to reconsider what other bits I should be checking to properly identify FMLS/FMLA versus FADD/FDIV. bit 21 in FMLS/FMLA has opcode meaning and could distinguish them from FADD/FDIV.\\n\\nLooking at FMLS encoding: `0x1e2e1000`, bit21=1, bit20=0\\nLooking at FADD encoding: `0x1e220000`, bit21=0, bit20=0\\nThese are differentiated by bits 21:15!\\n\\nLet me check if there's a simpler way. Looking at the ARM encoding:\\n- FMLS: bits15:10=0x04, bit21=1\\n- FMLA: bits15:10=0x0B, bit21=1\\n- FADD: bits15:10=0x08, bit21=0\\n- FDIV: bits15:10=0x1E, bit21=0\\n\\nActually wait, I should look at the actual instruction encoding more carefully. The FMLS instruction has bits 15:12 set to 0001, which creates the switchVal of 0x02 in decodeFpDataProc2Source. But my isFpMulMadd pattern only checks bits 14:10, which means it would see opcode 0x04 for FMLS, and I need to verify what opcode FADD actually has in that same bit range. For FMLS specifically, bit22 is 0, which means it's already excluded from the other pattern since that one requires bit22 to be 1. So FMLS naturally bypasses both `isFpDataProc1Source` and `isConvFloatPFixedP`, meaning it could potentially match `isFpMulMadd` instead. For FADD, the pattern would need to match the specific bits where FMLS differs from FADD to distinguish between them.\\n\\nWait, I'm confusing myself here. Let me check the actual bit values for FADD more carefully. Looking at `0x1e220000`, the opcode bits are `0010`, which is 0x02, not 0x08 like I thought. That's because the instruction encoding packs the opcode differently than I was assuming. I'm extracting the 5-bit opcode field from FADD's binary encoding and getting 0x04, which matches FMLS instead. Let me verify this is correct by checking the ARM manual's definition of the opcode bits for the Floating-point data-processing (2-source) instruction class.\\n\\nLooking at the actual instruction encodings: FADD and FMLS both produce switchVal=0x02, but FMUL gives 0x03 and FDIV gives 0x0F. The decoder distinguishes between them using the full 5-bit opcode field, where FADD's bits 14:10 are 00100 (0x04) while FMLS's are 00010 (0x02).\\n\\nThe real problem is that `isFpMulMadd` matches all three instructions because it only constrains bits 31:24, 23:22, and 22:20, leaving too many degrees of freedom. This causes FADD to incorrectly route to `decodeFpMulMadd` instead of its proper handler. I need to add the 5-bit opcode as an additional constraint to distinguish between FMLS, FMLA, and FADD. Looking at the ARM manual, I need to verify the exact opcode field positions for the scalar three-same FP instructions. For FMLS specifically, the opcode is split across bits 15:13 and bits 11:10, which gives me 0x01 when combined, not 0x04 as I initially thought. I'm realizing the ISA compiler already handles this decoding with switch statements, so I just need to check the 5-bit opcode field at bits 14:10 to distinguish between FMLS, FMLA, and other instructions. FMLS and FMLA have different values in that field, so I can use that to differentiate them.\\n\\nThe real issue is that my pattern is too broad—it's catching FADD instructions when it should only catch FMLS and FMLA. I need to refine the pattern to exclude FADD by requiring bit 15 to be 1, which would filter out the FADD case.\\n\\nLet me reconsider the bit positions in the encoding. The switch statement groups bits 15:12 and 23:22 together to create the switch value, so I need to be careful about which specific bits I'm checking to distinguish between these