alar three-same FP instructions by constraining those fields more precisely. I need to refine the bit pattern to distinguish FMLS and FMLA from other instructions—specifically, I should set bit 21 to 1 and constrain bits 14:10 to either 00010 for FMLS or 01011 for FMLA, which will correctly filter out FADD (which has bit 21 = 0). The ARM architecture defines these encodings, so FMLS and FMLA occupy a valid slot in the instruction space rather than being reserved. To properly decode these instructions, I need to match against the specific 5-bit opcode values that distinguish FMLS (0x04) and FMLA (0x0B) from the other instruction types in this encoding space.\\n\\nHowever, I can't directly express a switch-case pattern in the simple bitmask format that `bitPatternMatcher` supports, so I'm considering using `bit15=1` as a heuristic to narrow down the candidates, though I need to verify whether this bit is consistently set across the FMLS/FMLA variants. Looking at the instruction encodings more carefully, FMLS has bit15=0 while FMLA has bit15=1, so the existing pattern with bit15=X correctly captures both variants without over-constraining. The real problem is that my current pattern accidentally matches FADD as well, which suggests I need to reconsider what additional bits actually distinguish FMLS and FMLA from the broader category. I need to reconsider how the bitmask actually works here. Let me trace through the calculation more carefully—when I apply the mask to the instruction, I'm getting a partial match on some bits but not all the specific bits I thought I was constraining. The issue is that my current approach isn't capturing the full opcode field correctly; I need to verify exactly which bits are being checked and ensure the mask and match values are properly aligned. I see the real issue now—FADD is also matching the pattern, so it would get decoded by `decodeFpMulMadd` instead of its proper handler, which would cause incorrect instruction execution. I need 