coder-ns.cc.inc\",\"offset\":81450,\"limit\":60}}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":\"tool_use\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":42,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":107688,\"output_tokens\":96,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"a2cdfa21-7d0a-418f-ae3f-9db42f5a90bf\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:30:16.452Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"a2cdfa21-7d0a-418f-ae3f-9db42f5a90bf\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_706p8sfn4nj1_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"81450\\t    decodeFpDataProc2Source(ExtMachInst machInst)\\n81451\\t    {\\n81452\\t        if (bits(machInst, 31) ||\\n81453\\t                bits(machInst, 29)) {\\n81454\\t            return new Unknown64(machInst);\\n81455\\t        }\\n81456\\t        RegIndex rd = (RegIndex)(uint32_t)bits(machInst,  4,  0);\\n81457\\t        RegIndex rn = (RegIndex)(uint32_t)bits(machInst,  9,  5);\\n81458\\t        RegIndex rm = (RegIndex)(uint32_t)bits(machInst, 20, 16);\\n81459\\t        uint8_t switchVal = (bits(machInst, 15, 12) << 0) |\\n81460\\t                            (bits(machInst, 23, 22) << 4);\\n81461\\t        switch (switchVal) {\\n81462\\t          case 0x00: // FMUL Sd = Sn * Sm\\n81463\\t            return new FMulS(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81464\\t          case 0x10: // FMUL Dd = Dn * Dm\\n81465\\t            return new FMulD(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81466\\t          case 0x30: // FMUL Hd = Hn * Hm\\n81467\\t            return new FMulH(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81468\\t          case 0x01: // FDIV Sd = Sn / Sm\\n81469\\t            return new FDivS(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81470\\t          case 0x11: // FDIV Dd = Dn / Dm\\n81471\\t            return new FDivD(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81472\\t          case 0x31: // FDIV Hd = Hn / Hm\\n81473\\t            return new FDivH(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81474\\t          case 0x02: // FADD Sd = Sn + Sm\\n81475\\t            return new FAddS(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81476\\t          case 0x12: // FADD Dd = Dn + Dm\\n81477\\t            return new FAddD(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81478\\t          case 0x32: // FADD Hd = Hn + Hm\\n81479\\t            return new FAddH(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81480\\t          case 0x03: // FSUB Sd = Sn - Sm\\n81481\\t            return new FSubS(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81482\\t          case 0x13: // FSUB Dd = Dn - Dm\\n81483\\t            return new FSubD(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81484\\t          case 0x33: // FSUB Hd = Hn - Hm\\n81485\\t            return new FSubH(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81486\\t          case 0x04: // FMAX Sd = max(Sn, Sm)\\n81487\\t            return new FMaxS(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81488\\t          case 0x14: // FMAX Dd = max(Dn, Dm)\\n81489\\t            return new FMaxD(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81490\\t          case 0x34: // FMAX Hd = max(Hn, Hm)\\n81491\\t            return new FMaxH(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81492\\t          case 0x05: // FMIN Sd = min(Sn, Sm)\\n81493\\t            return new FMinS(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81494\\t          case 0x15: // FMIN Dd = min(Dn, Dm)\\n81495\\t            return new FMinD(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81496\\t          case 0x35: // FMIN Hd = min(Hn, Hm)\\n81497\\t            return new FMinH(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81498\\t          case 0x06: // FMAXNM Sd = maxNum(Sn, Sm)\\n81499\\t            return new FMaxNMS(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81500\\t          case 0x16: // FMAXNM Dd = maxNum(Dn, Dm)\\n81501\\t            return new FMaxNMD(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81502\\t          case 0x36: // FMAXNM Hd = maxNum(Hn, Hm)\\n81503\\t            return new FMaxNMH(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81504\\t          case 0x07: // FMINNM Sd = minNum(Sn, Sm)\\n81505\\t            return new FMinNMS(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81506\\t          case 0x17: // FMINNM Dd = minNum(Dn, Dm)\\n81507\\t            return new FMinNMD(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n81508\\t          case 0x37: // FMINNM Hd = minNum(Hn, Hm)\\n81509\\t            return new FMinNMH(machInst, rd, rn, rm);\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"d52b7809-604e-4f0a-988b-c2c368623f21\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:30:16.573Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"a2cdfa21-7d0a-418f-ae3f-9db42f5a90bf\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"d52b7809-604e-4f0a-988b-c2c368623f21\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d23d80140f18844fdc528b240e06d\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"`decodeFpDataProc2Source` checks `if (bits(machInst, 31) || bits(machInst, 29))`. FMLS has bit31=0 and bit29=0, so this passes!Then it computes `switchVal = (bits(machInst, 15, 12) << 0) | (bits(machInst, 23, 22) << 4)`.FMLS `0x1e2e1000`:\\n- bits15:12 = 0x1 = 0b0001\\n- bits23:22 = 0x0 = 0b00- switchVal = 0x1 | (0 << 4) = 0x01This is `case 0x01` which is `FDIV Sd = Sn / Sm`!But this is wrong - FMLS should not go through `decodeFpDataProc2Source` at all. \\n\\nNow I need to check whether `isFpDataProc2Source` actually matches the FMLS instruction encoding, and if so, I need to trace through the decode flow: `decodeAarch64Inst` routes to `decodeFpAdvSIMD`, which then calls `isFpMulMadd` to determine if this is a multiply-add instruction.\\n\\nBut wait—I'm realizing the actual issue might be simpler. If `decodeFpAdvSIMD` correctly matches `isFpMulMadd` and returns the right `FmlsDX` instruction, then the panic about \\\"unknown instruction\\\" shouldn't be happening at all. Either the decode is matching a different instruction entirely, or the execute function itself is failing to recognize it. Let me double-check what instruction we're actually decoding here. Looking at the decode function, it's checking the instruction encoding to route it properly—the issue is that the multiply-add variants (FMADD, FMLA, FMLS) are being caught by `isFpMulMadd` and dispatched to `decodeFpMulMadd`, which then creates the appropriate instruction objects based on the size and opcode fields.\\n\\nBut here's what's confusing me: if the decode is working correctly and creating valid FmlsDX objects, why is the simulator crashing? Let me trace through what `isFpMulMadd` is actually matching against to see if there's a pattern collision happening earlier in the decode chain. I'm tracing through the generated binary to verify the mask and match values being computed at compile time, and now I need to actually run through the bit operations to confirm the function returns true for the test instruction. I'm computing the match value by extracting specific bits from the instruction encoding, but I'm realizing I need to recalculate more carefully since the bit positions might be off. So the mask is indeed 0xf9c00000, which means the generated code should be computing these values at compile time using bitwise operations on the character literals. Let me trace through what the actual C++ template is generating to verify this is working correctly. I'm realizing the character '0' converts to 48 in ASCII, which would create a very different mask than intended—but the generated code must be handling this correctly since it works. Let me reconsider how the template parameters are actually being processed in the real implementation. I'm working through the bit manipulation to compute the mask value, checking each bit position systematically to verify the binary representation and convert it to hexadecimal. Now I'm verifying the match value by checking each bit position—'1' bits should be at 28 and 27, which gives me 0x18000000. The calculation checks out, so I'm moving on to verify that the AND operation between the instruction value and mask produces the expected match.\\n\\nThe math confirms the pattern should match, but it's still failing at runtime. I'm wondering if there's a subtle difference in how the pattern is being used—specifically whether the template is being instantiated differently in `decoder-ns.cc.inc` versus elsewhere. Let me search through the `decode-method.cc.inc` file to see if there's a duplicate definition of `isFpMulMadd` that might be shadowing the correct one.\\n\\nActually, I'm noticing the `using namespace ArmISAInst;` directive in the decode method—that's just opening up the class names without affecting any namespace-scoped functions. The `Aarch64` namespace functions should be called directly. But now I'm looking at `decodeFpMulMadd` more closely, particularly the opcode extraction logic...\\n\\nWait, I'm seeing the real issue now. The generated code for `decodeFpMulMadd` has `case 0:` that falls through without a return statement, so execution continues into the next case block. That's causing the wrong instruction to be returned. Tracing through the FMLS case with `0x1e2e1000`: the size extracts to 0 (half-p