ch`, if `machInst` is 64-bit but `mask`/`match` are 32-bit, the comparison might work correctly because the upper bits of `ExtMachInst` would be zero (or have decoder state bits).Wait, but the disassembly showed `ubfx x24, x0, #30, #8` - if `MachInst` is 32-bit, extracting bits 37:30 from a 64-bit register doesn't make sense...\\n\\nActually, wait. The `ExtMachInst` BitUnion has decoder state bits in the upper bits (63:32). The `bitPatternMatcher` might be using `MachInst` (32-bit) internally for the computation, but when applied to an `ExtMachInst` (64-bit), the compiler might need to extract the lower 32 bits.\\n\\nThe key insight is that `MachInst` (32-bit instruction) is stored in the lower bits of `ExtMachInst` (64-bit). When the pattern matcher uses `MachInst` in its template, the `value` parameter gets implicitly converted from `ExtMachInst` to `MachInst` (taking the lower 32 bits), then the mask and match are computed on those 32 bits. The upper 32 bits of `ExtMachInst` contain decoder state and get ignored during the pattern matching.\\n\\nBut the disassembly shows `ubfx x24, x0, #30, #8`, which extracts bits 30-37 as 8 bits—this doesn't align with a 32-bit `MachInst`. I'm wondering if `MachInst` is actually 64-bit, or if there's something else going on with how the bitfield operations are being compiled. Let me trace through the template instantiation more carefully to see what's actually happening.\",\"signature\":\"a6983c308313cc6779219efa5957a2126390b5b80635ece9040190d1bb0065e5\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":545,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":121376},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"09704a82-7534-40b9-9bf7-354cf40ca5d2\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:36:28.762Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-