el decode path is working as expected.\\n\\nLet me check the actual pattern matching logic. The `isFpMulMadd` pattern is checking specific bits: bit 31 is 0, bit 30 is 0, bit 29 is 0, bit 28 is 1, and bit 27 is 1. I need to verify these bit positions are being extracted correctly from the instruction encoding. I'm seeing that FMLA actually matches the pattern while FMLS doesn't, which seems backwards. Let me double-check the bit extraction logic against the actual instruction encoding to make sure I'm reading the bits correctly. Let me verify the encoding bits for both instructions to confirm the pattern matching. FMLS and FMLA should have the same first 32 bits, which would mean they both match or both don't—but the simulation shows only FMLA matching. Looking more carefully at the bit extraction logic, I need to check how the Python code is actually computing the masked value for FMLS to see where the mismatch is occurring. I'm checking whether FMLS actually matches the bit pattern, and it turns out the simulation result showing \\\"no match\\\" was misleading—the corrected Python code confirms FMLS should match. Now I need to figure out why gem5 isn't recognizing this, so I'm going to add debug output to trace what's happening.\",\"signature\":\"970ada1f7daba797f61336b95251af52faa6fb040d49b0cb105e4b03a5076260\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":935,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":128473},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"810dd858-62e8-47d6-8fcb-621749d15d5e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:40:15.894Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"810dd858-62e8-47d6-8fcb-621749d15d5e\",\"isSidechain\":