          } else if (bits(machInst, 26) == 0)\\n2897\\t                                      // bit 28:26=100\\n2898\\t                                      return decodeDataProcImm(machInst);\\n2899\\t                                  else\\n2900\\t                                      // bit 28:26=101\\n2901\\t                                      return decodeBranchExcSys(*this, machInst);\\n2902\\t                              } else if (bits(machInst, 25) == 0) {\\n2903\\t                                  // bit 27=1, 25=0\\n2904\\t                                  return decodeLoadsStores(machInst);\\n2905\\t                              } else if (bits(machInst, 26) == 0) {\\n2906\\t                                  // bit 27:25=101\\n2907\\t                                  return decodeDataProcReg(machInst);\\n2908\\t                              } else if (bits(machInst, 24) == 1 &&\\n2909\\t                                         bits(machInst, 31, 28) == 0xF) {\\n2910\\t                                  return new Gem5Op64(machInst);\\n2911\\t                              } else {\\n2912\\t                                  // bit 27:25=111\\n2913\\t                                  switch(decoderFlavor){\\n2914\\t                                  default:\\n2915\\t                                      return decodeFpAdvSIMD<GenericDecoder>(machInst);\\n2916\\t                                  }\\n2917\\t                              }\\n2918\\t                          }\\n2919\\t                          break;\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"3b7a6347-6867-4dae-90fd-6b9d8a2a9f09\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:23:44.488Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"ab7b3f77-e266-41fc-a307-f4c15ad6221d\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"3b7a6347-6867-4dae-90fd-6b9d8a2a9f09\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d30606d3656d622f7ce5af226078d\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Now I can see the issue! Line 2888-2894:\\n\\n```cpp\\n} else {\\n    if ((bits(machInst, 25) == 0x0) && (bits(machInst, 31) == 0x1)) {\\n        // bit 31:25=1xx0000\\n        return decodeSmeInst(machInst);\\n    } else {\\n        return new Unknown64(machInst);\\n    }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWait, FMLS has bit31=0. So `bits(machInst, 31) == 0x1` is FALSE. Then it goes to the `else` branch and returns `new Unknown64(machInst)`.\\n\\n\\nBut that doesn't make sense—if bit27:25=111 and bit28=1, the control flow should hit the final `else` clause that calls `decodeFpAdvSIMD<GenericDecoder>`. The `decodeInst` debug output confirms the function is being called with the FMLS instruction, but the backtrace is truncated and only shows the panic at the end, which suggests the crash happens after the decode attempt.\\n\\nThe real issue is that `machInst` is a 64-bit `ExtMachInst` value (0x1e2e1000), and when the decoder accesses specific bits like `bits(machInst, 31)`, it's reading from the upper bits of that 64-bit structure rather than the lower bits where the actual instruction encoding lives. This mismatch between where the bits are stored and where the decoder expects to find them is causing the wrong instruction to be decoded.\\n\\nNow I'm examining the bit extraction logic more carefully. The `bits` function shifts right by the lower bit position and masks to isolate the field, so `bits(0x1e2e1000, 31)` should correctly extract bit 31 as 0, but I need to trace through exactly what `ExtMachInst` contains at that point to understand why the decoder is making the wrong decision. I'm extracting specific bit ranges from the instruction encoding to verify the condition—bit 25 is 1, bit 31 is 0, bit 27 is 1, and bit 24 is also 1, which should all route to `decodeFpAdvSIMD`. But there's a problem: the `decodeInst` debug output is missing from the backtrace, which means that debug message either wasn't printed before the binary crashed, or it was printed but got lost somehow.\\n\\nActually, looking at the backtrace more carefully, `decodeInst` is in the call stack, but the `DBG decodeInst ENTRY` message should have been printed before the crash. If it wasn't, then either the message got buffered or stderr was redirected. Let me trace through what should have happened: the test runs, `decodeInst` gets called with the instruction, the debug message prints, then somewhere in the decode logic a different `Unknown64` gets returned and executed, which eventually panics.\\n\\nWait, I think I'm seeing the real issue now. The backtrace shows `UndefinedInstruction::invoke` being called with `unknown=1`, which means a `StaticInstPtr` was created as an `Unknown64` instance. So `decodeInst` was definitely called, but the instruction didn't match any decode pattern and returned `Unknown64` instead of the actual instruction.\\n\\nNow I need to check if `decodeFpAdvSIMD` was actually invoked during the decode process. The debug output should have printed from that function, but it's not showing up. Let me look more carefully at the decode logic to see where `decodeFpAdvSIMD` gets called versus where the `Unknown64` is being returned.\\n\\nLooking at the execution path, the instruction should hit the final `else` clause that calls `decodeFpAdvSIMD<GenericDecoder>`, but if that's not happening, then the condition checking `bits(machInst, 24) == 1 && bits(machInst, 31, 28) == 0xF` might be matching instead, routing it to `decodeGem5Ops` instead.\\n\\nLet me trace through the bit extractions for 0x1e2e1000: bit 24 is 0, and bits 31-28 are 0x1, so that condition is false. Now I'm checking the other branches - bit 27 is 1, bit 25 is 1, and bit 26 is 1, so those conditions also don't match.\\n\\nThis means the final else clause should be executing, which calls `decodeFpAdvSIMD<GenericDecoder>`. So why isn't that debug message showing up? Maybe the build didn't include my debug print statement, or there's something about how the generated code is being compiled that's preventing it from appearing in the binary.\",\"signature\":\"e86fb9966530dbbf63c1d0a55e29b7449ba826937dcb0a0486df7e919b726739\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1018,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":159897},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"00e9e622-3fb0-45a0-acb3-293ca8456981\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:24:14.664Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"00e9e622-3fb0-45a0-acb3-293ca8456981\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d30606d3656d622f7ce5af226078d\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me check if the generated decoder-ns.cc.inc has the debug:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1018,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":159897},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"d672e1af-111a-464f-9318-1004e5f05d85\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:24:15.074Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"en