on of floating-point and SIMD instructions. So the undefined instruction exception is likely happening because gem5's internal instruction handling is rejecting the instruction before it even reaches the FPU execution path.\\n\\nLet me reconsider where this fault could be coming from. The fault happens during execute, not decode, and I've confirmed the execute method exists in the binary. The decodeFpMulMadd is returning a properly instantiated FmlsDX object, so the issue must be elsewhere. Maybe there's a mismatch between what decodeFpMulMadd expects and what FmlsDX is actually providing.\\n\\nActually, looking back at the fault origin—the undefined instruction exception is being thrown from within gem5's runtime, not from my execute method. Let me trace through the execute body more carefully to see where the fault gets triggered. The enabled check passes, and then the actual computation happens, but there might be something in the template instantiation that's causing the issue.\\n\\nLooking at the code snippet, I see it's reading and writing to various FP registers and destination registers, then it sets up vector operations with 32-bit registers and element counts. The real question is whether the FMLS instruction is actually valid for the specific register width being used—maybe the 16-bit variant doesn't support this particular operation.\\n\\nLet me trace back through the decode function to see how it determines which operand widths are legal for FMLS. The opcode handling for half-precision elements suggests that FMLS is valid there, but I should verify the complete decode logic to confirm there's no constraint I'm missing.\\n\\nLooking at the operand setup, the FmlsDX class expects three consecutive 64-bit FP registers as its operands, which aligns with how D-register operations work in AArch64. This checks out so far.\\n\\nBut I'm realizing the real issue might not be with the register setup at all—the undefined instruction error is firing during execution