uld trace through exactly what that does with my mask and match values to see where the logic is breaking down. I see the issue now—the mask is capturing bits that should be wildcards. The problem is that when the character is a wildcard, I'm still setting the corresponding bit in the mask, which incorrectly constrains those positions. I need to modify the logic so that wildcards don't contribute to the mask at all, while still handling the bit extraction correctly for both masked and non-masked values. I see the issue now—when I apply the mask to extract just the constrained bits, the result isn't aligned to bit 0, so comparing it directly to the match value won't work. I need to shift the masked result right to align it properly before comparing.\\n\\nActually, looking at the gem5 implementation more carefully, the mask and match values are pre-aligned so that the comparison `(value & mask) == match` works directly without any shifting needed.\\n\\nLet me trace through with concrete bit positions to verify this logic holds. I'm mapping out how each constraint in the pattern corresponds to actual bit positions, then building up the mask and match values by iterating through the characters and setting the appropriate bits based on whether each position should be forced high, forced low, or left as a wildcard. I see the issue now—the match value needs to be shifted to align with the mask positions, so when I OR together the individual bit constraints, I'm actually building up the correct value in the right bit positions rather than always starting from bit 0. Continuing to verify the pattern for bit 27, then moving on to bit 24 which should be a wildcard 'X', and now working through bit 23 which needs to be set to 1.\\n\\nFinishing the calculation for bit 22 as a wildcard, then summing all the mask values to get 0xf9c00000 and the match value to 0x18800000. But wait—I'm realizing I made an error earlier about which bits should be constrained versus wildcards. Let me reconsider the corrected pattern from the beginning.\\n\\nFor the corrected bit assignments, bit 24 needs to be 0 instead of 1, which means the mask and match values recalculate to 0xf8800000 and 0x18000000 respectively. Checking against the instruction 0x1e2e1000 confirms this works correctly.\\n\\nBut I need to verify the bit ordering more carefully—the pattern uses MSB-first, so I should trace through which character maps to which bit position to make sure I'm not misaligning things.\\n\\nLooking at the FMLS encoding specifically, the instruction has bit 27 set to 1 and bit 24 set to 0, but the current pattern is matching bit 24 as 1 instead—that's the mismatch I need to fix.\\n\\nNow I'm checking the actual file pattern character by character to see what's being constrained and where the error lies.\\n\\nI notice the Python simulation was incorrectly adding mask bits for wildcards, but gem5's implementation handles this correctly by only setting mask bits for concrete values. Let me verify the gem5 calculation with bit24='0' instead of '1' to see if that's the actual source of the mismatch. bits15:10=XXXXX = char[16]='X', char[17]='X', char[18]='X', char[19]='X', char[20]='X'\\n\\nWait, I have 22 chars total (indices 0-21):\\n0: '0' (bit31)\\n1: '0' (bit30)\\n2: '0' (bit29)\\n3: '1' (bit28)\\n4: '1' (bit27)\\n5: 'X' (bit26)\\n6: 'X' (bit25)\\n7: '0' (bit24) ← CORRECTED from '1'\\n8: '1' (bit23)\\n9: '0' (bit22)\\n10: 'X' (bit21)\\n11: 'X' (bit20)\\n12: 'X' (bit19)\\n13: 'X' (bit18)\\n14: 'X' (bit17)\\n15: 'X' (bit16)\\n16: 'X' (bit15)\\n17: 'X' (bit14)\\n18: 'X' (bit13)\\n19: 'X' (bit12)\\n20: 'X' (bit11)\\n21: 'X' (bit10)\\n\\nSo the mask and match values are calculated by setting the appropriate bits for each constraint, giving mask=0xf8800000 and match=0x18000000.\\n\\nWhen I verify this against FMLS 0x1e2e1000, the bitwise AND operation produces 0x18000000, which matches the expected value, confirming the pattern is correct.\\n\\nThe issue was that char[7] (bit24) needed to be '0' instead of '1', and I also need to double-check the comment on the op1 field. I'm verifying the constraint ordering in the pattern—bit24 is '0' and bit23 is '1'—and making sure the comment matches the corrected values so it accurately reflects what the pattern expects. Verifying the bit constraints for the corrected pattern against the FMLS instruction 0x1e2e1000, confirming that all specified bits match correctly: bits31:28, 27, 24, 23, and 22 all align as expected.\\n\\nThe corrected pattern should now be applied, though I need to verify the exact character count—counting through the bit assignments from bit31 down to bit10 to ensure the pattern has the right number of elements. I'm mapping out how each character in the string corresponds to bit positions, working through the pattern to identify which bits are fixed versus wildcards, then calculating the mask and match values accordingly. For bit24, the correct value is '0', so I'm updating that position in the array.\",\"signature\":\"f0453a7b15afa8639235be804436ed7a61c06e512a913ffad18c0f78d38a0368\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":89027},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"89d78d15-6129-443f-85f0-8fb43f3be778\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:18:06.752Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"89d78d15-6129-443f-85f0-8fb43f3be778\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d20d237c10e16c2a1c7daaa87f6c3\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The Python simulation had a bug (setting mask bits for 'X'). The