r whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"3100d52b-1fb0-4d29-8563-aaec9d147dc7\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:36:55.804Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"26293123-bad2-4f46-90fe-ebb92f8d9a29\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"3100d52b-1fb0-4d29-8563-aaec9d147dc7\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d2568f2fd2abd4b3b74b44b3255d5\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"I see that the bitfields go from `Bitfield<63, 62>` down to at least `Bitfield<3, 0>`. This means the `ExtMachInst` IS a 64-bit value with:- Upper 32 bits: decoder state fields (bits 63-32)\\n- Lower 32 bits: the actual instruction bits (bits 31-0)\\n\\nSo `ExtMachInst` bit 31 IS the actual instruction bit 31. The pattern `bitPatternMatcher<31, 10, ...>` should correctly match the instruction bits at positions 31-10 of the `ExtMachInst` value.\\n\\n\\nWhen `MachInst` gets promoted to `ExtMachInst` in the decoder, the actual 32-bit instruction shifts to the lower 32 bits, which means bit 31 of the instruction corresponds to bit 31 of the `ExtMachInst` value. The pattern `bitPatternMatcher<31, 10, ...>` operates on `MachInst` in the `bitPatternMatcher` template, but since `MachInst` is `uint32_t`, bit 31 is the highest bit of that 32-bit value.\\n\\nSo `MachInst` IS the 32-bit instruction value (low 32 bits of `ExtMachInst`). And `bitPatternMatcher<31, 10>` correctly targets bits 31-10 of the 32-bit instruction.\\n\\nBUT, the generated pattern creates a lambda that takes a `MachInst` parameter, but when it's called with `machInst` (which is an `ExtMachInst`), there's a type mismatch. Unless the `bitPatternMatcher` template uses a different `MachInst` than the global one.\\n\\nActually, looking at the generated code in `decoder-ns.cc.inc`, the pattern is defined with a specific `MachInst` type from the namespace, and the `isFpMulMadd` lambda takes that type as a parameter. The key is understanding what `MachInst` actually refers to in that context.\\n\\nThe `isFpMulMadd` pattern in the namespace should work correctly with `ExtMachInst` since it defines bitfields for the full 64-bit range, including bits 31 down to 0.\\n\\nWhen I trace through the ARM64 FMLS instruction encoding, the bit positions align properly with the pattern's constraints—bits 31-28 are 0001, bit 27 is 1, and bit 25 is also 1, all matching the expected values for this instruction class.\\n\\nBut I'm noticing a discrepancy in the pattern definition. The bit specification shows `'1','X','X'` for bits 27-25, but the constraint comment suggests bit 25 should be 0 for FMLS/FMLA instructions. Looking at the actual FMLS encoding 0x1e2e1000, bit 25 is definitely 1, so either the constraint comment is outdated or there's a mismatch between what the pattern expects and what the comment claims. I see the pattern is checking bit 25 as a don't-care value, but the instruction does have a specific value there. The comment mentions that bits 26:25 should be 11 for FMLS/FMLA, but I need to verify whether the current pattern actually enforces this or if it's just describing the expected bit pattern.\\n\\nLooking at the constraint more carefully, bits 27:25 are currently marked as XXX (all don't-care), which means bit 25 isn't actually being validated at all. Since FMLS requires bit 25 to be 1, the pattern should be changed to reflect that constraint rather than leaving it as a wildcard.\\n\\nThe same issue applies to bit 24 — it's currently X but should be 0 for FMLS/FMLA instructions. I need to verify what the actual ARM encoding requires for these bits and update the pattern accordingly. I'm realizing I had the bit positions mapped incorrectly in my Python simulation—bit24 should be 0 and bit23 should be 1 for the FMLS/FMLA encoding, not the other way around. Looking at the binary representation, I can see the instruction encoding breaks down as bits31:28 equals 0001, bits27:24 equals 1110, bits23:22 equals 00, and bit21 is 1, so I need to correct how I'm extracting and validating these values against the expected pattern. I see the problem now—the comment in the pattern is misleading since it claims bits 26:25 should be \\\"XX\\\" but actually the pattern is treating them as don't-care values when FMLS requires bit25=1 and bit24=0. The bit layout is: bits31:28 match \\\"0001\\\", bit27 matches \\\"1\\\", but bits26:25 are wildcards when they shouldn't be, and bits24:23 are also wildcards when they should be \\\"0\\\" and \\\"1\\\" respectively. I see the issue now—the current pattern has `bit23=0` when FMLS actually needs `bit23=1`. Looking back at the character mapping, `bit24=0` is correct, but I mistakenly set `bit23=0` when it should be `bit23=1`. Let me correct this and verify the fix aligns with the actual instruction encoding.\\n\\nUpdating the pattern...\",\"signature\":\"e1c2d10267b8d0a6417602c23a2c5e3e9143ba09460dcb3294db16f4d6420669\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1288,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":123102},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"f0ff2c9e-0105-4141-85d4-5713da40d8f4\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:37:29.833Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"f0ff2c9e-0105-4141-85d4-5713da40d8f4\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d2568f2fd2abd4b3b74b44b3255d5\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"\\nFound the issue! Looking at `ExtMachInst` in `types.hh`, the bitfield definitions go from `Bitfield<63, 62>` down to `Bitfield<3, 0>`. The instruction bits ARE at their correct positions in `ExtMachInst`. But let me check the character mapping carefully:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":1288,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":123102},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"f9379b14-71f4-4985-866f-c94a6098f7b1\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:37:32.207Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"f9379b14-71f4-4985-866f-c94a6098f7b1\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d2568f2fd2abd4b3b74b44b3255d5\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_function_8sxzg3w5vp3l_1\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"python3 << 'PYEOF'\\n# The pattern chars: '0','0','0','1','1','X','X','0','0','0'\\n# Map to bits:     31  30  29  28  27  26  25  24  23  22\\n#                   0   0   0   1   1   X   X   0   0   0\\n\\n# FMLS 0x1e2e1000 = 00011110 00101110 00010000 00000000\\n# In binary: 0001_1110_0010_1110_0001_0000_0000_0000\\nfmls = 0x1e2e1000\\nprint(f\\\"FMLS 0x{fmls:08x}\\\")\\nfor pos in [31,30,29,28,27,26,25,24,23,22]:\\n    bit = (fmls >> pos) & 1\\n    print(f\\\"  bit {pos:2d}: {bit}\\\")\\n\\nprint()\\n# Check: what do