}))\\n  return new Unknown(machInst);\\n  break;\\n}\\nGEM5_UNREACHABLE;\\n```\\n\\n\\nAnd line 2866 is another `GEM5_UNREACHABLE;`. So line 2864 closes the DECODERFAULT switch, line 2865 closes the ILLEGALEXEC switch, line 2866 closes DEBUGSTEP, and then the THUMB switch closes after that at line 2920.\\n\\nLooking more carefully at the nesting structure: the THUMB switch (starting around line 22) contains two cases - case 0x0: with the AARCH64 switch inside it (lines 27-2920), and case 0x1: with the BIGTHUMB switch inside it (lines 2934-2932). So the AARCH64 switch is actually nested within case 0x0: of the THUMB switch.\\n\\nTracing the AARCH64 switch structure, it only has two explicit cases: case 0x0: at line 29 and case 0x1: at line 2868. When AARCH64=1, execution enters case 0x1, which uses the Aarch64 namespace and checks bit 27 of the instruction. The switch then continues through additional bit checks for bits 28 and 26, which eventually leads to calling `decodeFpAdvSIMD`.\\n\\nBut if the \\\"DBG AARCH64=1\\\" debug isn't firing, that means either AARCH64=0 (so case 0x0: executes instead) or one of the outer switches (THUMB, DECODERFAULT, ILLEGALEXEC, or DEBUGSTEP) has a non-zero value that prevents reaching this code path. The debug output would show which case is actually being taken, but if none of those outer conditions match, the switch would hit its default case with `GEM5_UNREACHABLE`.\\n\\nThe real issue is likely that `decodeFpAdvSIMD` is never being called at all—the instruction is being recognized as \\\"Unknown\\\" somewhere earlier in the pipeline before it reaches this point. The `DBG bit27:25=111` debug message isn't firing, which means the instruction never reaches the `decodeFpAdvSIMD` function. But `DBG decodeInst ENTRY` does fire, so I'm wondering if `decodeInst` is being called with a different `ExtMachInst` value than expected, or if `decodeInst` isn't even the decoder function being invoked—gem5's `Decoder` class might have multiple d