e instruction structure.\\n\\nNow looking at the scalar floating-point multiply-add instructions more carefully. For the 32-bit variants like FMADD and FMSUB, the opcode field spans bits 23, 22, and 21, with bits 15, 14-10, and 9-5 forming the register specifiers. The 64-bit variants follow a similar pattern but distinguish themselves through the opcode values—FMADD uses opcode=000 while FMSUB uses opcode=001, and they both share the same control bit structure.\\n\\nMoving to FMLA and FMLS, these are the scalar floating-point fused multiply-add instructions that encode differently. They use bits 23 and 21 for the opcode (with bit 22 set to 1), while bit 15 serves as the M field. The register specifiers are spread across bits 20-16, 14-10, and 9-5, with the register width indicator in bit 10.\\n\\nNow looking at the 128-bit variants, the encoding changes significantly. These use a 128-bit instruction format where bits 31-10 define the opcode (appearing twice for the wider format), and the register fields are arranged differently to accommodate the larger instruction space.\\n\\nFor the 128-bit FMLA specifically, I'm checking the encoding: bits 31-10 equal 0x1E20 with bit 9 set to 0, and bit 4 set to 1 to distinguish it from the scalar variant. The opcode calculation would be 0x1F4 (decimal 500), with the M bit at position 15 and the opcode field at positions 23, 22, and 21.\\n\\nLooking at the instruction encoding patterns, the scalar FMLA doesn't match the `isFpDataProc1Source` pattern because bit 23 is 0 instead of 1, and the 128-bit variant is even further removed since both bits 23 and 21 are 1 in that case. Now I need to focus on the specific instructions mentioned in the issue.\\n\\nThe root cause seems to be that FMADD and FMLA aren't being routed correctly to the decode function. FMADD uses the 32-bit scalar format where bits 23-21 are 000, which means it won't match any existing pattern that requires bit 23 to be 1. For FMLA, I need to trace through its encoding more carefully to see where the routing breaks down. I see the issue now — FMLA does match `isFpDataProc2Source` since bit[21] is 1, but the problem is that `isFpDataProc2Source` routes to `decodeFpDataProc2Source` instead of `decodeFpDataProc`, so FMLA would be decoded as FMUL instead. The decode function for 2-source operations needs to check whether bit[15] is 1 to distinguish FMLA from FMUL. I need to check whether `decodeFpDataProc2Source` already handles FMLA, FMLS, and related instructions, or if I need to modify the existing code to support them.\",\"signature\":\"f8489f1b6ea1b9f7d1f421ae010f9fdcb7573976cbe952a10e0bbc827e86193a\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":137,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":42820},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"8df27318-d1ae-4033-a2a0-94b80fd6f3c8\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:29:27.277Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"8df27318-d1ae-4033-a2a0-94b80fd6f3c8\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d07433392e0b7e7324316e96ac739\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me check what instructions `decodeFpDataProc2Source` handles and look at the test ELF file.\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":137,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":42820},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"93560c30-c1a9-450e-a5b6-9b480d03b156\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:29:28.607Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users