kes characters that represent bit values for SPECIFIC bit positions. Let me look at `isCryptoAES`:\\n\\n```\\n'0','1','0','0', // op0   -> bits 31:28 = 0100 = 0x4\\n'1','1','1',           -> bits 27:25 = 111 = 7\\n'0','X',               -> bits 24:23 = 0X (bit 24=0, bit 23=X)\\n'X','1','0','1',       -> bits 22:19 = X101\\n\\n\\n```\\n\\nSo the template parameters `31, 10` define the range, and the character sequence maps those bits from most significant to least significant. For `isCryptoAES`, bits[31:28] = 0100 (0x4), bits[27:25] = 111 (0x7), bits[24:23] = 0X, bits[22:19] = X101, and bits[18:10] = 00XXX10.\\n\\nThis decodes to 0 0100 | 111 | 0X | X101 | 00XXX10, which in ARM64 encoding terms represents 31:28=0100 (0x4), 27:25=111, 24:23=00 (M:0), 22:19=X101 (op:5), and 18:10=0010110 (0x16) — exactly matching the CryptoAES encoding from the ARMv8 reference manual.\\n\\nNow I need to figure out the bit positions for isFpDataProc1Source starting from bits[31:28]. I'm mapping out the encoding for the FMADD instruction across different floating-point precisions—half-precision, single-precision, and double-precision variants all follow the same pattern with M=0 and omode=0, which should all decode through the same isFpDataProc handler.\\n\\nNow I'm checking the bitPatternMatcher to see what those pattern characters actually represent—it looks like the op0 field spans bits 31-28 and needs to match the floating-point data processing encoding, so I need to trace through what each character position in that pattern corresponds to in the instruction encoding. Now I'm checking the FMADD encoding against the pattern—the bits match exactly, with bit21=1 and bits 15:10 set to zero, so this should definitely route through `isFpDataProc` and reach `decodeFpDataProc`. The mystery is why it's still failing, so I need to trace through the actual decode function call chain to see what's happening.\",\"signature\":\"9bea7198b7d361172d68c23cd1cf2b92230b31d5f8cfc48656a9f71bd6e07892\"}],\