lizing the switch statement uses the opcode field directly, so FMLS with opcode 0x1B would trigger the default case and return Unknown, which explains why the instruction isn't being handled properly in decodeFpDataProc2Source. Now I'm thinking through where to intercept this—whether to add a guard in the decode function itself or create a dedicated pattern matcher to catch these NEON instructions before they hit the scalar path. Looking at the ARM encoding specs more carefully, FMLA and FMLS do have distinct opcodes that should be routed to the same decoder, so the cleanest approach is to create a dedicated pattern matcher `isFpMulMadd` that captures both instructions and route them to `decodeFpMulMadd`. I need to check whether that decode function already exists in neon64.isa or if I need to implement it.\",\"signature\":\"9772abd155b0013566b5527b58785826283b21d7ee285cbb6182e40339c7f560\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":79241},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"fc8bab9c-165b-4264-8c28-67da28a1faf6\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T02:42:27.916Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"fc8bab9c-165b-4264-8c28-67da28a1faf6\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d0a855f9b485ec610bb647d468daa\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"The fix needs two changes: (1) add guard to `decodeFpDataProc2Source`, and (2) add a new pattern `isFpMulMadd` to catch FMLA/FMLS before the FP path. Let me check if `decodeFpMulMadd` already exists in neon64.isa.\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reaso