1\\nSo switchVal=0x13 for FMLS.\\n\\nNow I'm checking the actual FMLS instruction encoding. With opcode=0x09, switchVal=0x13, this corresponds to the FMLS_S case in the decoder. The switch statement confirms that FMLS_S is indeed case 0x13, and the instruction correctly routes through the FMLS handler for 32-bit operands.\\n\\nFor the actual test instruction with opcode=0x24, switchVal=0x48, this doesn't match any defined case in the switch statement, so it would fall through to the default case and return Unknown.\\n\\nI'm realizing there's a fundamental issue with my approach here. The switch statement is trying to decode multiple instruction types (FMAXNM, FMLA, FADD, FMULX, FCMEQ, FADD, FMAXNM, FMINNM, FMLS, FSUB) all using the same switchVal, which means the same switchVal could match different instructions depending on the actual opcode bits. I need to look at how the NEON decoder actually handles the scalar FP case to understand the proper decoding strategy.\",\"signature\":\"f835c54e7b344b08799e5d57f00b172e60fdfee4c840cb3a02a9bae8d664a8b0\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":335,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":114788},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"6289fc2f-127a-4a8d-97bc-d50a783f2f22\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T03:08:33.152Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"6289fc2f-127a-4a8d-97bc-d50a783f2f22\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d102c6fa43c172edfe6b537d95055\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me check the NEON decode function that handles scalar FP 3-same.\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMa