s provided as a template\\n573- * parameter (a series of 1,0 and X=don't care). It will produce\\n574- * a lambda that can be used to match a sequence of bits over\\n575- * the provided pattern.\\n576- *\\n577- * Example: match bit 31, 30, and 29 with '0X0'\\n578- *\\n579: * constexpr auto decoder = bitPatternMatcher<31, 29, '0', 'X', '0'>;\\n580- *\\n581- * uint32_t msbs_are_010 = 0x40000000;\\n582- * uint32_t msbs_are_000 = 0x10000000;\\n583- * uint32_t msbs_are_100 = 0x80000000;\\n584- *\\n585- * decoder(msbs_are_010) -> returns true\\n586- * decoder(msbs_are_000) -> returns true\\n587- * decoder(msbs_are_100) -> returns false\\n588- */\\n589-template<typename T, int msb, int lsb, char curr, char... rest>\\n590-constexpr auto\\n591:bitPatternMatcher()\\n592-{\\n593-    // No need to check if msb > lsb as it's already covered by\\n594-    // the last conditin in the assertion (impossible to get a negative\\n595-    // sizeof)\\n596-    static_assert(msb >= 0 && lsb >= 0 && msb - lsb == sizeof...(rest));\\n597-    constexpr T mask = dontCareMask<T, msb, lsb, curr, rest...>();\\n598-    constexpr T match = bitMatch<T, msb, lsb, curr, rest...>();\\n599-    auto ret = [] (T value) constexpr\\n600-    {\\n601-        return (value & mask) == match;\\n602-    };\\n603-    return ret;\\n604-}\\n605-\\n606-} // namespace gem5\\n607-\\n608-#endif // __BASE_BITFIELD_HH__\"}]},\"uuid\":\"98556c20-7bb9-4bfa-bf68-a03ad8964f79\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T03:54:31.047Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"ed0449e6-dd2d-4800-af1d-3e8c8e3ed298\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"98556c20-7bb9-4bfa-bf68-a03ad8964f79\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d1b77634f90e665f48379a11624ad\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Found it! The mask computation uses `bitMatch` which has a bug in handling 'X' characters!Looking at `bitMatch`:```cpp\\ntemplate<typename T, int msb, int lsb, char curr, char... rest>constexpr auto\\nbitMatch()\\n{\\n    if constexpr (msb < lsb) {\\n        return 0;\\n    } else if constexpr (msb == lsb) {        return bitMatch<T, msb, curr>();\\n    } else {\\n        return bitMatch<T, msb, curr>() |\\n               bitMatch<T, msb-1, lsb, rest...>();\\n    }\\n}\\n```And `bitMatch<T, msb, curr>()`:\\n\\n\\nWhen the character is 'X', it recursively calls `bitMatch` with the next character instead of returning 0 immediately, which causes it to skip over subsequent bits and match them incorrectly. So for an 'X' at bit position 24, the function doesn't properly handle it as a don't-care—it keeps processing subsequent bits and creates wrong matches.\\n\\nFor the '0' at bit 23, the mask correctly returns only bit 23 set, but then the function processes the next character 'X' in the same recursive call, causing it to incorrectly match against bit 22 instead of stopping at bit 23. So when I recursively call bitMatch with bit 22 and the rest of the arguments, the 'X' character maps to a 1 in the mask, which means bit 22 gets set to 1 instead of being ignored. But for the '0' character at bit 23, the mask correctly gives 0, so there's an inconsistency in how these characters are being handled.\\n\\nLet me trace through the actual recursive structure more carefully. The function is calling bitMatch with the current bit and the next character, then recursively with the remaining bits. When I look at the case where msb equals lsb, it seems to be handling the recursion differently than I initially thought, which might explain why the 'X' isn't being treated as a wildcard as expected. I'm realizing there's a logic error in how the bit matching recursion handles the don't-care character—when the current bit position equals the lower bound and we hit an 'X', it should terminate rather than continuing to process remaining characters. When `bitMatch` recurses with `msb=22, lsb=23` and encounters an 'X', it should return 0 since that represents an invalid state. But I'm second-guessing whether this logic is actually what's happening in the `bitPatternMatcher` code, so let me trace through a concrete example with the pattern `'0','X','X','X'` at bit positions 23, 22, 21, 20 to see where the mismatch occurs. Tracing through the bit matching logic with the concrete example of 0x1e2e1000 at position 24, where the character is '0' — since the bit value is 0, it correctly returns 0 instead of setting the bit, then recurses down through the remaining positions to build up the mask. The mask calculation seems sound, but I'm realizing the real problem might be elsewhere in the bitfield code itself. Let me reconsider whether `isFpMulMadd` is actually even being evaluated in the first place, or if there's something else I'm missing about the decoder flow.\\n\\nI'm wondering if gem5's short-circuit evaluation of the conditional checks could be affecting whether `isFpMulMadd` gets called at all. Looking at how the decoder template is generated, I suspect the template specialization for `GenericDecoder` might be in a separate compilation unit, which could mean my changes aren't being compiled into the decoder that actually runs. The `bitPatternMatcher` should be evaluated at compile time, so the decoder class template gets fully instantiated with all the pattern checks inlined.\\n\\nRather than continue tracing through the code logic, I should just add debug output directly to gem5's decoder to see what's actually happening at runtime, or test whether `isFpMulMadd` is being compiled correctly. I could also try modifying the condition to always return true to verify the function gets invoked at all.\\n\\nActually, I'm noticing the test ELF is little-endian ARM, but gem5 works with big-endian encoding. Looking at the hex dump more carefully: `1e 2e 10 00` in the file should be read as a 32-bit big-endian value of `0x1e2e1000` by gem5's memory system, which matches what the panic message shows. So the instruction encoding itself looks correct.\\n\\nLet me step back and trace through what happens when gem5 reads and decodes this instruction at the memory address where it's stored.\\n\\nActually, I'm wondering if there's a feature flag issue preventing certain instruction classes from being implemented. I should check whether `FmlsDX` requires a specific ARM feature that's not enabled on this CPU. Let me look at the gem5 configuration to see what features are active.\",\"signature\":\"8cfff610ff5e3ea584bd9dd6a5f98da43f5c3bf7a37a864692290b92640984c2\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":926,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":160426},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"2e381ea3-d937-469c-9e88-fcb249e34e98\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T03:55:00.037Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/gem5\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"2e381ea3-d937-469c-9e88-fcb249e34e98\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d1b77634f90e665f48379a11624ad\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me check the CPU features and if FMLS/FMLA are gated by specific feature flags.\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":926,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":160426},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"2c249b64-3ca0-4784-a0fa-d3a12b60e244\",\"ti