hich might not need it depending on the architecture version.\\n\\n```\\n\\npython\\nthreeEqualRegInstX(\\\"fmls\\\", \\\"FmlsElemDX\\\", \\\"SimdFloatMultAccOp\\\",\\n                   smallFloatTypes, 2, fmlsCode, True, byElem=True)\\nthreeEqualRegInstX(\\\"fmls\\\", \\\"FmlsElemQX\\\", \\\"SimdFloatMultAccOp\\\", floatTypes,\\n                   4, fmlsCode, True, byElem=True)\\nthreeEqualRegInstX(\\\"fmls\\\", \\\"FmlsElemScX\\\", \\\"SimdFloatMultAccOp\\\", floatTypes,\\n                   4, fmlsCode, True, byElem=True, scalar=True,\\n                   nepSrc=\\\"vd\\\")\\n# FMLS (vector)\\nthreeEqualRegInstX(\\\"fmls\\\", \\\"FmlsDX\\\", \\\"SimdFloatMultAccOp\\\", smallFloatTypes,\\n                   2, fmlsCode, True)\\nthreeEqualRegInstX(\\\"fmls\\\", \\\"FmlsQX\\\", \\\"SimdFloatMultAccOp\\\", floatTypes, 4,\\n                   fmlsCode, True)\\n```\\n\\nI see the pattern now—the `_ScX` variants are scalar operations with different element counts and a special `nepSrc` parameter, while the `_DX` and `_QX` variants handle the vector cases with their respective element sizes. I'm realizing the line numbers might not align with what I'm seeing in the code—the grep output suggests the FmlsDX and FmlsQX definitions should be around line 1935, but my earlier read showed them further down. Let me trace through this more carefully by looking at what the grep output actually shows versus what I read. I'm noticing a discrepancy in the line numbers—the grep results show fmlsCode appearing earlier in the file than where I initially read it, which means the file must have been modified after my first read, or I'm looking at different sections than I thought. Looking at the grep results, I can see the line numbers where `fmlsCode` appears and where `FmlsDX` and `FmlsQX` are defined—they should align if I'm reading the same file correctly. Looking at the grep output more carefully, I see that `threeEqualRegInstX(\\\"fmls\\\", \\\"FmlsDX\\\", ...)` appears at line 19