"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"467c8e42-5219-4ae4-8c37-d38f82b6330b\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_0bnw9t23tjcb_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"190\\tdef template NeonXExecDeclare {{\\n191\\t    template\\n192\\t    Fault %(class_name)s<%(targs)s>::execute(\\n193\\t            ExecContext *, trace::InstRecord *) const;\\n194\\t}};\\n195\\t\\n196\\tdef template NeonXEqualRegOpExecute {{\\n197\\t    template <class Element>\\n198\\t    Fault\\n199\\t    %(class_name)s<Element>::execute(ExecContext *xc,\\n200\\t            trace::InstRecord *traceData) const\\n201\\t    {\\n202\\t        Fault fault = NoFault;\\n203\\t        %(op_decl)s;\\n204\\t        %(op_rd)s;\\n205\\t\\n206\\t        const unsigned rCount = %(r_count)d;\\n207\\t        const unsigned eCount = rCount * sizeof(uint32_t) / sizeof(Element);\\n208\\t        const unsigned eCountFull = 4 * sizeof(uint32_t) / sizeof(Element);\\n209\\t\\n210\\t        union RegVect\\n211\\t        {\\n212\\t            uint32_t regs[rCount];\\n213\\t            Element elements[eCount];\\n214\\t        };\\n215\\t\\n216\\t        union FullRegVect\\n217\\t        {\\n218\\t            uint32_t regs[4];\\n219\\t            Element elements[eCountFull];\\n220\\t        };\\n221\\t\\n222\\t        %(code)s;\\n223\\t        if (fault == NoFault) {\\n224\\t            %(op_wb)s;\\n225\\t        }\\n226\\t\\n227\\t        return fault;\\n228\\t    }\\n229\\t}};\\n230\\t\\n231\\tdef template NeonXUnequalRegOpExecute {{\\n232\\t    template <class Element>\\n233\\t    Fault\\n234\\t    %(class_name)s<Element>::execute(ExecContext *xc,\\n235\\t            trace::InstRecord *traceData) const\\n236\\t    {\\n237\\t        typedef typename bigger_type_t<Element>::type BigElement;\\n238\\t        Fault fault = NoFault;\\n239\\t        %(op_decl)s;\\n240\\t        %(op_rd)s;\\n241\\t\\n242\\t        const unsigned rCount = %(r_count)d;\\n243\\t        const unsigned eCount = rCount * sizeof(uint32_t) / sizeof(Element);\\n244\\t        const unsigned eCountFull = 4 * sizeof(uint32_t) / sizeof(Element);\\n245\\t\\n246\\t        union RegVect\\n247\\t        {\\n248\\t            uint32_t regs[rCount];\\n249\\t            Element elements[eCount];\\n250\\t            BigElement bigElements[eCount / 2];\\n251\\t        };\\n252\\t\\n253\\t        union BigRegVect\\n254\\t        {\\n255\\t            uint32_t regs[2 * rCount];\\n256\\t            BigElement elements[eCount];\\n257\\t        };\\n258\\t\\n259\\t        union FullRegVect\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"57684777-9c38-40bd-aa2a-0238cef00b1a\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:08:33.108Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"467c8e42-5219-4ae4-8c37-d38f82b6330b\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"57684777-9c38-40bd-aa2a-0238cef00b1a\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d1ec139080775ce321f01c5f4150f\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"I see! `NeonXExecDeclare` (line 190-193) only generates **declarations**, not definitions. The definitions come from `NeonXEqualRegOpExecute` (line 196-228).\\n\\nFor `threeEqualRegInstX` with `readDest=True` (FmlsDX):\\n- Line 176: `header_output += NeonX2RegOpDeclare.subst(iop)` - declaration template\\n- Line 177: `exec_output += NeonXEqualRegOpExecute.subst(iop)` - **execute definition template**\\n\\nFor `threeUnequalRegInstX`:\\n\\n\\n- `exec_output += NeonXUnequalRegOpExecute.subst(iop)` - **execute definition template**\\n\\nSo the execute definitions should be generated. Looking at the generated exec file around line 247840, I can see `FmlsDX<Element>::execute` with the full template instantiation, which confirms the definition is being generated correctly. Now I need to trace back and see where this is being called.\\n\\nActually, I'm noticing something odd in the exec file structure. The template definition at line 247840 for `FmlsDX<Element>::execute` appears to be using uint32_t registers, but the operand layout suggests it's operating on 64-bit D registers with uint16_t elements. Let me look more carefully at what's happening with the register declarations and element types.\\n\\nThe template is computing element count based on the ratio of register size to element size—so with rCount=2 (128-bit Q regs) and uint16_t elements (2 bytes each), that's 16 total elements, meaning the RegVect union is actually handling a full Q register's worth of data, not just the lower 64 bits that a D register would use. I'm realizing the key difference between the two instruction variants: `threeEqualRegInstX` with `readDest=True` uses the Equal template and writes to a single destination, while the Elem variant with `byElem=True` uses the Unequal template and has different operand access patterns. Let me trace through the specific code to see which template each instruction is actually using.\\n\\nLooking at the generated execute function around line 247840, I can see it's working with a single destination register and two operand registers, where the destination gets read and written back in full register chunks, which matches the Equal template pattern.\\n\\nFor `uint16_t` elements, the register union holds two 32-bit registers (128 bits total) containing 8 elements, but the D register view only exposes the first element to the user—the upper bits get zeroed out. The `uint32_t` variant works similarly but with 4 elements per register.\\n\\nThe issue is that `FmlsDX<uint16_t>` needs proper handling for the D register case where only the first element should be accessible.\\n\\nLooking at the template definitions, `threeEqualRegInstX` generates the declaration and execute method using the NeonX2RegOpDeclare and NeonXEqualRegOpExecute templates respectively, so I need to trace through where the execute function is actually being instantiated for this specific case. The generated code shows `FmlsDX<uint16_t>` being declared but never instantiated or defined, while the generic template `FmlsDX<Element>` exists but isn't being specialized for the uint16_t case. This suggests the build either has an older version of the source file or there's a compilation issue preventing the instantiation from being generated. I need to verify whether this code was actually compiled into the binary or if there's a build issue preventing the instantiation from being generated properly.\",\"signature\":\"adcda09b807dc7b3eeb7b191a4a15b9eefd6e593b50981a85c78c653bd280a3b\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":461,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":74040},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"9d4d2bd2-d898-4ea5-a740-9f655c5c32b9\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:08:52.628Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBr