m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"75acc880-5193-42dd-ab78-867555f74457\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:53:43.859Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"75acc880-5193-42dd-ab78-867555f74457\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_qil06vp3vy0l_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"DEBUG decodeInst ENTRY: machInst=0x1e2e1000\\nsrc/arch/arm/faults.cc:785: panic: Attempted to execute unknown instruction (inst 0x1e2e1000)\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"uuid\":\"743d9438-a382-42b0-9e6b-db7ae40a3ae2\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:53:45.488Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"75acc880-5193-42dd-ab78-867555f74457\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"743d9438-a382-42b0-9e6b-db7ae40a3ae2\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d29598210e69c5d3ca67d3eb131a4\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"`decodeInst` is called with `machInst=0x1e2e1000`. This is the 32-bit value of the instruction. The upper 32 bits are 0. Now the `switch (AARCH64)` should check `machInst.aarch64` which should be... Let me think.\\n\\nThe instruction `0x1e2e1000` in binary is `00011110 00101110 00010000 00000000`. In AArch64 state:\\n- bits 31:28 = 0 = condition codes\\n- `aarch64` bitfield: Looking at types.hh, `Bitfield<34> aarch64` at bit 34. \\n\\nBit 34 of the 32-bit instruction is 0, and since the instruction hasn't been padded into a 64-bit container yet, `machInst.aarch64` evaluates to 0, which means the switch falls through to AARCH64=0 instead of matching case 1. This is the root cause of the issue. the `switch (AARCH64)` at case 0x1 (AARCH64 mode) and passes through the decode chain.\\n\\nWait, but bit 34 should be 0 in a 32-bit ARM instruction! And the AARCH64 state IS set. So why does AARCH64=0?\\n\\nOH WAIT. The switch is `switch (AARCH64) { case 0x0: case 0xf: }`. If `AARCH64` is 0xf, this matches case 0x0.\\n\\nBut bit 34 = 0, not 0xf. Unless the `AARCH64` macro is not just bit 34...\\n\\nLet me check the macro:\\n```cpp\\n#undef AARCH64\\n#define AARCH64  machInst.aarch64\\n```\\n\\nAnd `Bitfield<34> aarch64` extracts bit 34. For 0x1e2e1000, bit 34 is 0. So `AARCH64` = 0.\\n\\nSo case 0x0 would be taken, not case 0x1. This means AARCH64=0! But wait, the backtrace showed `AtomicSimpleCPU::tick()` → `advancePC()` → `UndefinedInstruction::invoke()`. The test IS running in atomic mode.\\n\\nLet me reconsider how `decodeInst` is actually called. Maybe `ExtMachInst` contains the AARCH64 mode info somewhere else, or perhaps the decoder isn't using AARCH64 at all for bare-metal binaries. I should check what the SEWorkload actually does during initialization. I'm tracing through the bitfield extraction for the `aarch64` field in the instruction encoding, but I'm getting confused about how the bits map to the actual instruction bytes—let me work through this more carefully. I need to verify whether the instruction at address 0x80010000 actually matches the 32-bit value I'm checking, so let me look at the test binary to see what instruction is actually there. Looking at the binary representation, bit 34 is definitely 0, which means `AARCH64` would be 0 in the switch statement. But this can't be right if the system is supposed to be in AArch64 mode—I need to reconsider how gem5 constructs the `ExtMachInst` value, since it's not just a simple cast of the instruction bytes. Looking at the bitfield definition, `aarch64` extracts bit 34 from the instruction encoding, but since the 32-bit instruction is only shifted into the lower bits of the 64-bit `emi`, bit 34 would actually be 0. This suggests the test binary might not be compiled as AArch64, or the encoding is different than expected. I'm realizing the issue might be that `instBits` doesn't actually capture all 32 bits of the instruction—if `emi` is only 64 bits total and some of those upper bits are already reserved for other fields, then bits 34-32 from the instruction might be getting truncated or stored elsewhere in the structure. I need to look at the actual bitfield definitions in `types.hh` to understand where the instruction encoding bits are mapped. The `Bitfield<34>` declaration means `aarch64` is pulling from bit 34 of the `emi` object, not from the raw instruction bytes, which is why my earlier analysis of the binary was misleading—I need to trace through how gem5 actually populates these bitfields when decoding. I'm seeing a critical contradiction here—if `emi` stores the 32-bit instruction in `instBits` at bits 31:0, then the bitfield `Bitfield<34>` for `aarch64` would be reading from an undefined upper bit of that 32-bit value, which should always be 0. This means the condition checking `emi.aarch64 == 0xF` would never be true unless gem5 is doing something special to set that bit during instruction fetch or decode initialization. Let me trace through the actual `decodeInst` implementation to understand how the 64-bit `emi` object is being constructed and which bits are actually being set.\\n\\nLooking at the code path, `emi.instBits` gets assigned the raw instruction data, then various bitfields like `emi.aarch64`, `emi.thumb`, and `emi.decoderFault` are explicitly set based on the processor state. So the upper bits of `emi` (above 32) should only contain these explicitly set fields, not the instruction data itself.\\n\\nBut here's the problem: when checking `case 0x1` in the decoder for the AArch64 path, the code reads from `emi.instBits`, which should only contain the 32-bit instruction. However, the code is also checking `bits(machInst, 28)` for the opcode, which means it's trying to extract bits from the full 64-bit value instead of just from `instBits`. This suggests either the code is reading from the wrong field, or there's something wrong with how the instruction is being stored or accessed. I'm realizing I might be misunderstanding how `InstBitField` works in the BitUnion—it could be that the entire 64-bit `emi` value gets masked down when reading the 32-bit field, rather than the 32-bit value being isolated first and then masked again. Let me trace through what actually happens when we access `bits(emi, 31, 28)` to see if this is where the extra masking occurs. I'm realizing the issue might be how the BitUnion extracts bits when only the lower 32 bits are populated—when we access bit 31 of the full 64-bit value, we need to understand whether `instBits` properly wraps around or if there's a mismatch in how the bits are being interpreted. I'm realizing the issue might be in how the BitUnion is actually laid out in memory—if `instBits` is declared as a uint32_t field, it only occupies the lower 32 bits of the 64-bit container, so accessing bits 34, 35, 36 from the upper half would read from whatever garbage or metadata happens to be there. The real problem could be that `instBits` is actually being mapped to bit positions 63-32 instead of 31-0, which would mean the upper bits of the union are storing the actual instruction data rather than the lower bits. So the bitfield layout confirms it—`aarch64` at bit 34 and `instBits` at bits 31:0 means they're completely non-overlapping. The upper 33 bits of the 64-bit value must contain some kind of mode or state information, while `instBits` holds the actual instruction in its lower 32 bits. I need to trace through gem5's decoder logic to see what's actually stored in those upper bits for a bare-metal ARM64 binary.\",\"signature\":\"608225ce53e507a253285de6ae871a07f621f405c9150ce9339c234a6a8f81f4\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":264,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":148762},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"a9cea363-9084-4db0-bb65-1cdbd713acdd\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:54:28.915Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"a9cea363-9084-4db0-bb65-1cdbd713acdd\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d29598210e69c5d3ca67d3eb131a4\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Now I can see the full picture. The entry debug shows `machInst=0x1e2e1000`. The `ExtMachInst` value is just the 32-bit instruction! But the upper 32 bits of `emi` (where `instBits` stores) are 0. Let me check how the `switch (AARCH64)` macro reads the mode:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":264,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":148762},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"73651059-3db3-4338-bfd9-ca46b4065ae0\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:54:31.475Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"73651059-3db3-4338-b