sSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_jon74bqtblng_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"55\\toutput decoder {{\\n56\\tnamespace Aarch64\\n57\\t{\\n58\\t    StaticInstPtr\\n59\\t    decodeDataProcImm(ExtMachInst machInst)\\n60\\t    {\\n61\\t        RegIndex rd = (RegIndex)(uint32_t)bits(machInst, 4, 0);\\n62\\t        RegIndex rdsp = makeSP(rd);\\n63\\t        RegIndex rdzr = makeZero(rd);\\n64\\t        RegIndex rn = (RegIndex)(uint32_t)bits(machInst, 9, 5);\\n65\\t        RegIndex rnsp = makeSP(rn);\\n66\\t\\n67\\t        uint8_t opc = bits(machInst, 30, 29);\\n68\\t        bool sf = bits(machInst, 31);\\n69\\t        bool n = bits(machInst, 22);\\n70\\t        uint8_t immr = bits(machInst, 21, 16);\\n71\\t        uint8_t imms = bits(machInst, 15, 10);\\n72\\t        switch (bits(machInst, 25, 23)) {\\n73\\t          case 0x0:\\n74\\t          case 0x1:\\n75\\t          {\\n76\\t            uint64_t immlo = bits(machInst, 30, 29);\\n77\\t            uint64_t immhi = bits(machInst, 23, 5);\\n78\\t            uint64_t imm = (immlo << 0) | (immhi << 2);\\n79\\t            if (bits(machInst, 31) == 0)\\n80\\t                return new AdrXImm(machInst, rdzr, int_reg::Zero,\\n81\\t                                   sext<21>(imm));\\n82\\t            else\\n83\\t                return new AdrpXImm(machInst, rdzr, int_reg::Zero,\\n84\\t                                    sext<33>(imm << 12));\\n85\\t          }\\n86\\t          case 0x2:\\n87\\t          case 0x3:\\n88\\t          {\\n89\\t            uint32_t imm12 = bits(machInst, 21, 10);\\n90\\t            uint8_t shift = bits(machInst, 23, 22);\\n91\\t            uint32_t imm;\\n92\\t            if (shift == 0x0)\\n93\\t                imm = imm12 << 0;\\n94\\t            else if (shift == 0x1)\\n95\\t                imm = imm12 << 12;\\n96\\t            else\\n97\\t                return new Unknown64(machInst);\\n98\\t            switch (opc) {\\n99\\t              case 0x0:\\n100\\t                return new AddXImm(machInst, rdsp, rnsp, imm);\\n101\\t              case 0x1:\\n102\\t                return new AddXImmCc(machInst, rdzr, rnsp, imm);\\n103\\t              case 0x2:\\n104\\t                return new SubXImm(machInst, rdsp, rnsp, imm);\\n\\n<system-reminder>\\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"17441d65-a158-4310-8148-5bd5aede5117\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:01:40.695Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"e4ed8345-e49f-404d-ab02-cf4379c5ee4e\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"17441d65-a158-4310-8148-5bd5aede5117\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d2b3575ffa50a5cc60ad1df8cafcc\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"Let me take a completely different approach. Instead of debugging endlessly, let me look at what happens with `bits(machInst, 31)` where `machInst` is `ExtMachInst`. \\n\\nThe `ExtMachInst` is a 64-bit BitUnion. The `bits()` function extracts bits. For a regular 64-bit value, `bits(x, 31)` extracts bit 31 of x. But `ExtMachInst` is a complex bit-field union where accessing `bits(machInst, 31)` might not work the same way.\\n\\nLet me check how `bits()` works with `ExtMachInst`. The `bits()` function is defined in `bitfield.hh`. For a `BitUnion64` type, it should extract bits from the underlying storage.\\n \\nNow I see the issue more clearly. When `bits(machInst, 31)` is called on an `ExtMachInst` that's 64 bits wide with the instruction stored in the lower 32 bits, it's actually extracting bit 31 of the entire 64-bit value—which is 0—rather than bit 31 of the instruction itself. That's why the guard conditions are failing.\\n\\nBut the generated code does this same `bits()` call and somehow it works for other instructions, so something else must be going on. Let me trace through what's happening with the value itself before it reaches that check.\\n\\nActually, I'm realizing the real problem might be upstream. The `ExtMachInst` is constructed in the decoder, and maybe the instruction data isn't being stored where I think it is. Let me check how the ELF is being loaded and what values the `machInst` actually contains when it gets to this point.\\n\\nLooking at the decoder code, the instruction bits are stored directly in `instBits` as a 32-bit value. So when `bits(machInst, 31)` is called, it's extracting bit 31 from that 32-bit field, which should give me the actual instruction bit at that position. For an instruction like `0x1e2e1000`, that's `00011110001011100001000000000000`, and bit 31 would be 0.\\n\\nBut I need to verify how the BitUnion actually maps these fields—whether `instBits` is truly at the lower 32 bits or if there's some bit-field reorganization happening that's shifting things around. The field definitions in the types file show `Bitfield<63, 62>` and other high-bit ranges, which suggests the bit-field layout might not be what I'm assuming.\\n\\nLooking at the actual issue: when `bits(machInst, 31)` is called, it's extracting bit 31 from the `instBits` value (which is 0), but the guard conditions are checking this bit to validate the instruction encoding. For FMLS instructions, bit 31 should be 0, but something in how the BitUnion extracts or stores these bits is causing the wrong value to be checked. The guard check in `decodeFpDataProc2Source` is supposed to catch instructions with bit 31 set and return Unknown64, but since it's returning Unknown instead, the guard isn't even being reached. I need to add debug output at every return point to trace where the instruction is getting lost.\",\"signature\":\"3cea2e5522f9f379ee5bcb1849e3714f9abf8f82feb09dbcceb18bd2fff5ae2a\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":130260},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"1e9481c9-2d65-4e5a-9685-e24761247a91\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:02:02.625Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"1e9481c9-2d65-4e5a-9685-e24761247a91\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d2b3575ffa50a5cc60ad1df8cafcc\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"\\nLet me add comprehensive debug at ALL return sites of `Unknown64` and `Unknown` in the decode chain:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":130260},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"b286c321-9fa3-4874-bfd9-f49558c3a6bb\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:02:03.364Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"b286c321-9fa3-4874-bfd9-f49558c3a6bb\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"ag