ove or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\\n</system-reminder>\\n\"}]},\"uuid\":\"6e881746-72fa-40da-8e37-9164e513a2ce\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:26:19.415Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"92f1a129-7ef2-48e0-85ce-4a0dfd178d0d\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"6e881746-72fa-40da-8e37-9164e513a2ce\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d30fb44605021677fc83c85cab5bb\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"I see it now! Line 3876-3877:\\n```c\\nif ((bits(machInst, 25) == 0x0) && \\\\\\n    (bits(machInst, 31) == 0x1)) {\\n    // bit 31:25=1xx0000\\n    return decodeSmeInst(machInst);\\n} else {\\n    return new Unknown64(machInst);\\n}\\n```This is the branch at the higher level. FMLS has bit31=0. So `bits(machInst, 31) == 0x1` is FALSE. Combined with `bits(machInst, 25) == 0x0` being TRUE, this gives (TRUE && FALSE) = FALSE, so it falls through to `return new Unknown64(machInst)`!\\n\\n\\nBut wait—looking at the full context around line 3875, I need to trace which condition actually reaches this code. Let me check the surrounding if-else structure more carefully to understand which branch we're actually in when this check appears.\\n\\nLooking at the logic flow: if bits 28 and 26 are both 0, we go to DataProcImm; if bit 28 is 1 and bit 26 is 0, we go to BranchExcSys. Then for bit 27: if it's 0 we get LoadsStores, if bit 27 is 1 and bit 26 is 0 we get DataProcReg, and if bit 24 is 1 with bits 31:28 equal to 0xF we get Gem5Op64—otherwise we reach the default case that calls decodeFpAdvSIMD.\\n\\nFor the FMLS instruction 0x1e2e1000, all these conditions check out: bits 28 and 26 are both 0, bit 27 is 1, bit 26 is 0, and bit 24 is 0, so we should definitely be hitting the decodeFpAdvSIMD path.\\n\\nBut wait—the debug output shows `DBG decodeInst ENTRY: mach=0x1e2e1000` followed immediately by `DBG UndefinedInstruction::invoke: unknown=1`, which means something's returning an Unknown64 between those two log points. I need to figure out whether the debug line at the bit27:25=111 branch is actually being reached, and if so, why we're getting an unknown instruction afterward. the backtrace shows that `UndefinedInstruction::invoke` is called from `advancePC`, not directly from `decodeInst`. This means the decode returns an Unknown64, which then gets executed, and only then does the fault fire. So the decode itself isn't printing debug output, which suggests either the decode is happening elsewhere or the execution path is different than expected.\\n\\nNow I'm tracing through the top-level decode logic in the generated code. The `decodeInst` function has a switch statement on `DEBUGSTEP`, and if that hits the default case, it should eventually reach the AArch64 decode path. But if `DEBUGSTEP` isn't 0, the control flow bypasses the switch entirely and goes to an unreachable section instead.\\n\\nLet me check what `DEBUGSTEP` actually is—it's defined as a macro that accesses a bitfield from the machine instruction encoding. I need to trace back what `DEBUGSTEP` resolves to and whether it could be causing the decode to skip the expected path.\",\"signature\":\"eb825969abfe2b18faf5331642d82d07d1a0428acbf29425444d472f751ed20a\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":480,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":164811},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"9604b98b-7002-48ea-948a-206ceeca7db0\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:26:40.175Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wang