 and the bit27:25=111 case, one of the earlier cases MUST have matched and returned.\\n\\nLet me add debug to ALL the early return cases in the generated decode-method.cc.inc. But wait - the file keeps getting regenerated. \\n\\nActually, I think the ISA compiler is the culprit here. The \\\"DO NOT EDIT\\\" comment at the top of decode-method.cc.inc tells me it's being regenerated from aarch64.isa and similar files. So even though I keep modifying the generated file directly, the ISA compiler overwrites it during the build process. The fact that the build doesn't explicitly show compilation means scons is either caching the result or the file gets regenerated as part of a larger compilation step.\\n\\nWait, but `DBG decodeInst` printed successfully in the test run, which means my edits to decode-method.cc.inc did take effect at some point. But then the binary that got linked doesn't have the later changes. This suggests scons only rebuilt what it detected as stale, so decoder.o got recompiled and linked, but other dependent objects weren't rebuilt with the new decoder object file.\\n\\nLet me trace through what's actually happening here. The real issue is that I need to add debug output to every branch in the `decodeInst` switch statement to see which path is actually being taken, but I keep getting sidetracked by build system complications.\\n\\nInstead of fighting the build system, I should add a simple debug print right at the start of `decodeInst` that logs the raw machine instruction value and the AARCH64 bit extracted from the top bits, then I can build and test without worrying about scons not detecting changes.\",\"signature\":\"b199c9967397024c3905baf19547932fb45808e43cb3cca61f96342df0cf9b65\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":220,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":165270},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"20cb60