echain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d24653dbb6f82c5e0f4a94b6435ff\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"`FmlsDX<uint16_t>` (IjE = `unsigned short`) and `FmlsDX<uint32_t>` (IjE = `unsigned int`) execute methods are in the binary. \\n\\nBut wait - which one is which?\\n- `FmlsDXIjE`: The `Ij` part is for `unsigned int` (`unsigned int` is `Ij` in the Itanium C++ ABI)\\n- `FmlsDXItE`: The `It` part is for `unsigned short` (`unsigned short` is `It` in the Itanium C++ ABI)\\n\\n\\n- So IjE corresponds to uint32_t and ItE to uint16_t\\n\\nLooking at the symbol names more carefully: `FmlsDXIjE` decodes to the uint32_t variant, while `FmlsDXItE` is the uint16_t one - the 'I' indicates an integer type, with 'j' being unsigned int and 't' being unsigned short. I'm realizing the compiler backtrace shows `UndefinedInstruction::invoke` calling `ArmStaticInst::invoke`, which then checks a disabled flag or unknown instruction flag. The issue is that `new FmlsDX<uint16_t>` is being created in `decodeFpMulMadd`, but this instruction object must have `unknown=true` or `disabled=true` set, triggering the panic even though the execute method exists in the binary. The panic message indicates the unknown flag is set, but `FmlsDX` shouldn't have that since it's a real instruction. Let me trace through the actual execution path: if `isFpMulMadd` matches and `decodeFpMulMadd` creates the instruction, then `execute()` gets called and should pass the `checkFPAdvSIMDEnabled64()` check in AtomicSimpleCPU, but something in that chain is setting the unknown flag or bypass flag instead.\\n\\nLooking at the backtrace more carefully, the crash happens in the undefined instruction handler at tick 0, which means the instruction was decoded but marked as unimplemented or unknown. If the decode were working correctly and returning a valid instruction, we'd see the fault from `checkFPAdvSIMDEnabled64()` inside `execute()` instead, or an actual arithmetic panic. Since we're seeing the undefined instruction panic, the instruction object itself must have been created with the wrong type or an uninitialized flag.\\n\\nNow I'm realizing the real issue: if `decodeFpMulMadd` were being called and working correctly, it would either succeed or hit the FHM check for half-precision variants. But we're crashing immediately at tick 0 with an undefined instruction panic, which suggests the instruction was decoded but immediately flagged as unknown. This points to `isFpMulMadd` not actually matching in the binary, even though our Python simulation says it should—either the binary wasn't rebuilt with our changes, or there's something else filtering it out before `isFpMulMadd` gets checked.\\n\\nLet me trace through the full decode path in `decodeFpAdvSIMD` to see if any of the earlier pattern checks are catching the instruction instead. I'm working through the bit patterns for the remaining functions to verify whether any of them match the FMLS instruction encoding, systematically checking each predicate against the binary representation. Continuing through the remaining bit patterns: the immediate encoding requires bit 25 to be 1, which doesn't match our instruction, so that's ruled out. The conditional compare and second source operand patterns also fail their bit checks. This means the FMLS instruction would only match the multiply-add predicate, routing it to the decode function for further processing.\\n\\nThe decode function extracts the vector length, element size, and opcode fields from the instruction encoding. Now I need to check what `switch (size)` does with a size value of 0, since that's what the decoder would pass.\\n\\nLooking at the switch statement, when size equals 0 (16-bit elements), the code checks the opcode's least significant bit to determine whether to execute FMLA or FMLS. Since the opcode is 0x04, the LSB is 0, so it routes to the FMLS path. With Q=0 (scalar mode), it instantiates a new FmlsDX instruction with uint16_t elements.\\n\\nThis should work correctly, so there must be something else causing the mismatch. I'm wondering if the ISA parser is generating code with a slightly different mask or match value than what the C++ template produces—maybe an off-by-one error in how it's interpreting the template parameters or character array.\\n\\nLet me check the generated decoder-ns.cc.inc file to see what the actual compiled values are. If the mask is something like 0x18 instead of 0x18000000, the matcher would accept any instruction with those top bits set. I can use a simple test instruction like 0x00000000 to verify whether the pattern is actually matching correctly.\",\"signature\":\"96171eb90135adddc7327513d9ae08f7f8cb0238680ba850d662ba9d16ac8623\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":239,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":111592},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"de203655-81f1-43ea-b695-71b5085e4a9c\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:33:04.824Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"de203655-81f1-43ea-b695-71b5085e4a9c\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d24653dbb6f82c5e0f4a94b6435ff\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me check what `isFpMulMadd` is producing in the actual generated C++ code. Let me add a test instruction that should NEVER match:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":239,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":111592},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"28e83575-dd04-40dd-a6f9-ed9685ac1d9e\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:33:05.948Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"28e83575-dd04-40dd-a6f9-ed9685ac1d9e\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d24653dbb6f82c5e0f4a94b6435ff\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"tool_use\",\"id\":\"call_function_a9fphgpyzzm2_1\",\"name\":\"Bash\",\"input\":{\"command\":\"python3 -c \\\"\\n# Test: if mask is 0xf9c00000, even 0x00000000 should NOT match\\n# mask=0xf9c00000 means we check: bit31=0, bit30=0, bit29=0, bit28=1, bit27=1, bit24=0, bit23=0, bit22=0\\n# 0x00000000: bit31=0,30=0,29=0,28=0 -> bit28!=1 -> NO MATCH\\nmask = 0xf9c00000; match = 0x18000000\\nprint(f'0x00000000 (all zeros): {(0 & mask) == match}')\\nprint(f'0x1e2e1000 (FMLS): {(0x1e2e1000 & mask) == match}')\\n# If mask was wrong (e.g., shifted), 0x00000000 might match\\n\\\"\"}}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":\"tool_use\",\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":287,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":239,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":111592,\"output_tokens\":1262,\"server_tool_use\":{\"web_search_requests\":0,\"web_fetch_requests\":0},\"service_tier\":\"standard\",\"cache_creation\":{\"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\":0,\"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\":0},\"inference_geo\":\"\",\"iterations\":[],\"speed\":\"standard\"},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"4f298631-d843-4922-ac57-f5193ba45438\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:33:10.660Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"4f298631-d843-4922-ac57-f5193ba45438\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"promptId\":\"520da623-e17e-4efe-b292-47222de157e3\",\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"type\":\"user\",\"message\":{\"role\":\"user\",\"content\":[{\"tool_use_id\":\"call_function_a9fphgpyzzm2_1\",\"type\":\"tool_result\",\"content\":\"0x00000000 (all zeros): False\\n0x1e2e1000 (FMLS): True\",\"is_error\":false}]},\"uuid\":\"352f7239-9c13-41fc-bb6d-4d7f009cc2c5\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:33:10.819Z\",\"sourceToolAssistantUUID\":\"4f298631-d843-4922-ac57-f5193ba45438\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"352f7239-9c13-41fc-bb6d-4d7f009cc2c5\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d24871ba33d19d9c1b3a77aa83215\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"OK so the pattern analysis is correct. Let me think about this differently.\\n\\nMaybe the issue is that `Unknown64` is not created by `decodeFpMulMadd` - instead, `decodeFpMulMadd` creates `FmlsDX<uint16_t>`, and then when the CPU tries to execute `FmlsDX<uint16_t>::execute()`, it fails.\\n\\nBut the error says \\\"execute unknown instruction\\\" not \\\"undefined instruction\\\". And `FmlsDX::execute` is in the binary.\\n\\nWait. Let me re-read the panic message more carefully:\\n\\n```\\nsrc/arch/arm/faults.cc:785: panic: Attempted to execute unknown instruction (inst 0x1e2e1000)\\n\\n\\n```\\n\\nAnd the code at line 785 is:\\n```cpp\\n} else if (unknown) {\\n    panic(\\\"Attempted to execute unknown instruction (inst 0x%08x)\\\",\\n          arm_inst->encoding());\\n}\\n```\\n\\nSo `unknown` is true. `Unknown64` sets `unknown=true`. Let me check what `Unknown64` looks like. Wait, actually - what if the decode is going through `decodeFpDataProc2Source` which returns `new FDivS` (since switchVal=0x01 for FMLS), and `FDivS` is an unimplemented instruction?\\n\\nNo wait, I already checked that. `FDivS::execute` exists in the binary, so that's not it either.\\n\\nLet me try something different. Instead of trying to figure this out theoretically, let me actually modify the `decodeFpMulMadd` function to return something distinctive like `new Unknown64` with a specific mnemonic, rebuild, and see what happens.\\n\\nActually, I'm looking at this wrong. Maybe the instruction is being decoded through a completely different path. What if the instruction `0x1e2e1000` is hitting a different decoder entirely, like `decodeNeonData` from the ARM32 top level?\\n\\n```\\nChecking bit 31: 0x1e2e1000 has bit 31 = 0\\n```\\n\\nSo it falls into the AARCH64 case. The bit patterns check out correctly for the AARCH64 path too, so it should still reach `decodeFpAdvSIMD`. Let me try adding debug output to trace the actual decode path being taken, or maybe there's a caching issue preventing the pattern from working as expected. I'll instrument the binary directly to see what's actually happening. I need to understand how the ISA parser generates the mask and match values as compile-time constants. For the `isFpMulMadd` pattern, the template recursively builds the mask by setting bits wherever a character isn't 'X', so I need to trace through which bits get constrained for that specific instruction.\\n\\nComputing the mask...\\n\\nSo the mask becomes 0xf9c00000 and the match value is 0x18000000. The generated code creates a lambda that checks if the instruction matches this pattern, which should work correctly. But if the runtime check is still failing despite the compile-time calculation being correct, maybe the generated code isn't actually being compiled into the binary being tested. Let me verify the timestamps and consider whether I should do a clean rebuild to rule out stale object files. I'm checking the build timestamp of the executable against the decoder-ns.o file to see if they align, though I'm realizing I might be looking at this wrong since the object files are stored elsewhere in the build directory.\",\"signature\":\"cfdaddea4ddd90f7bcce87a82bba4762aa4216faf0a34fd6f7742582c39162f8\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":112566},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"eefa6f92-d372-4728-9e37-71bd77f23e31\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:33:31.243Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"eefa6f92-d372-4728-9e37-71bd77f23e31\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d24871ba33d19d9c1b3a77aa83215\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"Let me check the binary's dependency on the generated files to verify it's using the right code:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":112566},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"682fb1b1-5f30-42ce-bf1e-2e0774e266bf\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:33:31.873Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"682fb1b1-5f30-42ce-bf1e-2e0774e266bf\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\"