on word. This is stored in `instBits`. But `instBits` is a `uint32_t` field, and in the BitUnion, it occupies bits 31:0. So the 32-bit instruction IS in bits 31:0.\\n\\nBut wait - I just realized something. Look at the `ExtMachInst` BitUnion. It has `Bitfield<63, 62>`, `Bitfield<61>`, `Bitfield<60>`, `Bitfield<59, 56>`, etc. \\n\\nThese are all BITFIELD ACCESSORS into the underlying 64-bit value. When `emi.instBits = data` sets the `uint32_t instBits` field, it sets the lower 32 bits of the 64-bit union. Then when I access `Bitfield<31, 28>` through the union, it reads from those same bits. For AArch64 mode with `emi.aarch64 = true`, the instruction is a 32-bit word, but the full `ExtMachInst` is 64 bits with decoder state in the upper bits.\\n\\nThe top-level decoder checks `bits(machInst, 28)` to extract bit 28 from the instruction, which comes from the lower 32 bits. But I need to verify whether the AArch64 instruction is actually positioned in the upper bits of `ExtMachInst` or if there's some other arrangement I'm missing—let me check the generated decoder code to see how it accesses the instruction bits.\\n\\nLooking at the disassembly of `decodeFpMulMadd`, I see `ubfx x24, x0, #30, #8` extracting bits 37:30, which suggests the instruction is in the upper 32 bits of a 64-bit `ExtMachInst`. But the top-level decoder checks `bits27:25 = 7` to route to `decodeFpAdvSIMD`, and if the instruction were in bits 37:29 of a 64-bit value, extracting bit 27 wouldn't give me the right bit. I need to figure out where exactly the 32-bit AArch64 instruction is positioned within `ExtMachInst`.\\n\\nLooking at the `ExtMachInst` structure more carefully, the `instBits` field is a 32-bit integer positioned at bits 31:0 of the BitUnion, but there are other bitfield definitions like `Bitfield<63, 62>` that seem to be overlapping with the same bit range. I need to understand how these overlapping fields interact and whether the AArch64 instruction encoding is actually in the upper bits rather than the lower bits. I'm realizing I need to look more carefully at the actual `ExtMachInst` bit layout to understand where the instruction bits are actually stored—the pattern suggests they're in the upper bits rather than the lower 32 bits, but I should verify this directly from the types definition instead of guessing.\",\"signature\":\"987231e8f239fa986aded95fd863b67c572d340da95706a33c7c6c690fc539b4\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":126132},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"d02ab513-663c-4f83-826d-02baaa6cb784\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:38:37.729Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"d02ab513-663c-4f83-826d-02baaa6cb784\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d25bb63dd179d6eeebdf28d07e464\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"`emi.instBits = data` stores the 32-bit instruction at bits 31:0. Let me look at the bitfield definitions that overlap with `instBits`:\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":0,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":0,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":126132},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"c926820d-0aa1-48e2-860f-dcc9dd6017ce\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T04:38:38.979Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parent