n. This means the instruction is never reaching `decodeFpAdvSIMD`.\\n\\nLet me try adding debug output to `decodeInst` itself - the very top of the decoder. This will tell me exactly what `decodeInst` does when it sees this instruction.\\n\\nBut wait, `decodeInst` is in `decode-method.cc.inc`. I can't easily modify that file.\\n\\nActually, let me think about this differently. Maybe the instruction `0x1e2e1000` is being handled by some OTHER code path. Maybe it's getting decoded to something else (like an AArch64 SVE instruction) and that instruction's execute method triggers an UndefinedInstruction fault.\\n \\nThe panic at line 785 in `faults.cc` is triggered when the `unknown` flag is set on an `UndefinedInstruction` exception. Looking at the backtrace, the instruction is actually being executed and triggering this exception in the `UndefinedInstruction::invoke` method, which means the decoder did return an instruction object—just one marked as unknown. So the real question is why the decoder is returning an unknown instruction in the first place. I should add debug output to the top-level `decodeInst` function in the generated code to see what's actually happening during decoding. I'm realizing the `Unknown` class being instantiated is likely the base `gem5::Unknown` class rather than `gem5::ArmISAInst::Unknown64`, which would explain why the panic message says \\\"unknown instruction\\\" instead of the formatted version with the instruction bytes.\\n\\nThe backtrace shows this is coming through `UndefinedInstruction::invoke` rather than the normal instruction execution path, which suggests the instruction isn't being decoded at all—it's being caught by the illegal execution fault path. Let me trace back through the fetch and decode logic to see where the actual decoding happens and why this instruction is triggering a fault instead.\\n\\nNow I'm noticing the backtrace shows `advancePC` calling `UndefinedInstruction::invoke` during `AtomicSimpleCPU::tick`, which