 indicates whether the processor is in Thumb (16-bit) mode, but AArch64 doesn't have a THUMB mode like ARM does. Looking at the bitfields in types.hh, I see THUMB is bit 36 and AARCH64 is bit 34, and the switch statement checks THUMB first. If we're in AArch64 state, THUMB should be 0, and then it checks AARCH64 to determine which mode we're actually in. I'm realizing the naming convention here—THUMB and BIGTHUMB are probably legacy flags that don't directly correspond to the actual instruction modes, and I need to figure out how they map to the AARCH64 bit or other state indicators in the decoder. BIT means in gem5's ExtMachInst.\\n\\nActually, let me think about this differently. The fact that `DBG bit27:25=111 case` doesn't fire but `DBG decodeInst ENTRY` DOES fire means execution goes through `decodeInst` and then gets routed somewhere else. The most likely explanation is that it enters the ARM path (`case 0x0:` for AARCH64) and eventually returns `Unknown` or some other instruction.\\n\\nLet me look at the ARM path to see where it would go with `0x1e2e1000`:\\n- In ARM (AArch32) state with bit31:28=0x1 (condition=0001=EQ)\\n- The instruction is a 32-bit ARM instruction\\n- In ARM state, bit27:25=111 indicates a coprocessor/MMU instruction\\n\\nSo the instruction would route through `decodeCp14_misc_crc`, `decodeCp14_misc`, or `decodeCp15_misc` depending on the coprocessor bits. But the `DBG decodeFpAdvSIMD` debug isn't firing, which means it's not reaching the FP decode path.\\n\\nActually, the pattern matching logic should catch it before the ARM path gets a chance. The `isFpAdvSIMD` pattern checks specific bits in the instruction encoding—if those bits match, it should return `decodeFpAdvSIMD` and then route to `decodeFpMulMadd`. But something in the bitfield extraction or pattern matching itself might be failing, even though my simulation says it should match. The `DBG bit27:25=111` debug message would only print if that branch is actually reached, so if it's not firing, the execution must be taking a different path through the switch statement—maybe hitting the default case or another condition I haven't traced yet. I need to add debug output to the default case in the AARCH64 switch to see what's actually happening with the instruction encoding.\",\"signature\":\"27864fe264896288f548535c74a6d30d5b822559343857384adbc398762c0d86\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":304,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":48281},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"31ac555f-0eb0-4e8e-9cbb-1d5172fc226d\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:38:36.052Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"31ac555f-0eb0-4e8e-9cbb-1d5172fc226d\",\"isSidechain\":true,\"agentId\":\"a5d384807705efc78\",\"message\":{\"id\":\"061d33bfcf46289ebeeabfc2bce37f73\",\"type\":\"message\",\"role\":\"assistant\",\"content\":[{\"type\":\"text\",\"text\":\"\\nWhile the build runs, let me also check the default case of the AARCH64 switch - if neither case 0x0 nor 0x1 is hit, it hits GEM5_UNREACHABLE.\\n\"}],\"model\":\"MiniMax-M2.7\",\"stop_reason\":null,\"stop_sequence\":null,\"usage\":{\"input_tokens\":218,\"output_tokens\":0,\"cache_creation_input_tokens\":304,\"cache_read_input_tokens\":48281},\"service_tier\":\"standard\"},\"type\":\"assistant\",\"uuid\":\"56d125cc-17ab-401a-80ae-591f1d7d09df\",\"timestamp\":\"2026-04-02T05:38:36.769Z\",\"userType\":\"external\",\"entrypoint\":\"cli\",\"cwd\":\"/Users/wangxu/1-project/sdc-fuzzing/qemu/build\",\"sessionId\":\"d910793e-44d5-4b76-a978-fb98052552f1\",\"version\":\"2.1.87\",\"gitBranch\":\"stable\",\"slug\":\"proud-popping-falcon\"}\n{\"parentUuid\":\"56d125cc-17ab-